hadiths[594]{hadithnumber,text}:
"9805","Ziyad ibn Jahwar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: A letter from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached me, in which was written: In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to Ziyad ibn Jahwar. You are safe; peace be upon you. I praise Allah, besides Whom there is no deity, to you. Then: I remind you of Allah and the Last Day. Then: Every religion that people profess shall be laid aside except Islam, so know that. > **""Every religion that people profess shall be laid aside except Islam.""**"
"9806","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""The Muslims will prevail over Rome, and the Muslims will prevail over Persia, and the Muslims will prevail over the Arabian Peninsula.""**"
"9807","Tamim al-Dari, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""This affair will surely reach as far as night and day reach, and Allah will not leave a house of mud-brick nor of hair except that Allah will enter this religion into it, bringing honor to an honored one or humiliation to a humiliated one — honor by which Allah honors Islam and its people, and humiliation by which Allah humiliates disbelief.""** Tamim used to say: I saw that among my own household. Whoever among them accepted Islam received good, nobility, and honor, and whoever among them remained a disbeliever received humiliation, abasement, and the jizya."
"9808","Al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""There will not remain upon the face of the earth a house of mud-brick nor of hair except that Allah will enter the word of Islam into it, bringing honor to an honored one or humiliation to a humiliated one. Either He will honor them and guide them to Islam, or He will humiliate them so they pay the jizya.""**"
"9809","'Aqil ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Quraysh came to Abu Talib and said: O Abu Talib, your nephew comes to our courtyards and our gatherings and makes us hear what harms us. If you see fit, restrain him from us. Abu Talib said to me: O 'Aqil, seek out your cousin for me. So I brought him out from one of Abu Talib's enclosures, and he walked with me seeking shade to walk in but could hardly find it, until he reached Abu Talib. Abu Talib said to him: O son of my brother, by Allah, as far as I know you have always obeyed me. Your people have come claiming you visit them in their Ka'ba and gatherings and make them hear what harms them. If you see fit, refrain from them. The Prophet ﷺ raised his gaze to the sky and said: > **""By Allah, I am no more able to abandon what I was sent with than any one of you is able to kindle a flame of fire from this sun.""** Abu Talib said: By Allah, my nephew has never lied. Return, rightly guided."
"9810","'Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Quraysh kept refraining from me until Abu Talib died.""**"
"9811","Abu Huraira, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When Abu Talib died, they seized their opportunity against the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: > **""How quickly I have felt your loss, O uncle.""**"
"9812","'Urwa ibn al-Zubayr reported from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, may Allah be pleased with them: I asked him, What is the worst harm you saw Quraysh inflict upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in their open enmity? He said: I was present when their chiefs gathered one day in the Hijr and mentioned the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They said: We never endured from any man what we have endured from this man. He has belittled our intellects, reviled our forefathers, criticized our religion, divided our unity, and cursed our gods. We have endured a grave matter from him. While they were thus, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appeared, walking until he touched the Corner, then passed by them circumambulating the House. As he passed, they taunted him with some of his words, and I saw displeasure on his face. He moved on. The second time he passed they did the same, and I saw it on his face. The third time they did the same, and he said: > **""Do you hear, O company of Quraysh? By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, I have come to you with slaughter.""** His words seized them, until there was not a man among them but stood as though a bird had fallen upon his head. Even the harshest among them before that began to placate him with the best words he could find, saying: Leave, O Abu al-Qasim, leave rightly guided, for by Allah you were never ignorant. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ left. The next day they gathered in the Hijr, and I was with them. They said to one another: You spoke of what you endured from him, yet when he confronted you with what you hate, you left him alone. While they were thus, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appeared, and they leapt upon him as one man, surrounding him, saying: Are you the one who says such-and-such against our gods and religion? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Yes, I am the one who says that.""** I saw one of them seize the collar of his cloak, while Abu Bakr stood before him weeping and saying: Would you kill a man for saying, My Lord is Allah? Then they left him. That was the worst I ever saw Quraysh do to him."
"9813","'Amr ibn al-'As, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I never saw Quraysh intend to kill the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except one day when they conspired against him while sitting in the shade of the Ka'ba, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying at the Maqam. 'Uqba ibn Abi Mu'ayt rose toward him, placed his cloak around his neck, and pulled it until he fell to his knees. The people shouted, thinking he had been killed. Abu Bakr rushed forward, seized the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from behind by his upper arm, saying: Would you kill a man for saying, My Lord is Allah? Then they withdrew from the Prophet ﷺ. When he finished his prayer he passed by them as they sat in the shade of the Ka'ba and said: > **""O company of Quraysh, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I was only sent to you with slaughter.""** And he pointed with his hand to his throat. Abu Jahl said to him: O Muhammad, you were never ignorant. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You are among them.""**"
"9814","Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them, was asked: What is the worst harm you saw the polytheists inflict upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? She said: The polytheists sat in the mosque discussing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and what he said about their gods. While they were thus, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came forward, and they all rose against him. A caller rushed to Abu Bakr saying: Reach your companion! He left us, and he had four braids of hair, saying: Woe to you, would you kill a man for saying, My Lord is Allah, when he has come to you with clear proofs from your Lord? So they left the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and turned upon Abu Bakr. Asma said: Abu Bakr returned to us, and whenever he touched any of his braids, it came away with his hand, while he said: Blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor."
"9815","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said: They once struck the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he lost consciousness. Abu Bakr stood calling out: Woe to you, would you kill a man for saying, My Lord is Allah? They said: Who is this? They said: Abu Bakr, the madman. Then they left the Prophet and turned upon Abu Bakr."
"9816","Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I was a youth near puberty, herding sheep for 'Uqba ibn Abi Mu'ayt, when the Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr came, having fled from the polytheists. They said: > **""O boy, do you have any milk to give us to drink?""** I said: I am entrusted, and I will not give you drink."
"9817","Jubayr ibn Nufayr said: We sat one day with al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad, and a man passed by us while we listened to him. He said: Blessed are these two eyes that saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. By Allah, we wish we had seen what you saw and witnessed what you witnessed. Al-Miqdad turned to him and said: What makes a man wish for a presence Allah kept him absent from, when he does not know how he would have acted in it? By Allah, peoples attended the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whom Allah will cast face-down upon their nostrils into Hell, for they did not answer him nor believe him. Will not one of you praise Allah that you know only your Lord, believing in what your Prophet brought, and have been spared the trial through others? By Allah, the Prophet ﷺ was sent in the harshest circumstances any prophet was sent in — in a period of interval and ignorance — when people saw no religion better than idol worship. Then he brought a Criterion that distinguished truth from falsehood and separated father from son, until a man would see his father or son or brother as a disbeliever, while Allah had opened the lock of his own heart to faith. He knows that whoever enters the Fire is ruined, and his eye finds no comfort knowing his beloved is in the Fire. It is they of whom Allah says: > **""Our Lord, grant us from our spouses and offspring comfort of our eyes.""**"
"9818","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the mosque, with Abu Jahl ibn Hisham, Shayba and 'Utba sons of Rabi'a, 'Uqba ibn Abi Mu'ayt, Umayya ibn Khalaf, and two other men — seven in the Hijr — the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying. When he prostrated and prolonged it, Abu Jahl said: Which of you will bring the entrails of the slaughtered camel of so-and-so and throw it upon Muhammad? The most wretched of them, 'Uqba ibn Abi Mu'ayt, went and brought it and threw it upon his shoulders while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was prostrate, unmoved. Ibn Mas'ud said: I stood unable to speak, having no protection to defend me. As I was leaving, Fatima, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, heard, came forward, removed it from his shoulders, then confronted Quraysh and reviled them, and they answered her nothing. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his head as he would at the completion of prostration, and when he finished his prayer he said: > **""O Allah, deal with Quraysh. O Allah, deal with 'Utba, 'Uqba, Abu Jahl, and Shayba.""** He said it three times. Then he left the mosque and met Abu al-Bakhtari, who had a whip at his side. When the Prophet ﷺ saw him, he noticed his face and said, Leave me alone. He said: By Allah, I will not leave you until you tell me your affair; something has befallen you. When the Prophet ﷺ knew he would not leave him, he told him: Abu Jahl ordered, and filth was thrown upon me. Abu al-Bakhtari said: Come to the mosque. So the Prophet ﷺ and Abu al-Bakhtari entered the mosque. Abu al-Bakhtari went to Abu Jahl and said: O Abu al-Hakam, are you the one who ordered filth thrown upon Muhammad? He said: Yes. He raised the whip and struck his head with it. Men rose against one another, and Abu Jahl shouted: Woe to you, leave it to him; Muhammad only intended to stir enmity between us and save himself and his companions."
"9819","In another narration, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his head, he praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: > **""O Allah, deal with the assembly of Quraysh.""**"
"9820","Qatada ibn Di'ama said: 'Utayba ibn Abi Lahab married Umm Kulthum, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while her sister Ruqayya was with his brother 'Utba ibn Abi Lahab, though neither marriage had been consummated before the Prophet ﷺ was sent. When Allah revealed: Perish the hands of Abu Lahab, Abu Lahab said to his two sons: My head is forbidden to your heads unless you divorce the daughters of Muhammad. Their mother, the daughter of Harb ibn Umayya, the carrier of firewood, said: Divorce them, my sons, for they have apostatized. So they divorced them. When 'Utayba divorced Umm Kulthum, he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: I disbelieve in your religion, I have left your daughter; you do not love me nor do I love you. Then he attacked him and tore the Prophet's shirt. The Prophet ﷺ, while 'Utayba was departing toward Syria as a trader, said: > **""I ask Allah to unleash His dog upon you.""** He set out with a Quraysh caravan until they camped at night at a place in Syria called al-Zarqa. A lion circled them that night, and 'Utayba cried: Woe to my mother, by Allah this will devour me as Muhammad said; the son of Abu Kabsha will kill me while he is in Mecca and I am in Syria. The lion leapt upon him from among the people, seized him by the head, crushed it, and killed him. Hisham ibn 'Urwa reported from his father that when the lion circled them that night then left, they slept with 'Utayba in their midst, but the beast stepped over them until it seized 'Utayba by the head and mangled him. Later 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah have mercy on him, married Umm Kulthum, may Allah be pleased with her, after Ruqayya."
"9821","'Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was passed by Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith, who said: > **""O 'Aisha, come so I may show you your cousin who lampooned me.""**"
"9822","Khalid ibn Sa'id said: My father fell gravely ill and said: If Allah heals me of this pain, the god of Muhammad ibn Abi Kabsha shall never be worshipped in the valley of Mecca. Khalid said: So he perished."
"9823","Abu Umayya al-Ta'ifi, descendant of Sa'id ibn al-'As, reported from his grandfather from his grandfather Sa'id ibn al-'As that his grandfather Abu Uhayha was ill when the Prophet ﷺ was sent. In his illness he said: Do not lift me from my bed; the god of the son of Abu Kabsha shall not be worshipped in Mecca. His son, standing at his head, said: O Allah, do not lift him."
"9824","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, said: Quraysh gathered one day against the Prophet ﷺ and said: Find the most knowledgeable among you in magic, soothsaying, and poetry to speak to this man who has divided our unity, scattered our affair, and criticized our religion, and see what he answers. They said: We know none but 'Utba ibn Rabi'a. They said: You, O Abu al-Walid. 'Utba came to him and said: O Muhammad, are you better, or 'Abdullah? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was silent. He said: Are you better, or 'Abd al-Muttalib? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was silent. He said: If you claim they are better than you, they worshipped the gods you criticize; and if you claim you are better than them, then speak so we may hear your words. By Allah, we never saw a calamity more ill-omened upon a people than you. You divided our unity, scattered our affair, criticized our religion, and exposed us among the Arabs until it spread that among Quraysh is a sorcerer and among Quraysh is a soothsayer. By Allah, we await nothing but the cry of a woman in labor, that some of us rise against others with swords until we annihilate one another. O man, if it is need that drives you, we will gather wealth for you until you are the richest man of Quraysh; and if it is desire for marriage, choose any women of Quraysh and we will marry you to ten. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Have you finished?""** He said: Yes. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited: Ha Mim, a revelation from the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, until he reached: But if they turn away, then say: I warn you of a thunderbolt like the thunderbolt of 'Ad and Thamud. 'Utba said: Enough, enough; is there nothing else with you? He said: No. He returned to Quraysh. They said: What is behind you? He said: I left nothing you would say to him except I said it. They said: Did he answer you? He said: Yes. By the One Who erected it as a House, I understood nothing of what he said except that he said: I warn you of a thunderbolt like the thunderbolt of 'Ad and Thamud. They said: Woe to you, a man speaks to you in Arabic and you do not know what he said? He said: No, by Allah, I understood nothing of what he said except mention of the thunderbolt."
"9825","Humayd ibn Munhib said: It reached Mu'awiya that Ibn al-Zubayr was reviling Abu Sufyan, so he said: Evil, by Allah, is what he says about his uncle; but I say only good about 'Abdullah, may Allah have mercy on him, if he was a righteous man. Abu Sufyan went out to his desert land with Hind riding behind him, and I went ahead of them as a boy upon a she-ass, when we met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abu Sufyan said: Dismount, O Mu'awiya, so Muhammad may ride. So I dismounted, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rode it and went ahead of them a while, then turned to them and said: > **""O Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, and O Hind bint 'Utba, by Allah you shall surely die, then you shall surely be resurrected, then the doer of good shall surely enter Paradise and the doer of evil the Fire. What I say to you is truth, and you are the first I have warned.""** Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited: Ha Mim, a revelation from the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, until he reached: They said: We come willingly. Abu Sufyan said to him: Have you finished, O Muhammad? He said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dismounted and I remounted. Hind turned to Abu Sufyan and said: For this lying sorcerer did you dismount my son? He said: By Allah, he is neither sorcerer nor liar."
"9826","Rabi'a ibn 'Abbad al-Du'ali said: I do not hear you saying that Quraysh harmed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — indeed the most I saw was that his house stood between the houses of Abu Lahab and 'Uqba ibn Abi Mu'ayt, and he would return home to find wombs, blood, and filth placed at his door. He would remove it with the tip of his bow and say: > **""What an evil neighborliness this is, O company of Quraysh.""**"
"9827","Al-Harith ibn al-Harith said: I asked my father: What is this gathering? He said: These are people gathered against one who has apostatized among them. He said: We alighted, and there was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ calling people to the Oneness of Allah and faith in Him, while they rebutted him and harmed him until midday, when the people dispersed from him. Then a woman came with her neck exposed, carrying a cup and a cloth. He took from her, drank, performed ablution, then raised his head and said: > **""O my daughter, cover your neck, and do not fear for your father.""** We said: Who is she? They said: This is Zaynab, his daughter."
"9828","Munayb al-Azdi said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the pre-Islamic days saying: > **""O people, say: There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and you will prosper.""** Some spat in his face, some threw dust upon him, and some reviled him until midday. Then a young girl came with a vessel of water; he washed his face and hands and said: > **""O my daughter, do not fear treachery nor humiliation for your father.""** I said: Who is she? They said: Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a radiant young girl."
"9829","Mudrik said: I performed Hajj with my father, and when we alighted at Mina we saw a gathering. I asked my father: What is this gathering? He said: This is the apostate. There was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: > **""O people, say: There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and you will prosper.""**"
"9830","A man of Banu Malik ibn Kinana said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the market of Dhu al-Majaz, moving through it, saying: > **""O people, say: There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and you will prosper.""** Abu Jahl threw dust upon him, saying: O people, let this man not mislead you from your religion, for he only wants you to abandon your gods and abandon al-Lat and al-'Uzza. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ paid him no attention. I said: Describe the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to us. He said: Between two red cloaks, stocky, full-fleshed, handsome-faced, intensely black-haired, intensely fair-skinned, with flowing hair."
"9831","Rabi'a ibn 'Abbad of Banu al-Dil, who lived in the pre-Islamic era, said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the market of Dhu al-Majaz saying: > **""O people, say: There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and you will prosper.""** People crowded around him, while behind him stood a fair-faced, squinting man with two braids, saying: He is an apostate liar, following him wherever he went. I asked about him, and they told me the lineage of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said to me: This is his uncle Abu Lahab."
"9832","In another narration: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would move away from him, while he followed him."
"9833","In another narration: He had lived in the pre-Islamic era, then accepted Islam."
"9834","In another narration: The people withdrew from him, yet I never saw anyone say anything while he did not fall silent — he kept calling."
"9835","Tariq ibn 'Abdullah said: I was at the market of Dhu al-Majaz when a young man passed wearing a suit of red برد, saying: > **""O people, say: There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and you will prosper.""** Behind him was a man whose ankles and shins bled, saying: O people, he is a liar, so do not obey him. I said: Who is this? They said: A youth of Banu Hashim who claims he is Allah's Messenger, and this is his uncle 'Abd al-'Uzza. Later, when Muhammad ﷺ emigrated to Medina and people accepted Islam, we departed from al-Rabadha with a woman of ours in a litter. When we reached the outskirts of Medina and changed our garments, a man on the road said: From where have the people come? We said: We seek provisions for our families, and we have a red camel standing bridled. He said: Will you sell me your camel? We said: Yes. He said: For how much? We said: For such-and-such measures of dates. He did not haggle at all, took the halter, and led it away. When he vanished behind the walls we said: By Allah, we did wrong selling to one we do not know. A seated woman said: I saw a man whose face was like a fragment of the full moon; by Allah he will not wrong you nor leave you perplexed, and I guarantee your camel. Then a man came and said: I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to you; here is your dates — eat, be satisfied, and measure. So we ate, were satisfied, measured in full, then entered Medina, came to the mosque, and there he was preaching upon the pulpit. We heard from his words: > **""Give charity, for charity is better for you.""**"
"9836","'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I set out with the Prophet ﷺ until we reached the Ka'ba. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: > **""Sit.""** He climbed upon my shoulders, but when I tried to rise with him he saw weakness in me, so he descended, sat for me, and said: > **""Climb upon my shoulders.""** So I climbed upon his shoulders, and he rose with me. It seemed to me that had I wished I could have reached the horizon of the sky, until I mounted the House. Upon it was a statue of brass or copper. I wrestled with it from its right and left, front and back, until I overpowered it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: > **""Throw it down.""** So I threw it down, and it shattered as glass shatters. Then I descended, and I and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ hastened away until we disappeared among the houses, fearing anyone might meet us."
"9837","In another narration: There were idols upon the Ka'ba. I went to carry the Prophet ﷺ but could not, so he carried me, and I began cutting them down; had I wished I could have touched the sky. After our concealment among the houses, those idols were never placed there again."
"9838","Burayda ibn al-Husayb, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ touched an idol, then performed ablution."
"9839","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to attend the gatherings of the polytheists with them. He said: Then he heard two angels behind him, one saying to his companion: Let us go stand behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The other said: How can we stand behind him when his recent pledge was to touch idols? He said: After that he never again attended the gatherings of the polytheists with them."
"9840","'Abdullah ibn 'Amir ibn Rabi'a reported from his mother Layla: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab was among the harshest against us in our Islam. When we prepared to leave for Abyssinia, 'Umar came while I was upon my camel intending to depart. He said: Where to, O Umm 'Abdullah? I said: You harmed us in our religion, so we go forth in Allah's land where we will not be harmed in worshipping Allah. He said: May Allah accompany you. Then he left. My husband 'Amir ibn Rabi'a came, and I told him of 'Umar's tenderness. He said: Do you hope he will accept Islam? I said: Yes. He said: By Allah, he will not accept Islam until the donkey of al-Khattab accepts Islam."
"9841","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us to the Negus, about eighty men, among them 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, Ja'far, 'Abdullah ibn 'Urfuta, 'Uthman ibn Maz'un, and Abu Musa. They reached the Negus, while Quraysh sent 'Amr ibn al-'As and 'Umara ibn al-Walid with a gift. When they entered upon the Negus they prostrated to him, hastened to his right and left, and said: Some youths of our cousins have settled in your land, turning from us and our religion. He said: Where are they? They said: They are in your land, so send for them. So he sent for them. Ja'far said: I am your spokesman today — follow me. He entered, greeted, and did not prostrate. They said to him: Why do you not prostrate to the king? He said: We prostrate only to Allah, mighty and majestic. He said: Why is that? He said: Allah sent His Messenger ﷺ to us and commanded us to prostrate to none but Allah, and commanded us with prayer and charity. 'Amr ibn al-'As said: They oppose you concerning Jesus. He said: What do they say about Jesus son of Mary and his mother? He said: They say as Allah says: He is Allah's word and spirit, cast to the chaste Virgin whom no man touched and no child claimed. He lifted a stick from the ground and said: O company of Abyssinians, priests and monks, by Allah they do not exceed what we say about him by more than this. Welcome to you and to him from whom you came. I testify he is Allah's Messenger ﷺ, whom we find in the Gospel, whom Jesus son of Mary foretold. Settle wherever you wish. By Allah, were I not occupied with kingship, I would come to him to carry his sandals and pour his ablution water. He ordered the others' gift returned to them. Later 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud hastened back until he reached Badr. It is claimed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sought forgiveness for the Negus when news of his death reached him."
"9842","Umm Salama, daughter of Abu Umayya ibn al-Mughira, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with her, said: When we alighted in Abyssinia we dwelt with the best neighbor, the Negus. We were safe in our religion, worshipping Allah alone without harm, hearing nothing hateful. When that reached Quraysh, they conspired to send two resolute men to the Negus with gifts of Meccan wares, the most prized being leather. They gathered much leather, left none of his generals without a gift, and sent it with 'Abdullah ibn Abi Rabi'a al-Makhzumi and 'Amr ibn al-'As ibn Wa'il al-Sahmi, instructing them: Deliver each general his gift before speaking to the Negus about them, then present the Negus his gifts, then ask him to surrender them to you before he speaks to them. So they set out and came to the Negus while we were with him in the best abode with the best neighbor. They gave each general his gift, then said to each: Some foolish youths of ours have fled to the king's land, leaving their people's religion without entering yours, bringing an invented religion we know not nor do you. Their people's nobles have sent us concerning them, to return them. When we speak to the king about them, advise him to surrender them to us without speaking to them, for their people know best their faults. They said: Yes. Then they presented their gifts to the Negus, who accepted them. Then they said: O king, some foolish youths of ours have apostatized to your land, leaving their people's religion, not entering your religion, bringing an invented religion we know not nor do you. Their fathers, sons, and clans have sent us to bring them back, for they best know their faults. Nothing was more hateful to 'Abdullah and 'Amr than that the Negus hear their words, but his generals said: They speak truth, O king; their people best know their faults — surrender them to them to return them to their land and people. The Negus grew angry and said: No, by Allah, I shall not surrender them. A people who sought my neighborhood, alighted in my land, and chose me over others — I will summon them and ask about what these two say. If they are as they claim, I shall surrender them; otherwise I shall protect them and treat them well while they remain. Then he sent for the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When his messenger came, they gathered and said to one another: What shall we say to the man when we come to him? They said: By Allah, we shall say what our Prophet ﷺ taught and commanded us, whatever happens. When they came to him, and the Negus had summoned his bishops who spread their scriptures around him, he asked: What is this religion by which you left your people, entering neither my religion nor any other nation's religion? The one who spoke to him was Ja'far ibn Abi Talib, peace be upon him. He said: O king, we were a people of ignorance, worshipping idols, eating carrion, committing indecencies, severing kinship, mistreating neighbors, the strong devouring the weak. Thus we remained until Allah sent to us a Messenger from among us whose lineage, truthfulness, trustworthiness, and chastity we know. He called us to Allah, to affirm His Oneness, worship Him, and abandon what we and our fathers worshipped besides Allah of stones and idols. He commanded truthful speech, fulfillment of trust, kinship, good neighborliness, restraint from forbidden things and blood, forbade indecencies, false testimony, devouring the orphan's wealth, and slandering chaste women. He commanded us to worship Allah associating nothing with Him, establish prayer, and give charity. So we believed him, followed what he brought, worshipped Allah alone, declared forbidden what he forbade and lawful what he allowed. Our people attacked us, tortured us, and tried us in our religion to return us to idol worship and lawfulize foul things. When they overpowered and wronged us and barred us from our religion, we left for your land, choosing you over others, desiring your neighborhood, hoping for no injustice with you, O king. The Negus said: Do you have anything of what he brought from Allah? Ja'far said: Yes. He said: Recite it. So he recited the opening of Kaf Ha Ya 'Ayn Sad. By Allah, the Negus wept until his beard was wet, and his bishops wept until their scriptures were wet. Then the Negus said: By Allah, this and what Moses brought come from one lamp. Go forth, for by Allah I shall never surrender them to you. Umm Salama said: When they left him, 'Amr ibn al-'As said: By Allah, tomorrow I shall come to him with what will uproot them. 'Abdullah ibn Abi Rabi'a, the more God-fearing of the two, said: Do not, for they have kinship, though they opposed us. He said: By Allah, I shall tell him they claim Jesus son of Mary is a servant. The next day he said: O king, they say a grave thing about Jesus son of Mary — send for them and ask what they say. He sent for them, and no trial like it had befallen us. They gathered and said: What shall we say about Jesus if he asks? They said: By Allah, we shall say what Allah said and what our Prophet ﷺ brought, whatever happens. When they entered he said: What do you say about Jesus son of Mary? Ja'far ibn Abi Talib said: We say of him what our Prophet ﷺ brought: > **""He is Allah's servant, His Messenger, His spirit, and His word cast to Mary the chaste Virgin.""** The Negus struck his hand to the ground, took a stick, and said: Jesus son of Mary did not exceed what you said by even this stick. His generals snorted around him. He said: Even if you snort, by Allah. Go, you are safe in my land — safe means secure — whoever reviles you shall be fined, whoever reviles you shall be fined, whoever reviles you shall be fined. I would not wish a mountain of gold to have harmed any of you — dubr in Abyssinian means mountain. Return their gifts to them; I have no need of them. By Allah, Allah took no bribe from me when He restored my kingdom, that I should take bribes concerning it, nor did people obey me therein that I should obey them therein. So they left him disgraced, their gifts returned. We remained with him in the best abode with the best neighbor. By Allah, while thus, one disputing his kingship descended upon him. Never did we know grief severer than our grief then, fearing he might prevail over the Negus and one arise who knew not our right as the Negus knew it. The Negus marched, with the Nile between the two armies. The companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Who will go witness the battle then return to us? Al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam said: I — the youngest among them. They inflated a waterskin, placed it upon his chest, and he swam until he reached the side of the Nile where the armies met, witnessed them, while we supplicated Allah for the Negus's victory and consolidation in his land. The Abyssinian affair settled in his favor, and we remained in the best dwelling until we came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in Mecca."
"9843","Muhammad ibn Hatib said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I have seen a land of palms, so emigrate.""** Hatib and Ja'far set out by sea toward the Negus. He said: I was born upon that ship."
"9844","'Umayr ibn Ishaq said: Ja'far said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to go to a land where I may worship Allah fearing no one. He permitted him, and he went to the Negus. 'Umayr said: 'Amr ibn al-'As told me: When I saw Ja'far and his companions secure in Abyssinia, I envied him and said: I shall not leave this man and his companions be. I came to the Negus and said: Admit 'Amr ibn al-'As. He admitted me. I entered and said: In our land is a cousin of this man who claims people have but one God. By Allah, unless you rid us of him and his companions, neither I nor any of my companions shall ever bring you this trade. He said: Where is he? I said: He comes with your messenger; he will not come with me. So he sent a messenger with me, and we found him seated among his companions. We summoned him and he came. At the door I called: Admit 'Amr ibn al-'As, and behind me he called: Admit the party of Allah. He heard his voice and admitted him before me. I entered to find the Negus upon a couch. I sat before him, placing Ja'far behind me, placing one of my companions between every two of his. The Negus said: Speak. I said: In your land is a man whose cousin is in our land, claiming people have but one God. Unless you kill him and his companions, neither I nor my companions shall ever bring you this trade. Ja'far said: My cousin speaks truth, and I follow his religion. He shouted and cried out until I said: What is wrong with the son of the Abyssinian woman that he does not speak? He said: Is it a revelation like the revelation of Moses? He said: What do you say about Jesus son of Mary? He said: I say he is Allah's spirit and word. He took something from the ground and said: He did not err in his affair by even this. By Allah, were it not for my kingship I would follow you. He said to me: I would not care whether you or any of your companions ever come to me. You are secure in my land; whoever strikes you I shall kill, whoever reviles you I shall fine. He told his chamberlain: Whenever he seeks permission, admit him unless I am with my family; if he comes, admit him. So we dispersed, and none was dearer for me to meet than Ja'far. He met me once on a road; I looked behind him and saw no one, behind me and saw no one. I drew near and said: Do you know that I testify there is no deity but Allah and Muhammad is His servant and Messenger? He said: Allah has guided you, so stand firm. He left me and went. I came to my companions as though they had witnessed it with me; they took a cloak and threw it over me until they smothered me. I kept poking my head out one side then the other until I escaped bare, without a rag. I passed an Abyssinian woman, took her head-cover, placed it over my nakedness, came to Ja'far, and entered upon him. He said: What is with you? I said: They took everything, leaving not a rag; I took a cover from an Abyssinian woman for my nakedness. He set out with me until we reached the king's door. Ja'far told his chamberlain: Seek permission for me. He said: He is with his family, but he admitted him. I said: 'Amr has followed me in my religion. He said: No. I said: Yes. He told a man: Go with him; if he does it, write down all he says. He came and said: Yes. I kept speaking and he kept writing until he wrote everything, even the cup. Had I wished to take anything of their wealth into mine, I could have. Then later I was among those who came in the ships as Muslims."
"9845","Ja'far ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Quraysh sent 'Amr ibn al-'As and 'Umara ibn al-Walid with a gift from Abu Sufyan to the Negus. They said to him while we were with him: Some lowly fools of ours have been sent to you — hand them over to us. He said: No, until I hear their words. He sent for us and said: What do you say? We said: Our people worship idols, but Allah sent us a Messenger; we believed and affirmed him. The Negus asked them: Are they slaves of yours? They said: No. He said: Do you hold a debt over them? They said: No. He said: Then leave them free. We left him, but 'Amr ibn al-'As said: They say about Jesus other than what you say. He said: If they do not say about Jesus as we say, I shall not leave them in my land an hour of day. He sent for us a second time — harder upon us than the first — and said: What does your companion say about Jesus son of Mary? We said: > **""He is Allah's spirit and word, cast to the chaste Virgin.""** He sent for a certain priest and monk; some of them came. He said: What do you say about Jesus son of Mary? They said: You know best — what do you say? The Negus took something from the ground and said: Thus is Jesus son of Mary; he did not exceed what these say by even this. Then he said to them: Does anyone harm you? They said: Yes. He ordered a caller to proclaim: Whoever harms any of these shall be fined four dirhams. He said: Does that suffice you? We said: No, so he doubled it. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ emigrated to Medina and prevailed there, we said to him: Our companion has gone to Medina and prevailed; we wish to depart to him, so provision us. He said: Yes. He carried us, provisioned us, and gave to us, then said: Inform your companion of what I did for you. This is my messenger with you, and I testify there is no deity but Allah and he is Allah's Messenger — ask him to seek forgiveness for me. Ja'far said: We departed until we reached Medina. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ met us and embraced me, saying: > **""I do not know whether I rejoice more at the conquest of Khaybar or the arrival of Ja'far.""** Then he sat, and the Negus's messenger stood and said: Here is Ja'far — ask him what our companion did with him? I said: Yes, he did such-and-such for us, carried and provisioned us, aided us, testified there is no deity but Allah and you are Allah's Messenger, and said: Ask him to seek forgiveness for me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose, performed ablution, then supplicated three times: > **""O Allah, forgive the Negus.""** And the Muslims said: Amen. Ja'far said: I told the messenger: Go inform your companion what you saw of the Prophet ﷺ."
"9846","Ja'far ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Negus asked him: What is your religion? He said: A Messenger was sent to us whose tongue, truthfulness, and loyalty we know. He called us to worship Allah alone, associating nothing with Him, to abandon what our people and others worshipped besides Him. He commands good and forbids evil, commanded prayer, fasting, charity, and kinship. He called us to what we recognize, reciting a revelation from Allah unlike anything else. So we believed, recognized its truth from Allah, and left our people, who harmed and overpowered us. When they inflicted what we hate and we could not defend ourselves, we left for your land, choosing you over others. The Negus said: Go, you are safe in my land — secure. Whoever reviles you shall be fined."
"9847","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to set out with Ja'far ibn Abi Talib to the Negus. That reached Quraysh, so they sent 'Amr ibn al-'As and 'Umara ibn al-Walid, gathering a gift for the Negus. They came, presented the gift, which he accepted, and prostrated to him. Then 'Amr said: Some people of our land have turned from our religion and dwell in your land. The Negus said: In my land? They said: Yes. So he sent for us. Ja'far said to us: None of you shall speak; I am your spokesman today. We reached the Negus seated in council, 'Amr on his right, 'Umara on his left, priests and monks seated in two rows. 'Amr and 'Umara had told him they would not prostrate to him. When we arrived, his priests hastened: Prostrate to the king. Ja'far said: We prostrate only to Allah. The Negus said: Why? He said: Allah sent us a Messenger — the Messenger foretold by Jesus, peace be upon him, after him, named Ahmad — commanding good and forbidding evil. The Negus was pleased with his words. Seeing that, 'Amr said: May Allah right the king; they oppose you concerning the son of Mary. The Negus said: What does your companion say about the son of Mary? He said: He says Allah's word: > **""He is Allah's spirit and word, brought forth from the chaste Virgin whom no man touched and no child claimed.""** The Negus lifted a stick from the ground and said: O priests and monks, these do not exceed what you say about the son of Mary by the weight of this. Welcome to you and to him from whom you came. I testify he is Allah's Messenger, foretold by Jesus. Were it not for my kingship, I would come to him to kiss his sandals. Remain in my land as long as you wish. He ordered food and clothing for us, and said: Return these two's gift. 'Amr was short, 'Umara handsome. They had come to the Negus and drank wine; 'Amr's wife was with him. When they drank, 'Umara said to 'Amr: Order your wife to kiss me. 'Amr said: Have you no shame? So 'Umara seized 'Amr and threw him into the sea; 'Amr pleaded until 'Umara pulled him into the ship. 'Amr bore a grudge, telling the Negus: When you leave, 'Umara stays behind with your family. The Negus summoned 'Umara and blew into his urethra, and he flew off with the wild beasts."
"9848","Ibn Shihab, regarding the names of those who emigrated to Abyssinia and remained there until they arrived after Badr: Shurahbil ibn 'Abdullah ibn Hasana — Hasana being his mother."
"9849","'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla said: Quraysh sent 'Amr ibn al-'As and 'Umara ibn al-Walid in the time of the Negus. 'Umara was handsome; he would throw 'Amr into the sea, and 'Amr would swim out, only for him to throw him again. 'Amr bore a grudge. When they arrived and entered upon the Negus, they said: Ja'far and his companions reviled their forefathers, opposed them in religion, oppose you, and do not greet you as people greet you. They disparaged them. The Negus sent for Ja'far and his companions and said: Why do you not greet me as people greet me? They said: We have a Lord to Whom we bow to none besides; had we bowed to anyone we would bow to our Prophet. He said: Do you have anything of your Book with you? They said: Yes. Ja'far read the chapter of Mary. He said: What do you say about Jesus? He said: He is Allah's spirit and word cast to Mary. He asked his companions: What do you say? They fell silent. He took something between his fingers and they said: By Allah, they did not contradict the affair of Jesus by even this, though I disavow you. I call you to witness I believe in what was revealed to Muhammad ﷺ. Then he said: If you wish I shall equip you to join your Prophet, or if you wish remain with me until his place is secure. 'Amr schemed against 'Umara, endearing himself to the Negus's wife, taking some of her perfume. Then he told the Negus: 'Umara visits your wife; the sign is he will enter upon you tomorrow scented with her perfume. In the morning he perfumed him and said: Let us go to the king. They entered, and he smelled the perfume; the Negus recognized his scent. He ordered 'Umara seized, blew into his urethra, and he was driven out to the deserts, roaming with wild beasts. Later his family found him, gave him a drink of barley gruel that choked him, and he died. When Ja'far and his companions came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, news of the Negus's death reached him."
"9850","'Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, regarding the names of those who went to Abyssinia the first time before Ja'far and his companions: al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, Sahl ibn Bayda', 'Amir ibn Rabi'a, 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, 'Uthman ibn 'Affan with his wife Ruqayya daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, 'Uthman ibn Maz'un, Mus'ab ibn 'Umayr of Banu 'Abd al-Dar, Abu Hudhayfa ibn 'Utba ibn Rabi'a with his wife Sahla bint Suhayl ibn 'Amr — who bore him Muhammad ibn Abi Hudhayfa in Abyssinia — Abu Sabra ibn Abi Ruhm with Umm Kulthum bint Suhayl ibn 'Amr, and Abu Salama ibn 'Abd al-Asad with his wife Umm Salama. Then those first emigrants returned before Ja'far when Allah revealed the chapter mentioning By the star when it descends. The polytheists of Quraysh said: If only this man would mention our gods well, we would affirm him and his companions, for he mentions none who oppose his religion among Jews and Christians with the evil and revilement he mentions our gods with. When Allah revealed the chapter of The Star and he recited: Have you seen al-Lat and al-'Uzza and Manat the third, the other — Satan cast into it mention of the false gods: They are the high cranes whose intercession is hoped for — from Satan's chant and trial. Those two phrases fell into every polytheist's heart, their tongues hastened with them, they rejoiced, saying: Muhammad has returned to his first religion and his people's religion. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached the end of the chapter of The Star, he prostrated, and all present prostrated with him, Muslim and polytheist, except al-Walid ibn al-Mughira, an aged man who lifted a handful of dust and prostrated upon it. Both groups marveled at their prostration following the Prophet's prostration. The Muslims marveled at the polytheists' prostration without faith or certainty — they had not heard what Satan cast upon the polytheists' tongues. The polytheists felt reassured toward the Prophet ﷺ and his companions upon hearing what Satan cast into the Prophet's recitation, and Satan whispered that the Prophet ﷺ had recited it in the prostration chapter, so they prostrated to glorify their gods. The word spread until it reached Abyssinia. When 'Uthman ibn Maz'un, 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and the Meccans with them heard that people had accepted Islam and joined the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and heard of al-Walid's prostration upon dust in his palm, they hastened back. That grieved the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. In the evening Gabriel, peace be upon him, came to him; he complained to him, was commanded to recite to him, and when he reached it Gabriel disavowed it, saying: Allah forbid these two — my Lord never revealed them nor did your Lord command me with them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was distressed, saying: I obeyed Satan and spoke his words; he shared in Allah's affair with me. Then Allah abrogated what Satan cast and revealed: Never did We send before you a messenger or prophet but that when he recited Satan cast into his recitation, then Allah abrogates what Satan casts then perfects His verses, and Allah is Knowing, Wise — to make what Satan casts a trial for those in whose hearts is disease and those whose hearts are hardened; indeed the wrongdoers are in far dissension. When Allah cleared him of Satan's chant and trial, the polytheists reverted to their error and enmity. The Muslims returning from Abyssinia, nearing Mecca, could not turn back for the severity of trial, hunger, and fear, fearing entry lest they be seized. None entered except under protection. Al-Walid ibn al-Mughira protected 'Uthman ibn Maz'un. When 'Uthman saw the trial befalling the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his companions — some tortured with fire and whips — while he remained safe untouched, he preferred trial to safety. He went to al-Walid and said: O cousin, you granted me protection and did well; I wish you to take me to your clan and disavow me before them. Al-Walid said: Son of my brother, perhaps someone harmed or reviled you under my protection, and you seek one more preventive than me — I shall suffice you. He said: No, by Allah, none confronted me. When 'Uthman insisted that al-Walid disavow him, he took him to the mosque where Quraysh sat in full assembly, with Labid ibn Rabi'a the poet reciting to them. Al-Walid took 'Uthman's hand, brought him to Quraysh, and said: He has overpowered me and made me relinquish my protection; I call you to witness I am quit of him. They sat with the people, and Labid recited: Indeed everything besides Allah is false. 'Uthman said: You speak truth. Then Labid completed the verse: And every bliss must surely fade. He said: You lie. The people fell silent, not knowing his intent. He repeated it a second time, and 'Uthman again said truth once and lie once — affirming him that all perishes, but denying that all bliss fades, for the bliss of Paradise never ends. At that a man of Quraysh slapped 'Uthman ibn Maz'un's eye, greening it. Al-Walid and his companions said: You were under strong protection; you left it for this — you could have been spared. They laughed. 'Uthman said: Rather I was in need of what I met from you; my eye that was not struck longs for what its fellow met, for one dearer to me than you is my example. Al-Walid said: If you wish I shall protect you again. He said: I have no need of your protection."
"9851","'Abdullah ibn Ja'far, may Allah be pleased with them, said: When Abu Talib died, the Prophet ﷺ went out to Ta'if on foot, calling them to Islam, but they did not answer him. He withdrew to the shade of a tree, prayed two rak'as, then said: > **""O Allah, I complain to You of the weakness of my strength and my lowliness before people. Most Merciful of the merciful, You are the Most Merciful of the merciful — to whom do You entrust me? To an enemy who frowns at me, or to a near one to whom You have given my affair? If You are not angry with me, I do not care — yet Your well-being is more spacious for me. I seek refuge in Your Face by which darknesses shone and the affair of this world and the Hereafter was set right, lest Your anger descend upon me or Your wrath befall me. Yours is the reproach until You are pleased, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"9852","Raqiqa said: When the Prophet ﷺ came seeking support at Ta'if, he entered upon her, and she ordered a drink of barley gruel for him; he drank. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: > **""Do not worship their idol, nor pray toward it.""** I said: Then they will kill me! He said: > **""When they tell you that, say: Lord of this idol. And when you pray, turn your back to it.""** Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left them. The daughter of Raqiqa said: My two brothers Sufyan and Wahb, sons of Qays ibn Aban, told me: When Thaqif accepted Islam, we went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: > **""What happened to your mother?""** We said: She died in the state you left her. He said: > **""Then your mother has accepted Islam.""**"
"9853","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would present himself to people at the standing place, saying: > **""Is there any man to carry me to his people? For Quraysh have prevented me from conveying the speech of my Lord, mighty and majestic.""** A man of Hamdan came to him. He said: > **""From whom are you?""** The man said: From Hamdan. He said: > **""Does your people have strength?""** He said: Yes. Then the man feared his people would betray him, so he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: I shall go inform them, then come to you next year. He said: Yes. He departed, and the delegation of the Ansar came in Rajab."
"9854","Rabi'a ibn 'Abbad said: I was with my father as a youth, watching the Messenger of Allah ﷺ follow the tribes, with behind him a red, fair, long-haired man. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would stand before a tribe and say: > **""O sons of so-and-so, I am Allah's Messenger to you. I command you to worship Him, associating nothing with Him, to believe me and protect me until I deliver from Allah what He sent me with.""** When he finished, the other behind him would say: O sons of so-and-so, this man wants you to strip off al-Lat and al-'Uzza and your allies among the clan of Banu Malik ibn Uqaysh for the innovation and error he brought — do not listen to him nor follow him. I asked my father: Who is this? He said: This is his uncle Abu Lahab."
"9855","Mahmud ibn Labid, brother of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal, said: When Abu al-Haysar Anas ibn Rafi' came to Mecca with youths of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal, among them Iyas ibn Mu'adh, seeking alliance from Quraysh against their people of Khazraj, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard of them, came, sat with them, and said: > **""Do you desire better than what you came for?""** They said: What is that? He said: > **""I am Allah's Messenger, sent to servants to call them to worship Him, associating nothing with Him, and He revealed a Book to me.""** Then he mentioned Islam and recited the Qur'an to them. Iyas ibn Mu'adh, a young boy, said: O my people, by Allah this is better than what you came for. Abu al-Haysar Anas ibn Rafi' took a handful of dust, struck Iyas's face with it, and said: Leave us; by my life we came for other than this. Iyas fell silent, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose from them, and they returned to Medina. Then came the battle of Bu'ath between Aws and Khazraj. Soon after, Iyas ibn Mu'adh perished. Mahmud said: Those of my people who attended him told me they kept hearing him declare Allah's Oneness, magnify, praise, and glorify Him until he died; they did not doubt he died a Muslim — Islam had entered his heart in that sitting when he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what he heard."
"9856","Jarir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We pledged the Prophet ﷺ upon the like of what the women pledged: whoever among us dies without committing any of them, Paradise is guaranteed for him; whoever dies having committed any of them and had the penalty applied, it is expiation; whoever dies having committed any of them but was concealed, his reckoning is upon Allah."
"9857","Muhammad ibn al-Aswad ibn Khalaf reported that his father al-Aswad attended the Prophet ﷺ pledging people. Men, women, young, and old came and pledged him upon Islam and testimony. Muhammad ibn al-Aswad said: Testimony that there is no deity but Allah and Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"9858","'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, may Allah be pleased with them, said: Umayma bint Ruqayqa came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pledging him upon Islam. He said: > **""I pledge you that you associate nothing with Allah, do not steal, do not commit adultery, do not kill your child, do not bring slander you fabricate between your hands and feet, do not wail, and do not display yourselves as in the former ignorance.""**"
"9859","Qutba ibn Qatada said: I pledged the Prophet ﷺ for my daughter al-Huwaysila."
"9860","Kadan ibn 'Abd said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ from Yemen, pledged him, and accepted Islam at his hands."
"9861","'Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Fatima bint 'Utba ibn Rabi'a came pledging the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He took upon her: That they associate not, nor steal, nor commit adultery — the verse. She placed her hand upon her head from modesty. What he saw of her pleased the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. 'Aisha said: Affirm, O woman, for by Allah we pledged only upon this. She said: Yes, then — so he pledged her upon the verse."
"9862","'Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Hind bint 'Utba ibn Rabi'a came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to pledge him. He looked at her hands and said: > **""Go change your hands.""** She went and changed them with henna, then came. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I pledge you that you associate nothing with Allah, do not steal, and do not commit adultery.""** She said: Would a free woman commit adultery? He said: > **""Do not kill your children fearing poverty.""** She said: Has he left us children to kill? So she pledged him. Then, wearing two gold bracelets, she said to him: What do you say about these two bracelets? He said: > **""Two embers from the embers of Hell.""**"
"9863","Salma bint Qays — one of the maternal aunts of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who prayed with him toward both qiblas, of the women of Banu 'Adi ibn al-Najjar — said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and pledged him among Ansari women. When he stipulated upon us: That we associate nothing with Allah, do not steal, do not commit adultery, do not kill our children, do not bring slander we fabricate between our hands and feet, and do not disobey in good — he said: > **""And do not defraud your husbands.""** She said: So we pledged him, then left. I told one of the women: Go back and ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: What is defrauding our husbands? She asked him. He said: > **""She takes his wealth and favors another with it.""**"
"9864","Umm 'Atiyya, may Allah be pleased with her, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Medina, he gathered the Ansari women in a house, then sent 'Umar ibn al-Khattab to them. He stood at the door, greeted them, and they returned the greeting. He said: I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to you. They said: Welcome to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: Pledge that you associate nothing with Allah, do not steal, do not commit adultery, do not kill your children, do not bring slander you fabricate between your hands and feet, and do not disobey in good. They said: Yes. 'Umar extended his hand from outside the door, and they extended theirs from inside. Then he said: O Allah, bear witness. He commanded that menstruating women and secluded maidens go out for the two Eids. We were forbidden from following funerals, and no Friday prayer is upon us. I asked him about slander and His saying: Nor disobey you in good — he said: It is wailing."
"9865","'Aisha bint Qudama said: I was with my mother Ra'ita bint Sufyan al-Khuza'iyya while the Prophet ﷺ pledged the women, saying: > **""I pledge you that you associate nothing with Allah, do not steal, do not commit adultery, do not kill your children, do not bring slander you fabricate between your hands and feet, and do not disobey in good.""** They said: Yes. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Say: Yes, as far as you are able.""** So I said as they said."
"9866","Umm al-'Ala, a woman among them — Ya'qub said: He was informed — pledged the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"9867","'Azza bint Khabil reported that she came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he pledged her that she not commit adultery, nor steal, nor bury alive, openly or secretly. I said: The open burying I know; the hidden burying I did not ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about, nor did he tell me, but it occurred to me that it is spoiling the child. By Allah, I shall never spoil a child of mine."
"9868","Fatima bint 'Utba ibn Rabi'a reported that Abu Hudhayfa ibn 'Utba took her and her sister Hind to pledge the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he stipulated upon them, Hind said: Do you know among your people's women anything of such vileness? Abu Hudhayfa said: Pledge him, for thus he stipulates."
"9869","Asma bint Yazid said: I am among the women from whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took the pledge. I was a mature, bold young girl in questioning him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, extend your hand that I may shake it. He said: > **""I do not shake hands with women; rather I take upon them what Allah took upon them.""** Then she mentioned the hadith."
"9870","'Aqila bint 'Atik ibn al-Harith said: I came with my mother Qurayra bint al-Harith al-'Itwariyya among Muhajir women, and we pledged the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he had a tent pitched at al-Abtah. He took upon us that we associate nothing with Allah — the whole verse. When we affirmed and extended our hands to pledge him, he said: > **""I do not grasp women's hands.""** He sought forgiveness for us, and that was our pledge."
"9871","Ma'qil ibn Yasar reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to shake hands with women from beneath the garment."
"9872","'Urwa ibn Mas'ud al-Thaqafi said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would have water with him; when he pledged women, they dipped their hands in the water."
"9873","Ibn 'Abbas reported from the Prophet ﷺ that when he pledged women he said: > **""Do not display yourselves as in the former ignorance.""** A woman said: O Messenger of Allah, you stipulate upon us not to display, yet so-and-so woman helped me, and her brother has died. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Go console her, then come pledge me.""**"
"9874","Abu Nasr said: Ibn 'Abbas was asked: How did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ test the women? He said: When a woman came to accept Islam, he made her swear by Allah she had not left from hatred of her husband, by Allah she had not left to gain worldly life, by Allah she had not left one land for another, and by Allah she had left only from love of Allah and His Messenger."
"9875","Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn al-Husayn reported that the Prophet ﷺ pledged al-Hasan and al-Husayn, 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, and 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far while they were small, before sprouting hair, before puberty — and he pledged no small child except from us."
"9876","'Urwa said: When the season came, a group of Ansar of Banu Mazin ibn al-Najjar performed Hajj, among them Mu'adh ibn 'Afra' and As'ad ibn Zurara; of Banu Zurayq: Rafi' ibn Malik and Dhakwan ibn 'Abd Qays; of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal: Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan; of Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf: 'Uwaym ibn Sa'ida. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them, told them of what Allah had chosen him with of prophethood and honor, and recited the Qur'an to them. Hearing his words, they listened, their souls trusted his call, recognizing what they had heard from the People of the Book mentioning him with his description and call. So they believed and were among the causes of good. They said to him: You know the bloodshed between Aws and Khazraj. We love what Allah has guided your affair with, striving for Allah and you. We counsel what you see: remain, in Allah's name, until we return to our people, inform them of your affair, and call them to Allah and His Messenger. Perhaps Allah will reconcile us and unite our affair. Today we are distant and hateful; if you come now unreconciled, we shall have no unity upon you. We promise you next year's season. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted their words. They returned calling secretly, informing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his mission, reciting the Qur'an, until hardly a house of the Ansar remained but some had accepted Islam. Then they sent to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Send us a man from you to call people by Allah's Book, for he is more likely followed. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Mus'ab ibn 'Umayr of Banu 'Abd al-Dar, who alighted among Banu Ghanm with As'ad ibn Zurara, calling secretly. Islam spread and its people multiplied, concealed in their call. Then As'ad and Mus'ab went to the well of Marr or near it, sat, and sent for a group who came concealed. While Mus'ab narrated and recited the Qur'an, Sa'd ibn Mu'adh was told of them. He came in armor with a spear, stood over them, and said: Why does this lone, solitary, outcast stranger come into our homes, misleading our weak with falsehood and calling them to it? I shall see nothing of your neighborhood after this. They left, then returned a second time at the well. Sa'd was told again, threatened them less harshly. Seeing leniency, As'ad said: O son of my maternal aunt, hear his words; if you hear evil, reject it, and if you hear good, answer Allah. He said: What does he say? Mus'ab recited: Ha Mim, by the clear Book — We made it an Arabic Qur'an that you may understand. Sa'd said: I hear only what I recognize. He returned, Allah having guided him, though he did not reveal Islam until he returned to his people. He called Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal to Islam, revealed his Islam, saying: Whoever doubts, small or great, male or female — let him bring us better than it, and we shall take it. By Allah, an affair has come for which necks shall grieve. Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal accepted Islam upon Sa'd's Islam and call, except the unmentioned. It was the first of the Ansar's houses to accept wholly. Then Banu al-Najjar expelled Mus'ab and pressed As'ad ibn Zurara, so Mus'ab moved to Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, continually calling, Allah guiding at his hands until hardly an Ansar house remained but some had accepted, their nobles accepted, 'Amr ibn al-Jamuh accepted, their idols were broken. The Muslims were the mightiest of its people, their affair set right. Mus'ab returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, called The Reciter."
"9877","Ibn Ishaq said: When Allah willed to manifest His religion, honor His Prophet ﷺ, and fulfill His promise, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out in the season in which the group of Ansar met him — six, it is claimed, among them Jabir ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ri'ab."
"9878","Ka'b ibn Malik said: When twelve men came from 'Aqaba, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had commanded them to bring seventy men the next year, we remained a year, each walking to his companion with counsel, provision, and food, until seventy men of us met him."
"9879","'Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood offering himself to the Arab tribes, tribe by tribe, in the season, finding none to answer him, until Allah brought this clan of the Ansar — Allah making them fortunate, driving honor to them. They sheltered and aided. May Allah reward them for their Prophet with good. By Allah, we did not fulfill for them as we pledged: we said to them: We are the princes and you the ministers. If I remain until next year, no youth of mine shall remain except Ansari."
"9880","Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would present himself every year to the Arab tribes, asking them to shelter him among their people so that he could convey the speech of Allah and His messages, and Paradise would be theirs. Yet no tribe among the Arabs responded to him, until Allah willed to manifest His religion, aid His Prophet, and fulfill what He had promised him. So Allah drove him to this clan of the Ansar, and they responded to him, and Allah made for His Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, a home of migration."
"9881","Kab ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who was among those present at Aqaba and who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, narrated: We set out with the pilgrims of our people who were polytheists, and we had already prayed and gained understanding. With us was Al-Bara ibn Marur, our elder and chief. When we set out on our journey and left Madina, Al-Bara said to us, By Allah, I have formed an opinion, and by Allah I do not know whether you will agree with me or not. We said, What is it. He said, I have decided never to turn my back on this Building, meaning the Kaba, but to pray facing it. We said, By Allah, we have only heard that our Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, prays facing Syria, and we do not wish to oppose him. We said, We will not do it. Whenever prayer came, we prayed toward Syria while he prayed toward the Kaba until we reached Mecca. My brother said, We had reproached him for what he did, but he refused except to persist. When we arrived at Mecca he said, O son of my brother, let us go to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, so I may ask him about what I did on this journey, for by Allah something has entered my heart because of your disagreement with me. So we went out asking about the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, not knowing him, never having seen him before. A man of Mecca met us, and we asked him about the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. He said, Do you know him. We said, No. He said, Do you know Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, his uncle. We said, Yes, for we knew Al-Abbas, who used to come to us as a trader. He said, Enter the mosque, he is the man sitting with Al-Abbas. So we entered the mosque, and Al-Abbas was sitting with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. We greeted and sat. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said to Al-Abbas: > **""Do you know these two men, O Abu al-Fadl.""** He said, Yes, this is Al-Bara ibn Marur, chief of his people, and this is Kab ibn Malik. I never forget the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saying, The poet. He said, Yes. Then Al-Bara said, O Prophet of Allah, I set out on this journey while Allah had guided me to Islam, so I refused to turn my back on this Building and prayed toward it, but my companions opposed me until doubt entered my heart. What do you think, O Messenger of Allah. He said: > **""You were upon a qibla, had you remained steadfast upon it.""** So Al-Bara returned to the qibla of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and prayed with us toward Syria, though his family continued praying toward the Kaba until he died, not as others claimed. We then went for Hajj and appointed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, at Aqaba in the middle of the days of Tashriq. When we finished Hajj, on the night appointed, with us was Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Haram, Abu Jabir, a chief among our chiefs. We concealed our affair from our polytheist people, so we spoke to him and said, O Abu Jabir, you are a chief and noble, and we desire for you to escape what you are in lest you become fuel for the Fire tomorrow. Then we invited him to Islam and informed him of the appointment, so he accepted Islam and witnessed Aqaba with us and became a naqib. We slept that night with our people in our camps until a third of the night had passed, then we slipped out stealthily like sand-grouse until we gathered in the valley at Aqaba, seventy men with two women, Nasiba bint Kab, Umm Umara of Bani Mazin ibn al-Najjar, and Asma daughter of Amr ibn Adi of Bani Salama, mother of Mani. We waited until the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came with his uncle Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, who was still upon his people's religion but wished to attend his nephew's affair and secure terms for him. Al-Abbas spoke first and said, O Khazraj, for the Arabs called both Aws and Khazraj by that name, Muhammad is from us as you know, and we have protected him from our people who share our view, and he is in honor among his people and protection in his land. We said, We have heard what you said, so speak, O Messenger of Allah, and take for your Lord and yourself what you love. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, spoke, recited, called to Allah, and encouraged Islam, saying: > **""I pledge you that you protect me as you protect your women and children.""** Al-Bara took his hand and said, Yes, by Him who sent you with truth, we will protect you as we protect our own honor, so pledge us, O Messenger of Allah, for we are people of war and arms inherited from elder to elder. But Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan, ally of Bani Abd al-Ashhal, interrupted while Al-Bara was speaking and said, O Messenger of Allah, between us and some men are bonds, meaning covenants, and we will sever them. If we do so and Allah grants you victory, will you return and leave us. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, smiled and said: > **""Rather, blood is blood, and destruction is destruction. You are from me and I am from you. I wage war against whom you wage war, and make peace with whom you make peace.""** And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Bring forth to me twelve naqibs from among you to stand over their people.""** So they brought forth twelve, nine from Khazraj and three from Aws, among them Asad ibn Zurara for Bani al-Najjar, Al-Bara ibn Marur and Abdullah ibn Amr for Bani Salama, Sad ibn Ubada and Al-Mundhir ibn Amr for Bani Saida, Rafi ibn Malik for Bani Zurayq, Abdullah ibn Rawaha and Sad ibn al-Rabi for Bani al-Harith ibn Khazraj, Ubada ibn al-Samit for Bani Awf, Usayd ibn Hudayr and Abu al-Haytham for Bani Abd al-Ashhal, and Sad ibn Khaythama for Bani Amr ibn Awf. Mabad ibn Kab reported from his brother from his father Kab that the first to strike his hand upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was Al-Bara ibn Marur, then the people pledged. When we pledged, Satan shouted from the top of Aqaba in the most piercing voice I ever heard, O people of the dwellings, do you want Mudhammam and the apostates with him who have agreed to war against you. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""This is Azab al-Aqaba, son of Azyab. Hear, O enemy of Allah, by Allah I shall soon deal with you.""** Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Return to your camps.""** Al-Abbas ibn Ubada ibn Nadla said, By Him who sent you with truth, if you wish we will fall upon the people of Mina tomorrow with our swords. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""We have not been commanded with that.""** We returned and slept till morning. In the morning the chiefs of Quraysh came to our dwellings and said, O Khazraj, it has reached us that you came to this companion of ours to extract him from among us and pledge him to war against us. By Allah, there is no Arab with whom we hate war between us more than you. Our polytheists swore by Allah that nothing of this happened and they knew nothing, and they spoke truthfully, for they did not know. We looked at one another. Among them was Al-Harith ibn Hisham wearing new sandals. I said a word as if to share in their denial, How can you, O Abu Jabir, chief among chiefs, not take sandals like this Qurayshi youth. Al-Harith heard, removed them and threw them to me, saying, By Allah you shall wear them. Abu Jabir said, You have alarmed the youth, return his sandals. I said, By Allah I will not return them. By Allah, if the omen proves true, I shall surely strip him."
"9882","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, spent ten years following the people in their dwellings at Ukaz and Majanna, and at Mina during the season, saying: > **""Who will shelter me, who will aid me so that I may convey the message of my Lord, and Paradise will be his.""** Until a man would come from Yemen or from Mudar, and his people would come to him saying, Beware the youth of Quraysh, lest he tempt you, while he walked among their camps and they pointed at him with fingers. Until Allah sent us from Yathrib, so we sheltered him and believed him. A man among us would go, believe in him, and he would teach him the Quran, then he would return to his family and they would accept Islam through his Islam, until no house among the Ansar remained except therein were Muslims manifesting Islam. Then they all conspired and said, How long shall we leave the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, driven about in the mountains of Mecca, fearful. So seventy men among us rode to him in the season and appointed the valley of Aqaba. They gathered there one by one and two by two until we were complete. We said, O Messenger of Allah, upon what shall we pledge you. He said: > **""You pledge me to hear and obey in energy and lassitude, to spend in hardship and ease, to enjoin good and forbid evil, to speak for Allah fearing no blame of any blamer, and to aid me and protect me when I come to you as you protect yourselves, your wives, and your children, and Paradise is yours.""** So we rose to him and pledged him. Asad ibn Zurara, the youngest, took his hand and said, Gently, O people of Yathrib, for we only undertook this journey knowing he is the Messenger of Allah, and taking him out today means severance from all Arabs, killing of the best among you, and biting of swords. If you fear weakness from yourselves, then say so now, for it is an excuse before Allah. They said, O Asad, remove your hand from us, for by Allah we shall never abandon this pledge nor rescind it."
"9883","In a narration with Ahmad, he said, If you fear any apprehension from yourselves. This is a brief variant of the pledge narration, in which Asad ibn Zurara cautioned the people of Yathrib to consider the consequences of war and to declare any fear before pledging."
"9884","In another narration with him, the wording is, Until a man would journey from Mudar, from Yemen. This is a brief variant describing how men coming for Hajj were warned against the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, while he called them to shelter him."
"9885","Urwa, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated: The first to pledge the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan. He said, O Messenger of Allah, between us and other people are bonds, meaning oaths and covenants, and perhaps we shall sever them, then you will return to your people after we have severed bonds and fought people. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, laughed at his words and said: > **""Blood is blood, destruction is destruction.""** When Abu al-Haytham was pleased with the reply, he turned to his people and said, O my people, this is the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. I testify he is truthful. Today he is in Allah's sanctuary and security among his people and kin. Know that if you take him out, the Arabs will shoot at you with one bow. If your souls are content to fight in Allah's path and lose wealth and children, then invite him to your land, for he is truly the Messenger of Allah. If you fear betrayal, then from now. Thereupon they said, We accept from Allah and His Messenger what he gives us, and we give you of ourselves what you asked, O Messenger of Allah. Leave between us, O Abu al-Haytham, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, so we may pledge him. Abu al-Haytham said, I am the first to pledge, then all pledged. Satan shouted from the mountain top, O Quraysh, these Khazraj and Aws are pledging Muhammad to war against you. They were alarmed. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Let not this voice alarm you, for he is Allah's enemy Iblis, whom none of those you fear has heard.""** The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, stood and shouted at Satan: > **""O son of the snake, this is your doing. I shall soon be free for you.""**"
"9886","Abu Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, appointed us at the base of Aqaba on the day of sacrifice, while we were seventy men. Uqba said, I was the youngest. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came and said: > **""Be brief in speech, for I fear for you the disbelievers of Quraysh.""** We said, O Messenger of Allah, stipulate for your Lord and yourself and your companions, and inform us what reward from Allah and from you is ours. He said: > **""As for what I ask for my Lord, that you believe in Him and associate nothing with Him. As for what I ask for myself, I ask that you obey me so I may guide you to righteousness. And I ask for myself and my companions that you console us from your wealth and protect us as you protect yourselves. If you do so, Paradise from Allah and from me is yours.""** So we extended our hands and pledged him."
"9887","Al-Shabi, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, went with his uncle Al-Abbas to the seventy Ansar at Aqaba under the tree. He said: > **""Let your spokesman speak but not prolong, for the polytheists have eyes upon you, and if they learn of you they will expose you.""** Their spokesman, Abu Umama, said, Ask, O Muhammad, for your Lord what you wish, then ask for yourself and your companions what you wish, then inform us what reward from Allah and from you is ours if we do so. He said: > **""I ask for my Lord that you worship Him and associate nothing with Him, and I ask for myself and my companions that you shelter us, aid us, and protect us as you protect yourselves.""** They said, What is ours if we do so. He said: > **""Paradise is yours.""** They said, Then it is settled."
"9888","Al-Shabi, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Neither the old nor the young ever heard a sermon like it. This is an athar praising the address given at the Aqaba pledge."
"9889","Abu al-Zubayr, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated: I asked Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, about Aqaba. He said, Seventy witnessed it, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, made a covenant with them while Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib held his hand. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""I have taken and given.""**"
"9890","Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: When the nuqaba of the Ansar met the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, he said to them: > **""You will shelter me and protect me.""** They said, What is ours. He said: > **""Paradise is yours.""**"
"9891","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: Thabit ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed upon the arrival of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, saying, We shall protect you as we protect ourselves and our children. What is ours, O Messenger of Allah. He said: > **""Paradise is yours.""** They said, We are pleased."
"9892","Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: My uncle Jadd ibn Qays carried me among the seventy riders who came as a delegation from the Ansar to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, on the night of Aqaba. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came with his uncle Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib and said: > **""O uncle, take pledge upon my maternal relatives.""** The seventy said, O Muhammad, stipulate for your Lord and yourself whatever you wish. He said: > **""What I ask for my Lord is that you worship Him and associate nothing with Him, and what I ask for myself is that you protect me as you protect yourselves.""** They said, What is ours if we do so. He said: > **""Paradise.""**"
"9893","Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: We were with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, on the night of Aqaba. Jabir said, My two maternal uncles brought me out, while I was unable even to throw a stone, due to young age."
"9894","Urwa, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated: Al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said, By Allah, he took the hand of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, when the seventy Ansar came to him at Aqaba, and he took for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, pledges from them and stipulated conditions upon them. That was in the dawn of Islam and its beginning, before anyone worshipped Allah openly."
"9895","Ubada ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: Asad ibn Zurara, may Allah be pleased with him, said, O people, do you know upon what you pledge Muhammad, peace and blessings be upon him. You pledge him to wage war against Arabs and non-Arabs, jinn and men. They said, We are at war with whom he wars and at peace with whom he is at peace. They said, O Messenger of Allah, stipulate. He said: > **""You pledge me to testify there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah, to establish prayer, give zakat, hear and obey, not to dispute authority with its people, and to protect me as you protect yourselves and your families.""**"
"9896","Husayn ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: The Ansar came to pledge the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, at Aqaba, and he said: > **""O Ali, stand, O Ali, and pledge them.""** He said, Upon what shall I pledge them, O Messenger of Allah. He said: > **""That Allah be obeyed and not disobeyed, and that you protect the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and his household and progeny as you protect yourselves and your offspring.""**"
"9897","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""I was sent before the Hour with the sword until Allah alone without partner is worshipped, my provision was placed under the shade of my spear, and humiliation and disgrace were placed upon whoever opposes my command.""**"
"9898","Ibn Shihab, may Allah have mercy on him, named those who attended Aqaba: From the Ansar, then Bani al-Najjar: Aws ibn Thabit, Aws ibn Yazid ibn Asram, and Abu Umama Asad ibn Zurara. From Bani Salama: Al-Bara ibn Marur, the first to bequeath a third of his wealth and to face the Kaba in his land, and he was a naqib. From Bani al-Harith ibn Khazraj: Bashir ibn Sad ibn al-Numan. From the Ansar: Jabir ibn Abdillah ibn Amr, Jabbar ibn Sakhr. From Bani Zurayq: Al-Harith ibn Qays ibn Malik who witnessed Badr, Dhakwan ibn Abd al-Qays ibn Khalda, Rafi ibn Malik who witnessed Badr. From Bani Hubla: Rifaa ibn Amr. From Bani Saida ibn Kab: Sad ibn Ubada, a naqib. From Bani Amr ibn Awf: Sad ibn Khaythama, a naqib. From Bani Abd al-Ashhal: Salama ibn Salama ibn Waqsh. From Bani Salama: Kab ibn Malik. From Bani Haritha ibn al-Harith: Zahir ibn Rafi. From Bani Haritha: Abu Burda ibn Niyar. This is an athar listing names, with a single chain to Ibn Shihab whose men are trustworthy."
"9899","Urwa, may Allah have mercy on him, named the companions of Aqaba who pledged the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him: From Bani Salama ibn Yazid ibn Jusham: Al-Bara ibn Marur ibn Sakhr ibn Khansa, a naqib, the first to bequeath a third and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, permitted it. From Bani Haritha: Bahir ibn al-Haytham. From the Ansar: Thabit ibn Ajda. From the Ansar: Jabir ibn Abdillah ibn Amr ibn Haram ibn Kab ibn Ghanm ibn Kab ibn Salama. From Bani Zurayq: Al-Harith ibn Qays ibn Makhlad who witnessed Badr. From Bani Bayada: Zayd ibn Labid. From Bani al-Harith ibn Khazraj: Sad ibn al-Rabi ibn Abi Zuhayr. From Bani al-Najjar: Sahl ibn Atik. From Bani Haritha: Zahir ibn Rafi. From Bani Mazin ibn al-Najjar: Amr ibn Ghaziya with extended lineage. From Bani al-Harith ibn Khazraj: Uqba ibn Amr ibn Thalaba, known as Abu Masud. From Bani Salama: Kab ibn Malik ibn Abi al-Qayn. This is an athar of names transmitted with one chain."
"9900","Abu Umama ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: Asad ibn Zurara, may Allah be pleased with him, was one of the nuqaba on the night of Aqaba. This is an athar report."
"9901","Kab ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: We set out in the Hajj in which we pledged the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, at Aqaba. The naqib of Bani Zurayq was Rafi ibn Malik ibn al-Ajlan, and the naqibs of Bani Saida were Sad ibn Ubada and Al-Mundhir ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them."
"9902","Urwa, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, remained after Hajj the rest of Dhu al-Hijja, Muharram, and Safar. Then the polytheists of Quraysh resolved their affair and plot when they thought the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would depart, and they learned Allah had made for him in Madina a refuge and protection, and news of the Ansars Islam and the Emigrants who went to them reached them. So they agreed to seize the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, either to kill him, imprison him, drag him, expel him, or bind him. Allah informed him of their plot, revealing, They plot against you to confine you or kill you or expel you, they plot and Allah plots, and Allah is the best of plotters. On the day this reached him, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came to Abu Bakr's house, and it reached them that they would attack him by night upon his bed. He left under cover of night with Abu Bakr toward the cave at Thawr, the cave mentioned in the Quran. Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, lay upon his bed to conceal him from eyes. The polytheists spent the night debating whether to fall upon the sleeper and bind him, until morning, when Ali rose from the bed. They asked him about the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and he said he had no knowledge of him. Then they knew he had left, so they rode in every direction seeking him, sent to the watering places promising great reward, and came to Thawr where the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and Abu Bakr were. Abu Bakr feared, and the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Grieve not, indeed Allah is with us.""** Allah diverted them. They remained three nights, with Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr bringing news at night and Amir ibn Fuhayra grazing sheep to erase tracks. Then the guide Abdullah ibn Urayqit brought two mounts, and they set out along the coast, while Suraqa ibn Malik pursued for the bounty but his horse stumbled, so he sought amnesty and returned, diverting seekers. They continued until Quba."
"9903","Mariya, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated: I lowered myself for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, until he climbed a wall, on the night he fled from the polytheists."
"9904","Abu Musab al-Makki reported: I met Zayd ibn Arqam, Al-Mughira ibn Shuba, and Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with them, narrating that on the night the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, spent in the cave, Allah commanded a tree which grew before the cave and screened the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him. Allah commanded a spider which wove over the cave mouth. Allah commanded two wild doves which perched at the cave entrance. The polytheists came from every pass until they were forty cubits from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, with bows and staffs. One advanced, saw the two doves, returned and said to his companions, There is nothing in the cave, I saw two doves at its mouth, so I knew no one is inside. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, heard his words and knew Allah had repelled them by the two doves, so he blessed them, prescribed their recompense, and kept two chicks in Allah's sanctuary. I think he said, The origin of every dove in the sanctuary is from their chicks."
"9905","Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, used to come to us at Mecca twice daily. One day he came at noon. I said, O father, this is the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. May my father and mother be ransom, he has come only for some matter. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Have you felt that Allah has permitted me to depart.""** Abu Bakr said, Companionship, O Messenger of Allah. He said: > **""Companionship.""** Abu Bakr said, I have two mounts which I have fed for such and such time awaiting this day, so take one. He said: > **""For its price, O Abu Bakr.""** He said, For its price, may my father and mother be ransom, if you wish. So we prepared provisions, and I cut my waist-band and tied part of it. They left and stayed in the cave at Mount Thawr. When they reached it, Abu Bakr entered before him and blocked every hole with his fingers fearing vermin. Quraysh, missing them, offered a hundred she-camels for the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and searched the mountains until they reached the mountain. Abu Bakr saw a man facing the cave and said, O Messenger of Allah, he sees us. He said: > **""No, angels screen us with their wings.""** The man sat and urinated facing the cave. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Had he seen us, he would not have done this.""** They stayed three nights, while Amir ibn Fuhayra, freedman of Abu Bakr, brought Abu Bakr's sheep in the evening and left before dawn with the shepherds, returning at night. Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr brought Meccan news each night. After three nights their guide Abdullah ibn Urayqit brought the mounts, Asma brought provisions, and they set out by the lower route toward Madina, pursued by Suraqa, whose horse sank until he sought writing of amnesty, which Amir wrote for him."
"9906","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: A polytheist came and faced the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, urinating with his private part exposed, while we were in the cave. I said, O Messenger of Allah, does the man not see us. He said: > **""Had he seen us, he would not have faced us with his private part.""**"
"9907","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and Abu Bakr emigrated and entered the cave, there was a burrow, so Abu Bakr placed his heel over it until morning fearing something might emerge against the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. They stayed three nights, then departed until they stopped at the tents of Umm Mabad. She sent word, I see handsome faces, and the tribe is more able to honor you than I. When evening came she sent with a small son a knife and a ewe. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Return the knife and bring me a vessel.""** Meaning a cup. She sent that there is no milk nor offspring in her. He said: > **""Bring me a vessel.""** She brought a vessel, he stroked its back, it spread its legs and yielded milk. He milked, filled the cup, drank, gave Abu Bakr to drink, and sent some to Umm Mabad."
"9908","Aws ibn Abdillah ibn Hujr al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, passed by me with Abu Bakr at Hadhwat between Juhfa and Harsha, both upon one camel heading for Madina. I mounted them upon my stud camel, Ibn al-Rida, and sent with them my slave Masud, saying, Take them by protected paths and do not leave them while they need you and your camel. He took them by Thaniyyat al-Zamha, then Thaniyyat al-Kuwa, then al-Murra, then Shubat Dhat Kasht, then al-Mudlaja, then al-Ghassana, then Thaniyyat al-Murra, then brought them to Madina after their need was fulfilled. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, returned Masud to his master Aws, who was heedless and did not brand camels. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, ordered him to tell Aws to brand them on their necks like a horse's fetter. Sakhr ibn Malik said, By Allah they brand them today, and the horse's fetter as I see is two rings with extension between them."
"9909","Burayda al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, approached during his migration, he met riders and said: > **""O Abu Bakr, ask the people who they are.""** They said, From Aslam. He said: > **""You are safe, O Abu Bakr. Ask them from which Aslam.""** They said, From Bani Sahm. He said: > **""Cast your arrow, O Abu Bakr.""**"
"9910","Hubaysh ibn Khalid, companion of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, left Mecca emigrating to Madina with Abu Bakr, freedman Amir ibn Fuhayra, and their guide Al-Laythi Abdullah ibn al-Urayqit, they passed the two tents of Umm Mabad al-Khuzaiya, a strong dignified woman sitting in the tent courtyard who gave water and food. They asked to buy meat and dates, but found nothing, for the people were in drought. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saw a goat in the tent corner and said: > **""What is this goat, O Umm Mabad.""** She said, Hunger kept it behind from the flock. He said: > **""Is there milk in her.""** She said, She is too exhausted for that. He said: > **""Do you permit me to milk her.""** She said, May my father and mother be ransom for you, yes, if you see milking in her then milk her. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, called for her, wiped her udder, mentioned Allah, supplicated, and she spread her legs, yielded and continued. He called for a vessel sufficient for a group, milked copiously until foam rose, gave her to drink till satisfied, gave his companions till satisfied, drank last, peace and blessings be upon him, then rested, then milked again after a while till full, left it with her, pledged her, and departed. Soon her husband Abu Mabad came driving lean goats with little marrow. Seeing milk he wondered, Whence this milk, O Umm Mabad, while the goat is distant without calf and no milch in the house. She said, By Allah, only that a blessed man passed, of such and such state. He said, Describe him to me. She said, I saw a man manifestly radiant, bright-faced, good-mannered, neither marred by bulk nor belittled by smallness, handsome proportioned, with intense black eyes, long lashes, resonant voice, elevated neck, dense beard, arched eyebrows joined, dignified when silent, majestic when speaking, most beautiful from afar and sweetest near, speech sweet, decisive, neither idle nor scant, like strung pearls descending, middle-statured, neither wearying tall nor contemptibly short, a branch between two branches, the most striking of the three in appearance and noblest in rank. He has companions surrounding him, when he speaks they listen, when he commands they hasten, neither frowning nor trivial. Abu Mabad said, By Allah he is the companion of Quraysh whose affair was mentioned at Mecca. I intended to accompany him and shall do so if I find a way. A loud voice was heard at Mecca, hearing the voice but not knowing its owner, saying verses: May the Lord of men reward with best reward the two companions who stopped at Umm Mabad's tents. They alighted with guidance and she was guided, fortunate is who becomes companion of Muhammad. O Qusay, what Allah has diverted from you of unmatched deeds and glory. Congratulations to Bani Kab for the maiden's place, her seat a watchpost for believers. Ask your sister of her goat and vessel, if you ask the goat it will testify. He called for a barren goat and its udder flowed with pure frothing milk. He left it as pledge with a milker returning in source and watering. When Hassan ibn Thabit heard, he answered the caller with verses: Lost are a people whose Prophet departed them, holy is he who journeys to them morning and evening. He departed a people whose minds were foolish and alighted upon a people with renewed light. Their Lord guided them through him after misguidance, and whoever seeks truth is guided. Are misguided foolish folk equal to one guided guiding every guided. There alighted upon Yathrib riders of guidance in most fortunate abode. A Prophet who sees what people around him see not, reciting Allah's Book in every mosque. If he speaks one day of the absent, its confirmation comes that day or next morning. Congratulations to Abu Bakr for his grandfather's fortune in his companionship, whoever Allah makes fortunate is fortunate. Congratulations to Bani Kab for the maiden's place, her seat a watchpost for believers."
"9911","Qays ibn al-Numan, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and Abu Bakr went secretly, they stopped at Abu Mabad. He said, By Allah we have no sheep, and our sheep are pregnant, no milk remains. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""What is that goat.""** They brought it, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, supplicated blessing over it, then milked a large cup and gave him to drink, then they drank. Abu Mabad said, Are you the one Quraysh claim has apostatized. He said: > **""They say so.""** He said, I testify what you brought is truth. Then he said, Shall I follow you. He said: > **""No, until you hear that we have prevailed.""** So he followed him afterwards."
"9912","Faid, freedman of Abadil, narrated: I went out with Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Rabia. Ibrahim sent to Ibn Sad, and when we were at al-Arj, Ibn Sad came to us. Sad is the one who guided the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, to his riding route. Ibrahim said, Inform me what your father narrated to you. Ibn Sad said, My father narrated that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came to them with Abu Bakr, and Abu Bakr had a foster daughter among us. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, wished to shorten the route to Madina, so Sad said, This low rugged part of his riding has two thieves from Aslam called Al-Muhanan, the two disgraced. If you wish we take that way. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Take us by them.""** Sad said, We set out until we overlooked them, when one said to his companion, This is the Yemeni. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, called them and presented Islam, so they accepted Islam. Then he asked their names. They said, We are Al-Muhanan. He said: > **""Rather you are Al-Mukarraman, the two honored.""** He ordered them to come to him at Madina. We went until we reached outer Quba, where Bani Amr ibn Awf met them. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Where is Abu Umama Asad ibn Zurara.""** Sad ibn Khaythama said, He was stricken before me, O Messenger of Allah, shall I not inform him of you. Then he proceeded until overlooking the palms, where the drinking place was full. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, turned to Abu Bakr and said: > **""O Abu Bakr, this is the dwelling I was shown descending to basins like the basins of Bani Mudlij.""**"
"9913","Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, departed, Abu Bakr departed with him, taking all his wealth, five thousand or six thousand dirhams. My grandfather Abu Quhafa, whose sight had gone, came and said, By Allah I think he has distressed you by taking his wealth with himself. I said, No, father, he has left much good for us. I took stones, placed them in the niche where my father kept money, covered with cloth, took his hand and said, Father, place your hand upon this wealth. He placed his hand and said, No harm if he left this, he has done well, and this suffices you. By Allah he left nothing, but I wished to reassure the elder thereby."
"9914","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, emigrated, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, rode while Abu Bakr was behind him. Abu Bakr knew the road through travel to Syria, so when they passed people, they asked, Who is this before you. He would say, This one guides me. When they neared Madina, he sent to the Ansar Muslims, to Abu Umama and his companions. They came out to them and said, Enter safe and obeyed. So they entered."
"9915","Suhayb, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""I have been shown your migration home, a salty land between two lava tracts, either Hajr or Yathrib.""** He said, The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, departed for Madina with Abu Bakr. I intended to depart with him, but youths of Quraysh detained me. That night I kept standing and not sitting. They said, Allah has distracted him from you with his belly, though I was not ill. They slept, so I departed. Some pursued after I traveled to return me. I said, Will you accept that I give you ounces of gold and a striped garment at Mecca, and you let me go with covenant. They did, so I followed them to Mecca and said, Dig under the threshold, beneath it are the ounces, and go to such woman with such sign and take the two garments. I departed until I reached the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, at Quba before he moved. When he saw me he said thrice: > **""O Abu Yahya, profitable was the sale.""** I said, O Messenger of Allah, none preceded me to you, and none informed you except Gabriel, peace be upon him."
"9916","Al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The first of the Emigrants to come to us was Musab ibn Umayr, brother of Bani Abd al-Dar ibn Qusay, may Allah be pleased with him. I said to him, What has the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, done. He said, He is in his place, and his companions follow after me."
"9917","Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: We had deemed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, slow in coming to us. The Ansar would go to the back of the lava field and sit until the day rose high. When the sun grew hot they returned to their homes. Umar said, We were awaiting the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, when a Jewish man atop one of their fortresses shouted loudly, O Arabs, this is your companion whom you await. Umar said, I heard commotion among Bani Amr ibn Awf, put out my head, and the Muslims had donned arms. I set out with the people at noon, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, turned right until he alighted among Bani Amr ibn Awf."
"9918","Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: We gathered for migration. I, Ayyash ibn Abi Rabia, and Hisham ibn al-As appointed al-Maydat of Bani Ghifar above Shurf, saying, Whoever does not appear there by morning is detained, so his two companions should proceed. Hisham ibn al-As was detained from us. When we reached our dwelling among Bani Amr ibn Awf, Abu Jahl ibn Hisham and Al-Harith ibn Hisham went to Ayyash ibn Abi Rabia, their cousin and maternal brother, until they came to us at Madina and spoke to him, saying, Your mother vowed no comb shall touch her head until she sees you. He softened toward her. I said, O Ayyash, by Allah they only wish to turn you from your religion, so beware. By Allah, if lice trouble your mother she will comb, and if Meccan heat intensifies upon her she will comb. He said, I have wealth there to take. I said, By Allah you know I am among Quraysh's wealthiest, half my wealth is yours, do not go with them. He refused except to go with them. When he refused, I said, Since you insist, take this she-camel of mine, docile, keep to her back, and if you suspect treachery, spur her. He went with them upon her until partway, Abu Jahl said, By Allah my camel is slow, will you carry me upon your she-camel. He said, Yes. He made her kneel and they knelt to transfer. When level on ground, they assaulted him, bound him, brought him to Mecca, tempted him and he was tempted. We used to say, By Allah Allah accepts neither exchange nor ransom from one tempted, nor repentance of people who knew Allah then returned to disbelief for affliction. They said that to themselves. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, arrived at Madina, Allah revealed concerning them and our saying and their saying to themselves: O My servants who transgressed against themselves, despair not of Allah's mercy, indeed Allah forgives all sins, indeed He is the Forgiving, Merciful, until, before punishment comes suddenly while you perceive not. Umar said, I wrote it on a sheet and sent it to Hisham ibn al-As. Hisham said, I kept reciting it at Dhu Tuwa, ascending therein until I understood it. Then it was cast into my heart that it was revealed concerning us and what we said to ourselves and was said about us. So I returned, mounted my camel, and joined the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, at Madina."
"9919","Urwa, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated: Umar ibn al-Khattab and Ayyash ibn Abi Rabia with companions set out and alighted among Bani Amr ibn Awf. Abu Jahl ibn Hisham and Al-Harith ibn Hisham sought Ayyash, their maternal brother. They came to Madina and mentioned his mother's grief, saying, She swore no house shall shade her nor oil touch her head until she sees you. Had it not been so we would not seek you. We remind you by Allah concerning your mother. He was merciful to her and knew her love, so he believed them and softened. He refused to follow them until Al-Harith ibn Hisham made covenant to him. When he went with them, they bound him. He remained bound until he came out with those who came out before the conquest of Mecca. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, had supplicated for his deliverance and protection."
"9920","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: We used to say, There is no repentance for one tempted who abandons his religion after Islam and knowledge. So Allah revealed concerning them: O My servants who transgressed against themselves, despair not of Allah's mercy, until, before punishment comes suddenly while you perceive not. I wrote it by hand and sent it to Hisham ibn al-As ibn Wail. Hisham said, When it came, I recited it aloud but did not understand, then it fell into my heart that it was revealed concerning us and what we said. So I mounted my camel and joined Madina. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, remained awaiting permission for migration, while his Emigrant companions had arrived in groups. Abu Bakr had sought permission for migration, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Do not hasten, perhaps Allah will make for you a companion.""** Abu Bakr hoped the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, meant himself. Abu Bakr had prepared two mounts, feeding them in his house for that."
"9921","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: May Allah curse whoever claims I migrated before my father. He only sent me ahead with his baggage. This is an athar report."
"9922","Ibn Ishaq, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, alighted at Quba upon Kulthum ibn Hadm, brother of Bani Amr ibn Awf. It is also said he alighted upon Sad ibn Khaythama. He stayed among Bani Amr ibn Awf, and Friday overtook him among Bani Salim ibn Awf, so he prayed the great Friday in the mosque in the valley bottom. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, alighted upon Abu Ayyub, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, ordered the building of his mosque in that year."
"9923","Asim ibn Adi, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, arrived at Madina on Monday, after twelve nights of Rabi al-Awwal had passed, and he remained in Madina ten years."
"9924","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, arrived at Madina and his mount knelt between the house of Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn Ali and the house of Al-Hasan ibn Zayd. People came and said, O Messenger of Allah, the dwelling. His mount rose with him, and he said: > **""Leave her, for she is commanded.""** Then she went with him until she reached the pulpit site, knelt and settled. Nearby people had a trellis they sprinkled and maintained and cooled in, until the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, dismounted, sought shade and alighted there. Abu Ayyub came and said, O Messenger of Allah, my house is nearest, so transfer your baggage. He said: > **""Yes.""** He took his baggage home. Another man came and said, O Messenger of Allah, alight with me. He said: > **""A man is with his baggage wherever it is.""** The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, remained in the trellis twelve nights until the mosque was built."
"9925","Ibn Ishaq, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, alighted upon Hubayb, also said Khubayb ibn Yasaf, brother of Al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj, at al-Sunh. It is also said he alighted upon Kharija ibn Zayd ibn Abi Zuhayr, brother of Bani al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj. This is an athar report."
"9926","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: Abdullah ibn Jahsh, may Allah be pleased with him, was the last remaining of the migrants, and his sight had weakened. When he resolved to migrate, his wife, daughter of Harb ibn Umayya, disliked it and advised him to migrate elsewhere. So he migrated concealed from Quraysh with his family and wealth until he reached the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, at Madina. Abu Sufyan ibn Harb seized and sold his house at Mecca. Afterwards Abu Jahl ibn Hisham, Utba ibn Rabia, Shayba ibn Rabia, Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, and Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza passed by it, wherein were tanned skins. Utba's eyes shed tears and he recited verse: Every house, however long its safety, one day misfortune and ruin shall overtake it. Abu Jahl turned to Al-Abbas and said, This is what you brought upon us. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, entered Mecca on the conquest, Abu Ahmad stood claiming his house, so the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, ordered Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, who went to Abu Ahmad and restrained him, so he fell silent. Ibn Abbas said, Abu Ahmad used to say, while the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, reclined upon his hand on the conquest day: How excellent Mecca as a valley, wherein I walk without guide, wherein my visitors abound, wherein my stakes are fixed."
"9927","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: Our arrival to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was five years after Hijra. We set out attached to Quraysh in the year of the Confederates, I with my brother Al-Fadl and our slave Abu Rafi, until we reached al-Arj, where our mount strayed. We took the route by al-Jathjatha until we emerged upon Bani Amr ibn Awf and entered Madina. We found the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, at the Trench. I was then eight years old, and my brother thirteen."
"9928","Suhayb, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: When the polytheists surrounded in search of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, approaching and retreating from the cave, he said, O Suhayb, but no Suhayb for me. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, intended to depart, he twice or thrice sent Abu Bakr to Suhayb, who found him praying. Abu Bakr said to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, I found him praying and disliked interrupting his prayer. He said: > **""You did right.""** They departed that night. In the morning Suhayb went to Umm Ruman, wife of Abu Bakr, who said, Do I see you here while your two brothers departed and we placed some of their provisions for you. So I went to my wife Umm Amr, took my sword, quiver, and bow, until I reached the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, at Madina, finding him and Abu Bakr sitting. When Abu Bakr saw me he stood, congratulated me with the verse revealed concerning me, took my hand. I partly blamed him and he apologized. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, made me profitable, saying: > **""Profitable was the sale.""**"
"9929","Hudhayfa, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, gave me choice between migration and support. So I chose migration."
"9930","Ziyad ibn Jahwar, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: A letter from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, reached me, containing: > **""In the Name of Allah, the Merciful, the Compassionate. From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, to Ziyad ibn Jahwar. Be safe, peace be upon you. I praise to you Allah besides whom there is no god. To proceed, I remind you of Allah and the Last Day. To proceed, every religion people profess shall surely be laid aside except Islam, so know that.""**"
"9931","Jubayr ibn Mutim, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: Al-Mutim ibn Adi said, You have done to Muhammad what you have done, so be the most restrained of people from him. Abu Jahl said, Rather be the harshest as you were. Al-Harith ibn Amir ibn Nawfal said, By Allah, the affair of Muhammad, peace and blessings be upon him, shall ever remain manifest, whether what he called you to openly or secretly. Abu Yusuf said, Al-Harith was killed on Badr day as a disbeliever."
"9932","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""I was aided by the Saba wind, and Ad were destroyed by the Dabur wind.""**"
"9933","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: > **""I was aided by the Saba wind, and Ad were destroyed by the Dabur wind.""**"
"9934","Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""I was given five never given to any prophet before me: I was sent to the red and the black, whereas each prophet was only sent to his people; I was aided by terror for a month's journey; booty was made lawful for me, never made lawful for anyone before me.""** He mentioned the rest of the hadith, whose like and other narrations appear in the signs of prophethood."
"9935","Muawiya ibn Hayda al-Qushayri, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and when I was brought to him he said: > **""Indeed I have asked Allah to suffice me without a year that destroys you, and without terror He places in your hearts.""** Then he joined both hands and said, I have sworn thus and thus never to believe in you nor follow you. Then drought kept afflicting me, and terror kept settling in my heart, until I stood before you."
"9936","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: The Saba wind came to the Shamal wind on the night of the Confederates and said, Proceed so we may aid the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. The Shamal said, The free woman does not travel by night. So the wind by which the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was aided was Saba."
"9937","Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would not raid in the sacred month unless raided or fought. When that occurred, he waited until it expired."
"9938","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, arrived at Madina, Juhayna came and said, You have alighted among us, so make covenant for us so we may come to you in security. He made covenant, and they accepted Islam. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent us in Rajab, less than a hundred, ordering us to raid a clan of Bani Kinana beside Juhayna. We raided them, but they were many, so we sought refuge with Juhayna, who protected us and said, Why do you fight in the sacred month. We said, We only fight those who expelled us from the sacred land in the sacred month. Some said among themselves, What do you think. Some said, Let us go to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and inform him. Others said, Rather let us stay here. I with some others said, Rather let us go to Quraysh caravan and intercept it. We went to the caravan, for booty then was whoever takes is his. Our companions went to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and informed him. He stood angry, red-faced, and said: > **""You went from me together and came back divided. Those before you were destroyed only by division. I shall surely send over you a man not the best among you but the most patient in hunger and thirst.""** So he sent over us Abdullah ibn Jahsh, may Allah be pleased with him, the first commander appointed in Islam."
"9939","Zirr, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated: The first banner raised in Islam was the banner of Abdullah ibn Jahsh, and the first wealth of which a fifth was taken in Islam was the wealth of Abdullah ibn Jahsh, may Allah be pleased with him. This is an athar report."
"9940","Jubayr ibn Mutim, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: Abu Jahl said when he arrived at Mecca returning from Hamza, O Quraysh, Muhammad has settled at Yathrib and sent scouts, seeking to seize something from you. Beware passing his path or approaching him, for he is like a ravenous lion, enraged against you. You expelled him as ticks are expelled from camel feet. By Allah, he has vehemence, never did I see him or any companion except I saw devils with them. You know the enmity of the two sons of Qayla, an enemy aided by an enemy. Mutim ibn Adi said, O Abu al-Hakam, by Allah I never saw one truer in tongue nor truer in promise than your brother whom you expelled. Since you did what you did, be the most restrained from him. Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith said, Be the harshest as you were, for if the two sons of Qayla prevail they will observe neither kinship nor pact toward you. If you obey me, convey to them news of Kinana or expel Muhammad from among them so he becomes solitary and driven. As for the two sons of Qayla, by Allah they and your few people in humiliation are equal, and I shall suffice you their edge, and he recited: I shall grant a harsh side from me, despite nearness or distance. Men of Khazraj are people of disgrace when jest follows earnest. This reached the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: > **""By Him in whose Hand is my soul, I shall surely kill them, crucify them, and guide them though hateful. Indeed I am a mercy, Allah sent me, and He shall not take me until Allah manifests His religion.""**"
"9941","Amr ibn Awf al-Muzani, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: We raided with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, the first raid he raided, Abwa. When we were at al-Rawha, he alighted at Irq al-Zubya, prayed, then said: > **""Do you know what this mountain is called.""** They said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: > **""This is Khimt, among the mountains of Paradise. O Allah, bless it and bless its people.""** And he said of al-Rawha: > **""This is Sajasij, a valley among Paradise valleys. Seventy prophets prayed in this mosque before me. Musa passed here with two Qatwani cloaks upon a grey she-camel among seventy thousand of the Children of Israel pilgrims to the Ancient House. The Hour shall not stand until Isa son of Maryam, servant and Messenger of Allah, passes here as pilgrim or performer of lesser pilgrimage, or Allah combines that for him.""**"
"9942","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: On Badr day every three were upon a camel. Ali ibn Abi Talib and Abu Lubaba, may Allah be pleased with them, were the two companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. When the turn of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came, they said, We shall walk for you. He said: > **""You are not stronger than me, nor am I less in need of reward than you.""**"
"9943","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: With the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, on Badr day were a hundred water-carrying camels, and two horses, one ridden by Al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad, the other alternated by Musab ibn Umayr and Sahl ibn Hunayf, may Allah be pleased with them. His companions took turns upon the camels on the road. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and Mirthad ibn Abi Mirthad al-Ghanawi, ally of Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with them, took turns upon one camel. This is an athar report."
"9944","Sad, meaning Ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, looked at Umayr ibn Abi Waqqas when going out to Badr and deemed him small, then permitted him. Sad said, It is said his sword betrayed him. Abdullah, meaning Ibn Jafar al-Makhrami, said, He was killed on Badr day. This is an athar report."
"9945","Rifaa ibn Rafi ibn Malik ibn al-Ajlan al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: We came on Badr day and missed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. Groups called to one another, Is the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, among you. They halted until the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came with Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him. We said, O Messenger of Allah, we missed you. He said: > **""Indeed Abu al-Hasan felt colic in his belly, so I stayed behind for him.""**"
"9946","Atika bint Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated: I saw a rider who took a rock from Abu Qubays and threw it toward the Corner, so the rock clung, and no house among Quraysh houses remained except a fragment entered it, other than houses of Bani Zuhra. Al-Abbas said, This is a dream, conceal it and do not mention it. Al-Abbas went out and met Al-Walid ibn Utba ibn Rabia, mentioned it to him, Al-Walid mentioned it to his father, and the talk spread. Al-Abbas said, I went to circumambulate the Kaba while Abu Jahl among Quraysh discussed Atika's dream. When Abu Jahl saw me he said, O Abu al-Fadl, when you finish your circumambulation come to us. When I finished I came and sat with them. Abu Jahl said, O Bani Abd al-Muttalib, were you not content that your men prophesy until your women prophesy. Atika claimed in this dream that he said, March forth in three days, so we shall wait these three. If what she says is true, it shall be, and if three pass with nothing, we shall write against you that you are the most lying household among Arabs. Al-Abbas said, By Allah I had nothing to him except denial and rejection that she saw anything. Al-Abbas said, In the evening a woman from the daughters of Abd al-Muttalib came to me and said, Are you content that this wicked one assails your men then your women while you listen with no rebuke. By Allah had Hamza been present he would not have said what he said. I said, By Allah he did, and I had no rebuke, and by Allah I shall confront him, and if he repeats I shall suffice you. Al-Abbas said, I came early on the third day after Atika's dream, angry, hoping to catch something, and by Allah as I walked toward him, a sharp man, sharp-faced, sharp-tongued, sharp-sighted, he went toward the mosque leaning. I thought, What is with him, may Allah curse him. Has he eaten this and fears me to revile him. Then he had heard what I had not heard, the voice of Damdam ibn Zura ibn Amr al-Ghifari shouting in Mecca's valley, having hamstrung his camel, reversed his saddle, torn his shirt, saying, O Quraysh, Muhammad has gone out with his companions, I do not think you will reach the caravan, help, help. Al-Abbas said, What came of the affair distracted me from him and him from me."
"9947","Urwa, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated: Atika bint Abd al-Muttalib, paternal aunt of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, lived with her brother Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib. She saw a dream shortly before Badr and was frightened, so she sent that night to her brother Al-Abbas upon waking, saying, I have seen a dream and feared destruction for your people. He said, What did you see. She said, I shall not narrate until you covenant not to mention it, for if they hear they will harm us and make us hear what we dislike. Al-Abbas covenanted. She said, I saw a rider approaching upon his mount from upper Mecca shouting loudly, O treacherous folk, O dissolute folk, go forth in two or three nights. Then he entered the mosque upon his mount and shouted three shouts in the mosque, while men, women, and children inclined toward him, and people were intensely frightened. Then I saw him as if upon the Kaba's back upon his mount, shouting three shouts, O treacherous folk, O dissolute folk, go forth in two or three nights, until all Mecca between the two mountains heard. Then he went to a huge rock, uprooted it and hurled it upon Mecca's people. The rock came roaring until at the mountain base it split, and I know no house or dwelling in Mecca except a piece entered it. I feared evil would befall your people. Al-Abbas was frightened, left her, met that night Al-Walid ibn Utba ibn Rabia, intimate friend, narrated Atika's dream and ordered silence. Al-Walid told his father, Utba told his brother Shayba, and it rose until it reached Abu Jahl and spread widely. In the morning Al-Abbas circumambulated and found Abu Jahl, Utba, Shayba, Umayya ibn Khalaf, Zamaa ibn al-Aswad, and Abu al-Bakhtari discussing. Seeing Al-Abbas circumambulating, Abu Jahl called, O Abu al-Fadl, when you finish come to us. When he finished and sat, Abu Jahl said, O Abu al-Fadl, what dream did Atika see. He said, She saw nothing. He said, Nay, were you not content, O Bani Hashim, with lies of men until you bring lies of women. We and you were like two racehorses vying for glory since long. When you caught up, you said, From us is a prophet. Nothing remains but you say, From us is a prophetess. I know no household more lying in man or woman than you. They harmed him severely that day. Abu Jahl said, Atika claimed the rider said, Go forth in two or three nights, so when these three pass, Quraysh's lie about you will be clear, and we shall write a document and hang it on the Kaba that you are the most lying house among Arabs in man and woman. Were you not content, O Bani Qusay, that you took veiling, council, watering, and banner until you come claiming a prophet among you. They harmed him severely. Al-Abbas said, Gently, O yellow one, will you desist, for lying is in you and your household. Those present said, O Abu al-Fadl, you were never ignorant nor senile. Al-Abbas suffered severe harm from Atika for spreading her report. On the evening of the third night after the dream, the caravan came which Abu Sufyan had sent, Damdam ibn Amr al-Ghifari, saying, O treacherous folk, march forth, for Muhammad and his companions have gone to intercept Abu Sufyan, so guard your caravan. Quraysh were intensely frightened, fearing Atika's dream, and marched upon every hard and gentle mount."
"9948","Musab ibn Abdillah and others of Quraysh reported that Atika bint Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with her, said concerning the truth of her dream and Quraysh denying her, when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, struck them at Badr, in verse: Was not the dream true, and did there not come to you its interpretation with fugitives fleeing. He saw and brought you the certainty he saw with his eyes, what sharp swords effect. You said, though I lied not, You have lied, but only a liar denies me for truth. None fled but fearing death among them, Hakim, when paths straitened upon him. Did the caller flee their hearts resolve, mere desires and distant dreams. They passed your blood with whetted swords face to face, as southern clouds pour rain. How did the cousins see Muhammad on encounter day, with war's vicissitudes therein. Did he not overwhelm them with blows bewildering the coward, stars appearing by day. Shall Muhammad not come on encounter day when war's aid bites the shoulders. As his swords emerged from inner fervor, tempests rosy after fever. I swear if you return, they shall uproot you askew, flanked by squadrons. As sunlight gleams with lightning, its sides of piercing radiant light. This is an athar of poetry."
"9949","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: Utba ibn Rabia was friend to Sad ibn Muadh in pre-Islamic times. When Utba came to Madina he lodged with Sad, and when Sad came to Mecca he lodged with Utba. Utba called him my Yathribi brother. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, arrived at Madina, Sad came to Mecca as usual and lodged with Utba. He said, I wish to circumambulate the House. Utba said, Wait till Quraysh notables disperse from the mosque around the House. He waited a little, then he said, Go with me. When they reached the House, Abu Jahl met Sad and said, O Sad, you sheltered Muhammad then circumambulate safe. Sad said, If you prevent me, I shall surely cut off your trade to such place, mentioning a place. Their voices rose. Utba said to Sad, Do you raise your voice against Abu al-Hakam. Sad said, And you say that. I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saying: > **""Indeed he will kill you.""** He loosened his hand from his hand and said, Muhammad does not lie. Sad circumambulated then left. Utba came to his wife and said, Did you hear what my Yathribi brother said. She said, What did he say. He said, He claimed Muhammad will kill me, and Muhammad does not lie. Only a little passed until Badr occurred, when Abu Jahl toured the people. He mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"9950","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said while we were at Madina: > **""I have been informed that Abu Sufyan's caravan is approaching, shall we go out before this caravan, perhaps Allah will grant it to us as booty.""** We said, Yes. So he went out and we went with him. After a day or two, he said to us: > **""What do you think about the enemy, for they have been informed of your going out.""** We said, No, by Allah, we have no strength to fight the enemy, but we intended the caravan. Then he said: > **""What do you think about the enemy.""** We said the like. Al-Miqdad ibn Amr said, Then we shall not say to you, O Messenger of Allah, as Musa's people said to Musa, Go you and your Lord and fight, indeed we sit here. We Ansar wished we had said as Al-Miqdad said, dearer to us than great wealth. Then Allah revealed to His Messenger, peace and blessings be upon him: As your Lord brought you out of your house with truth though a party of believers disliked, disputing with you about truth after it became clear as if driven to death while looking. Then Allah revealed: I am with you, so make firm those who believe, I shall cast terror into hearts of disbelievers, so strike above necks and strike every fingertip. And He said: When Allah promised you one of the two parties would be yours, and you wished the unarmed one would be yours. Thorn means the troops, unarmed means the caravan. When Allah promised one of the two, either the troops or the caravan, our souls were pleased. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent to scout ahead of the enemy. He said, I saw a black mass but know not. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""It is they, come let us count.""** We were three hundred and thirteen men. We informed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, of our number, which pleased him, and he said: > **""The number of Talut's companions.""** Then we met the enemy and lined up. A rash impulse darted before the rank. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, looked and said: > **""With me, with me.""** Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""O Allah, I beseech Your promise.""** Ibn Rawaha said, O Messenger of Allah, I wish to counsel you, though the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, is too great to be counseled, and Allah too great to be beseeched for His promise. He said: > **""O son of Rawaha, I shall surely beseech Allah His promise, for Allah breaks not the tryst.""** He took a handful of dust and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, threw it in the enemy faces, so they were defeated. Allah revealed: You threw not when you threw but Allah threw. We killed and captured. Umar ibn al-Khattab said, O Messenger of Allah, I see no captives for you, for we are callers seeking to reconcile. We Ansar said, Only envy for us carries Umar to what he says. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, slept then awoke and said: > **""Call Umar for me.""** He was called. He said: > **""Indeed Allah has revealed to me: It is not for a prophet to have captives until he has thoroughly subdued in the land, you desire worldly goods while Allah desires the Hereafter, and Allah is Mighty, Wise.""**"
"9951","Muadh ibn Rifaa al-Ansari reported from his father, may Allah be pleased with them: I and my brother Khallad set out with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, to Badr upon our lean camel. When we reached the mail station behind al-Rawha, our camel knelt. I said, O Allah, upon us, if You bring us near Madina we shall slaughter it. While thus, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, passed and said: > **""What is with you two.""** We told him it had collapsed upon us. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, dismounted, performed ablution, spat in his ablution water, ordered us to open the camel's mouth, poured inside the young camel from his ablution, then poured upon its head, neck, withers, hump, rump, and tail, then said: > **""O Allah, carry Rafi and Khallad.""** The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, proceeded. We mounted and journeyed, overtaking the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, at midday, our camel foremost. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saw us he laughed. We proceeded till Badr, and when near Badr it knelt again. We said, Praise be to Allah, so we slaughtered it and gave its meat in charity."
"9952","Utba ibn Abd al-Sulami, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said to his companions: > **""Rise and fight.""** They said, Yes, O Messenger of Allah, and we shall not say as the Children of Israel said to Musa, Go you and your Lord and fight, indeed we sit here. But proceed you and your Lord, O Muhammad, and fight, and we fight with you."
"9953","Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: When we arrived at Madina we ate its fruits and found them disagreeable, suffering fever. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, kept inquiring about Badr. When it reached us that the polytheists had advanced, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, marched to Badr, and Badr is a well. The polytheists preceded us to it. We found therein two of their men, a Qurayshi and a freedman of Uqba ibn Abi Muayt. The Qurayshi slipped away, but Uqba's freedman we seized. We kept saying, How many are the people. He said, By Allah they are many in number, severe in might. Whenever he said that, Muslims beat him until they brought him to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said to him: > **""How many are the people.""** He said, By Allah many in number, severe in might. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, strove to make him inform but he refused. Then the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, asked him: > **""How many camels do they slaughter.""** He said, Ten each day. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""The people are a thousand, each camel for a hundred and odd.""** Then light rain afflicted us, so we went under trees and shields seeking shade from rain. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, spent the night supplicating his Lord, saying: > **""O Allah, if You destroy this band, You shall not be worshipped.""** When dawn broke he called: > **""Prayer, O servants of Allah.""** People came from under trees and shields. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, prayed with us and urged fighting, then said: > **""Indeed Quraysh's host is beneath this red slope of the mountain.""** When the enemy neared and we faced them, a man among them upon a red camel moved among the people. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""O Ali, call Hamza for me, who is nearest to the polytheists, who is the owner of the red camel and what does he say to them.""** Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""If there is any among the people enjoining good, perhaps it is the owner of the red camel.""** Hamza came and said, He is Utba ibn Rabia, forbidding fighting and saying to them, O people, I see a people courting death, you shall not reach them while good is in you. O people, bind it today upon my head and say, Utba ibn Rabia has cowardiced, though you know I am not your most cowardly. Abu Jahl heard and said, Do you say that. By Allah, had another said it I would have bitten him. Your chest has filled with fear. Utba said, At me you mean, O yellow one, today you shall know which is coward. So Utba, his brother Shayba, and his son Al-Walid advanced zealously, saying, Who will duel. Six Ansar youths went out. Utba said, We want not these, but let our cousins from Bani Abd al-Muttalib duel us. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: > **""Stand, O Ali, stand, O Hamza, stand, O Ubayda ibn al-Harith ibn al-Muttalib.""** Allah killed Shayba and Utba sons of Rabia and Al-Walid ibn Utba. Ubayda was wounded. We killed seventy among them and captured seventy. A short Ansari man brought Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib captive. Al-Abbas said, O Messenger of Allah, by Allah this one did not capture me, one hairless, most handsome-faced upon a piebald horse captured me, whom I see not among the people. The Ansari said, I captured him, O Messenger of Allah. He said: > **""Be silent, for Allah has aided you with a noble angel.""** Ali, peace be upon him, said, We captured, among Bani al-Muttalib, Al-Abbas, Aqil, and Nawfal ibn al-Harith."
"9954","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated: When the Muslims alighted at Badr and the polytheists advanced, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, looked at Utba ibn Rabia upon a red camel and said: > **""If there is good with anyone among the people, it is with the owner of the red camel. If they obey him they shall be guided.""** He was saying, O people, obey me concerning these people. If you do, rancor shall ever remain in your hearts, every man looking at his brother's killer and father's killer. So place its side upon my head and return. Abu Jahl said, By Allah his sorcery has swollen when he saw Muhammad and his companions. Muhammad and his companions are but a camel's morsel, if only we meet. Utba said, You shall know who is the coward corrupting his people. By Allah I see a people who shall strike you severely. Do you not see as if their heads are vipers and their faces swords. Then he called his brother and son, walked between them, and called for single combat."
"9955","Narrated by Ali ibn Abi Talib (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr he was drawing water at a well when three violent winds blew in succession, the second fiercer than what he had ever seen except the first. The first was Mikail ('alayhi al-salam) with a thousand angels on the right of the Prophet, the second was Israfil ('alayhi al-salam) with a thousand angels on the left of the Prophet, and the third was Jibril ('alayhi al-salam) with a thousand angels. Abu Bakr (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) stood on the right of the Prophet ﷺ and Ali on his left. When Allah routed the disbelievers, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mounted Ali on his horse; when he settled, he slipped toward its neck, supplicated to Allah, and Allah steadied him, and he thrust with his spear until blood reached his armpit."
"9956","Narrated by Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' al-Ansari (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that when Iblis saw what the angels were doing to the polytheists at Badr, he feared being killed. Al-Harith ibn Hisham clung to him, mistaking him for Suraqah ibn Malik, so he struck al-Harith on the chest, threw him off, fled, cast himself toward the sea, raised his hands asking his Lord for respite, fearing death. Then Abu Jahl came forward urging the people not to let the desertion of Suraqah rout them, claiming he had an appointment with Muhammad, nor let the killing of Utbah and Shaybah sons of Rabi'ah dismay them since they had hastened, swearing by al-Lat and al-Uzza they would not return until they bound the Muslims in ropes, ordering them to seize men alive rather than kill, to reproach them for leaving their people and turning from al-Lat and al-Uzza, then recited boastful verses that war the obstinate finds him a seasoned camel of two years yet youthful, for such as this his mother bore him."
"9957","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that on the day of Badr a barren wind that brings no rain seized the polytheists."
"9958","Narrated by Abu Hurayrah (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that Allah revealed to His Prophet ﷺ in Makkah that the host will be routed and turn their backs. Umar ibn al-Khattab (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) asked which host that was, before Badr. On the day of Badr when Quraysh were routed, he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pursuing their traces with sword unsheathed, reciting that verse, and it was fulfilled that day. Allah revealed concerning them verses about seizing their affluent with punishment, about those who exchanged Allah's blessing for disbelief, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ threw dust at them which spread to cover them and filled their eyes and mouths, so that a man would come wiping his eyes and mouth. Then Allah revealed that you did not throw when you threw but Allah threw, and revealed concerning Iblis that when the two hosts met he turned on his heels declaring disavowal, saying he sees what they see not and fears Allah, and Allah is severe in punishment. Utbah ibn Rabi'ah and some polytheists said that their religion had deluded these Muslims, so Allah revealed the verse about hypocrites and diseased hearts saying their religion deluded them."
"9959","Narrated by Umar ibn al-Khattab (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that when the verse that the host will be routed and turn their backs was revealed, he wondered which host was meant. On the day of battle he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with sword unsheathed in hand, reciting that verse. > **[""The host will be defeated and they will turn their backs.""]**"
"9960","Narrated by Jabir (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that Abu Jahl ibn Hisham claimed Muhammad asserts that if they disobey him there will be slaughter among them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirmed it and warned that Abu Jahl himself would be among that slaughter. When he saw him slain on the day of Badr, he praised Allah for fulfilling His promise to him. He then sent Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Asad toward Abu Jahl. Ibn Mas'ud was asked if he had killed him; he first said Allah killed him, then affirmed he had, that he had killed him and stripped his armor, and Abu Salamah questioned how he could have overpowered him. Ibn Mas'ud described as proof a black mole on the inside of his right thigh, which Abu Salamah recognized, affirming he had stripped him and no other Qurashi. > **[""Yes, I say that, and you are among that slaughter.""]**"
"9961","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr he reached Abu Jahl whose leg had been struck while he lay fallen, fending people off with his sword. Ibn Mas'ud praised Allah who had disgraced Allah's enemy. Abu Jahl retorted that he was but a man killed by his own people. Ibn Mas'ud struck at him with his own short sword, hit his hand so his sword fell, seized it, struck him until he killed him. He then hastened to the Prophet ﷺ as if gliding above the ground and informed him. The Prophet ﷺ made him swear thrice by Allah besides whom none is worthy of worship, and he swore. Then the Prophet ﷺ walked with him until he stood over the body and praised Allah who disgraced Allah's enemy, declaring him the Pharaoh of this Ummah. > **[""By Allah besides whom none is worthy of worship?""]**"
"9962","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): In another wording of the same encounter, the Prophet ﷺ declared of Abu Jahl that he is the Pharaoh of his Ummah. > **[""This is the Pharaoh of my Ummah.""]**"
"9963","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): In another wording he added that the Prophet ﷺ granted him the arms and belongings of Abu Jahl as extra spoils."
"9964","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr he was brought to Abu Jahl while he lay incapacitated, seized his sword, struck his head with it, and Abu Jahl mocked him as their little shepherd of Makkah. He struck him with his own sword until he grew cold. He then told the Prophet ﷺ that he had killed Abu Jahl, but Uqayl, then a captive with the Prophet ﷺ, accused him of lying. Ibn Mas'ud called him the lying sinful enemy of Allah, swore by Allah he had killed him, and when asked for the mark described a ring-like circle on his thigh resembling a ringed partridge mark, which Uqayl confirmed as true."
"9965","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that he found Abu Jahl fallen on the day of Badr and said that Allah had disgraced Allah's enemy. Abu Jahl asked in what way he was disgraced beyond a man killed by his people. Ibn Mas'ud struck him with his own sword to little effect, then struck his hand where he held a fine sword so it fell, seized it, uncovered his head and struck his neck. He informed the Prophet ﷺ, who asked him to swear by Allah besides whom none is worthy of worship, then ordered him to go and verify. He ran like a bird, returned laughing like a bird, confirmed it, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to go and show him. When the Prophet ﷺ stood over him he declared him the Pharaoh of this Ummah. > **[""By Allah besides whom none is worthy of worship?""]**"
"9966","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): In the narration of the same standing over Abu Jahl, the Prophet ﷺ magnified Allah and praised Allah who kept His promise and aided His servant. > **[""All praise belongs to Allah who kept His promise and aided His servant.""]**"
"9967","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): In yet another wording there is the addition that He honored His religion. > **[""And honored His religion.""]**"
"9968","Narrated by Ali (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him on the day of Badr to fill in and blind their wells."
"9969","Narrated by Anas (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that when the Prophet ﷺ arrived at Badr he pointed with his hand naming the exact spot where each leader would fall. By Allah, not one of them fell beyond the spot he named. > **[""This is the place where so-and-so will fall.""]**"
"9970","Narrated by Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl asking who had struck his father. He replied that it was the one who cut his leg, so the Prophet ﷺ awarded his spoils to Muadh ibn Amr ibn al-Jamuh (Radiya Allahu 'anhu). > **[""Who struck your father?""]**"
"9971","Narrated by Ibn Ishaq in naming Badr participants: He recorded among the Ansar of Banu Khazraj who witnessed Badr was Muadh ibn Amr ibn al-Jamuh, who killed Abu Jahl while Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl cut off his hand, and he lived on until the era of Uthman (Radiya Allahu 'anhu). The full roll of Badr participants will come later."
"9972","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that when Abu Jahl was dragged to the pit, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that had Abu Talib been alive he would have known their swords clung to fingertips. In the extended wording Abu Talib's own verses are cited, swearing by the House that if what he foresaw came to pass their swords would cling to fingertips and men in armor would rise against the foe like water-camels advancing on a firm road, with a linguistic note that narrators say both halahil and jalajil for the last word. > **[""Had Abu Talib been alive he would have known our swords clung to fingertips.""]**"
"9973","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Umar (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that while traveling by Badr a man emerged from a pit with a chain around his neck calling O Abdullah give me water thrice, and he knew not whether he knew his name or called in the Arab manner O servant of Allah. Then another man emerged from the pit with a whip warning him not to give the disbeliever water, struck him, and drove him back. He hastened to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him. The Prophet ﷺ asked if he had indeed seen him, and when he affirmed, told him it was Allah's enemy Abu Jahl and that is his punishment until the Day of Resurrection. > **[""Did you indeed see him?""]**"
"9974","Narrated by Al-Sha'bi: He reported that a man called Hawdhah came to Muawiyah (Radiya Allahu 'anhu), who asked if he had witnessed Badr. He replied yes, Commander of the Faithful, though it counts against him not for him. Asked his age then, he said he was a sturdy youth firm as smooth rock. He still sees them arrayed in a long rank, swords gleaming like sunbeams through clouds. Before he recovered, their vanguard overwhelmed his side, foremost Ali ibn Abi Talib (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) like a mighty lion feeding strangers, declaring they would never again eat dates in the belly of Makkah, followed by Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) with a white feather on his chest as his badge, like a camel smashing a structure. He slipped away from those two and they turned upon Hanzalah, brother of Muawiyah. Muawiyah invoked Allah's pleasure on him, warned against ingratitude for a lapse, and asked when he found relief. He swore he found no rest until he saw the hills of Arbad, then wondered what became of Hanzalah. Muawiyah replied his mention of Hanzalah is like a rich man mentioning his poor brother, scarcely remembering except drowsy or feigning sleep."
"9975","Narrated by Al-Harith al-Taymi: He reported that Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) on the day of Badr wore an ostrich feather as his badge. A polytheist asked who the man marked with an ostrich feather was, was told it was Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, and said that is the one who did such deeds to us."
"9976","Narrated by Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that Umayyah ibn Khalaf asked him who was the man marked with an ostrich feather on his chest on the day of Badr. He replied it was the uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib. Umayyah said that is the one who did such deeds to us."
"9977","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that on setting out to Badr the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised Badr and that Allah would grant their force as booty, and that whoever kills an enemy or takes a captive shall have such-and-such from their spoils if Allah wills. When the hosts faced, Allah cast terror into the hearts of the polytheists, and when they fought Allah routed them. The swift pursued them, killing seventy and capturing seventy. > **[""Allah has promised me Badr and will grant me their force as booty, and whoever kills a man shall have such-and-such of their spoils if Allah wills, and whoever captures a captive shall have such-and-such of their spoils if Allah wills.""]**"
"9978","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that they never heard one demanding his right more earnestly than Muhammad ﷺ on the day of Badr, imploring Allah by what He had promised, warning that if this band perishes He will not be worshipped. Then he turned with face like the moon, declaring he can already see the spots where the enemy will fall by evening. > **[""O Allah, I beseech You for what You promised me; if this band perishes, You will not be worshipped.""]**"
"9979","Narrated by Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr men gathered around Umayyah ibn Khalaf, so they advanced to him. He saw a piece of his armor broken open beneath his armpit and thrust him with the sword. He himself was shot that day by an arrow which burst his eye, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spat in it and supplicated for him, and thereafter nothing troubled him in it."
"9980","Narrated by Ali (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr the Prophet ﷺ told him and Abu Bakr that with one of them is Jibril, with the other is Mikail, and Israfil, a mighty angel, witnesses the fighting or stands in the ranks. > **[""With one of you is Jibril and with the other is Mikail, and Israfil, a mighty angel, witnesses the fighting.""]**"
"9981","Narrated by Ali (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): In a variant with sound chain he reported that on the day of Badr the Prophet ﷺ told him and Abu Bakr that with one is Jibril and with the other is Mikail, and Israfil, a mighty angel, witnesses the fighting or stands in the ranks. > **[""With one of you is Jibril and with the other is Mikail, and Israfil, a mighty angel, witnesses the fighting.""]**"
"9982","Narrated by Ali ibn Abi Talib (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr he and Hamzah aided Ubaydah ibn al-Harith against al-Walid ibn Utbah, and as he recalls, the Prophet ﷺ did not reproach them for it."
"9983","Narrated by Amir al-Sha'bi: He reported that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) was asked when he received the invocation, and replied on the day of Badr when he shot arrows before the Prophet ﷺ, placing each arrow in the bow's center and praying O Allah shake their feet, fill their hearts with terror, and do such-and-such to them, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ prayed for Allah to answer Sa'd. > **[""O Allah, answer Sa'd.""]**"
"9984","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that Sa'd (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) fought alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Badr with the fighting of both horseman and foot-soldier."
"9985","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that the badge of the angels on the day of Badr was white turbans with ends let down behind their backs, and on the day of Hunayn red turbans. They fought only on the day of Badr; on other days they were present only as numbers and reinforcement, not striking."
"9986","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that the angels fought alongside the Prophet ﷺ only on the day of Badr; besides that they were only reinforcement. With the Prophet ﷺ there were only two horses, one belonging to al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad and the other to Abu Marthad al-Ghanawi (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma)."
"9987","Narrated by Al-Bahi: He reported that on the day of Badr there were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ two horsemen, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) on a horse on the right flank and al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) on a horse on the left flank."
"9988","Narrated by Abu al-Malih from his father: He reported that on the day of Badr the angels descended in the likeness of al-Zubayr wearing yellow turbans."
"9989","Narrated by Abu Hazim al-Ansari (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr the Prophet ﷺ was in the shade while his Companions fought in the sun. Jibril came to him noting he sits in shade while Muslims fight in the sun, so he rose and moved into the sun."
"9990","Narrated by Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that Abu Barzah al-Harithi came on the day of Badr carrying three heads to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him he congratulated his right hand. Abu Barzah said he himself had killed two, but as for the third he saw a handsome fair man strike off his head. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him it was such-and-such an angel among the angels. > **[""Your right hand has prospered.""]**"
"9991","Narrated by Abu Dawud al-Mazini (Radiya Allahu 'anhu), a Badr veteran: He reported that he pursued a polytheist to strike him, but the man's head fell before his sword could reach him, so he knew another besides himself had killed him."
"9992","Narrated by Jabir (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that they were praying with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Badr campaign when he smiled during prayer. When he finished they asked why he smiled. He said Mikail passed by him with traces of dust on his wing, returning from pursuing the enemy, laughed to him, so he smiled back. > **[""Mikail passed by me with dust on his wing, returning from pursuing the enemy, and laughed to me, so I smiled to him.""]**"
"9993","Narrated by Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf from his father (Radiya Allahu 'anhum): His father told him that on the day of Badr one of them would merely point his sword toward a polytheist's head and the head would fall from its body before the blade reached him."
"9994","Narrated by Sahl ibn Sa'd (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that Abu Usayd (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) told him that were they both now at Badr with his sight restored, he would show him without doubt or dispute the mountain pass from which the angels emerged upon them."
"9995","Narrated by Urwah: He reported that on the day of Badr Jibril ('alayhi al-salam) descended in the likeness of al-Zubayr (Radiya Allahu 'anhu), wrapping a yellow turban."
"9996","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr the enemy were made few in their eyes, so that he asked his companion beside him whether he saw seventy or a hundred. Then they seized one of them and asked him, and he said they were a thousand."
"9997","Narrated by Hakim ibn Hizam (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that they heard a sound falling from sky to earth like a pebble in a basin, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ threw that pebble, whereupon they were routed."
"9998","Narrated by Hakim ibn Hizam (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took a handful of pebbles, faced them, threw them and said that faces be deformed, whereupon they were routed, and Allah revealed that you did not throw when you threw but Allah threw. > **[""May the faces be deformed.""]**"
"9999","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that the Prophet ﷺ told Ali to hand him a handful of pebbles. He handed them, the Prophet ﷺ threw them at the faces of the enemy, and none remained whose eyes were not filled with gravel. Then the verse was revealed that you did not throw when you threw but Allah threw. > **[""Hand me a handful of pebbles.""]**"
"10000","Narrated by Ali ibn Abi Talib (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that whomever they could capture from Banu Abd al-Muttalib should be taken alive, for they had been brought out under coercion. > **[""Whomever you are able to capture from Banu Abd al-Muttalib, take him alive, for they came out under coercion.""]**"
"10001","Narrated by Al-Bara and others (Radiya Allahu 'anhum): They reported that an Ansari man brought al-Abbas as his captive. Al-Abbas protested to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that this man had not captured him; rather a man of striking appearance had captured him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told the Ansari that Allah had aided him with a noble angel. > **[""Allah has aided you with a noble angel.""]**"
"10002","Narrated by Abu al-Yusr (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr he looked at al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib standing like an idol with eyes flowing. He reproached him for fighting his nephew alongside his enemy despite kinship. Al-Abbas asked what had happened and whether the Prophet had been killed. He replied Allah is mightier and more helping than that. Al-Abbas asked what he wanted, and he said captivity, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden killing him. Al-Abbas said this was not his first kinship, so he captured him and brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"10003","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that he asked his father how Abu al-Yusr, whom he could have held in his palm, had captured him. He replied not to say that, for he had met one who appeared to his eye greater than al-Khandamah mountain."
"10004","Narrated by Jabir ibn Abdullah (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that al-Abbas was captured on the day of Badr and no shirt could be found large enough to fit him."
"10005","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that al-Mujadhdhar ibn Ziyad told Abu al-Bakhtari ibn Hisham that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden killing him. > **[""The Messenger of Allah has forbidden killing you.""]**"
"10006","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that Abu al-Yusr ibn Amr, who is Ka'b ibn Amr of Banu Salamah, captured al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked how he had captured him. He said a man he had never seen before or since, of such-and-such appearance, had aided him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him a noble angel had aided him. He then told al-Abbas to ransom himself and his nephew Aqil ibn Abi Talib and Nawfal ibn al-Harith and his ally Utbah ibn Jahdam of Banu al-Harith ibn Fihr. Al-Abbas claimed he had been Muslim before and was coerced. The Prophet ﷺ replied Allah knows best his affair; if his claim is true Allah will reward him, but his outward case was against them, so he must ransom himself. The Prophet ﷺ had taken twenty ounces of gold found with him. Al-Abbas asked to count it toward his ransom. The Prophet ﷺ refused, saying it was something Allah had granted from him. When he claimed he had no wealth, the Prophet ﷺ asked about the wealth he had left in Makkah with Umm al-Fadl when none but they two were present, appointing so much for al-Fadl, so much for Qutham, so much for Abdullah if he died on the journey. Al-Abbas swore by Him who sent him with truth that none but he and she knew it, and declared he knows he is Allah's Messenger. > **[""A noble angel aided you against him.""]**"
"10007","Narrated by Abu Aziz ibn Umayr, brother of Mus'ab ibn Umayr: He reported that he was among the captives on the day of Badr when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded kindness to prisoners. He was among a group of Ansar who, when they brought morning and evening meals, ate dates themselves and fed him wheat in observance of the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **[""Show kindness to the prisoners.""]**"
"10008","Narrated by Abdullah (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked what should be done with the captives. Abu Bakr (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) advised they are his people and kin, to spare them and grant respite hoping Allah will turn to them. Umar (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) advised they expelled and belied him, to bring them forward and strike their necks. Abdullah ibn Rawahah (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) advised to find a valley full of firewood, enter them into it, then set it ablaze upon them. Al-Abbas accused him of severing kinship. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered without answering. People debated which counsel he would take. He then came out and explained that Allah softens hearts of men till softer than milk and hardens hearts till harder than stone, likened Abu Bakr to Ibrahim who said whoever follows me is of me and whoever disobeys, Allah is Forgiving Merciful, and to Isa who said if You punish them they are Your servants and if You forgive them You are Mighty Wise, and likened Umar to Nuh who prayed to leave no disbeliever dwelling on earth, and to Musa who prayed to harden their hearts till they see painful punishment. He ruled they are needy, so none shall return except with ransom or striking of neck. Abdullah added that he himself pleaded to except Suhayl ibn Bayda since he had heard him mention Islam. The Prophet ﷺ fell silent, and he never feared stones from heaven as that day, until the Prophet ﷺ excepted Suhayl ibn Bayda. Then Allah revealed that were it not for a prior decree from Allah a great punishment would have touched them for what they took, and that it is not for a prophet to have captives until he has thoroughly subdued the land, seeking worldly gain while Allah seeks the Hereafter. > **[""Allah softens hearts of men till softer than milk and hardens hearts till harder than stone; you are needy, so none of them shall return except with ransom or striking of neck, except Suhayl ibn Bayda.""]**"
"10009","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Jahsh (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): In a variant he stood and said the enemies of Allah belied, expelled, and fought the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while he dwells in a valley full of firewood, implying burning."
"10010","Narrated by Abdullah (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): In a variant Abu Bakr (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) pleaded that they are his kin, household, and people, to pardon them so Allah may save them through him from the Fire."
"10011","Narrated by Abu Bakr and Umar (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): In a variant with al-Tabarani, Abu Bakr argued that killing them sends them to Fire while taking ransom may make them a support, while Umar argued to parade them and strike their necks, for they are the chiefs and leaders of disbelief who were not content with expulsion until they were the first Arabs to raid the Muslims."
"10012","Narrated by Anas and al-Hasan: They reported that the Prophet ﷺ consulted the people about the captives on the day of Badr, declaring Allah had enabled them over them, though but yesterday they were their brothers. Umar ibn al-Khattab stood twice urging to strike their necks, and the Prophet ﷺ turned away from him. The Prophet ﷺ repeated his address, then Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (Radiya Allahu 'anhu) stood urging to pardon them and accept ransom. The grief left the Prophet's face, so he pardoned them and accepted ransom, and Allah revealed that were it not for a prior decree from Allah they would have been touched for what they took. > **[""Allah has enabled you over them, and but yesterday they were your brothers.""]**"
"10013","Narrated by Ikrimah from Abu Rafi (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that he was a lad belonging to al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib while Islam had entered them; he, Umm al-Fadl, and al-Abbas had accepted Islam, though al-Abbas feared his people and concealed it. Abu Lahab stayed back from Badr and sent al-As ibn Hisham ibn al-Mughirah in his place, as was their custom to send substitutes. When the news of defeat came Allah abased and disgraced Abu Lahab and they found strength. The report continues without chain in Yaqub's book that among captives was Abu Wada'ah ibn Subayrah al-Sahmi, about whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold his shrewd merchant son in Makkah would soon come for his ransom. Quraysh warned against hastening ransom lest Muhammad and his Companions exploit them. Al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'ah agreed, then slipped away by night, came to Madinah, ransomed his father for four thousand dirhams, while Mikraz ibn Hafs ibn al-Akhyath came to ransom Suhayl ibn Amr, captured by Malik ibn al-Dukhshun of Banu Malik ibn Awf. > **[""He has in Makkah a shrewd merchant son with wealth; you will soon see him come for his father's ransom.""]**"
"10014","Narrated by Abu Rafi (Radiya Allahu 'anhu), freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: He reported that he was a lad of al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, already Muslim with Umm al-Fadl and al-Abbas, who concealed his Islam for fear of his people. Abu Lahab stayed back from Badr and sent al-As ibn Hisham, his debtor, telling him to suffice for the campaign in exchange for his debt. When the news came Allah abased Abu Lahab. Abu Rafi, a weak man, was carving arrow-wood in the Zamzam chamber with Umm al-Fadl beside him when the wicked Abu Lahab dragged his feet and sat by the tent rope, back to back with him. Men announced Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith. Abu Lahab called his nephew over, men gathered, and asked how the people fared. He replied it was nothing but that they offered their shoulders, to be killed and captured at will, and he blamed no one, for he saw fair men on piebald horses whom nothing withstands. Abu Rafi lifted the tent rope declaring by Allah they were angels, whereupon Abu Lahab struck his face, grappled him, threw him to the ground and mounted him. Umm al-Fadl seized a tent pole, struck Abu Lahab splitting his head with a grievous wound, calling him Allah's enemy for oppressing the weak in his master's absence. He rose abased, lived only seven nights till Allah struck him with lentil-like sores and killed him. His sons left him two or three days unburied till he stank. A Qurashi shamed them, they feared the sores as plague, a man accompanied them, they washed him only by pouring water from afar, carried him, cast him by a wall in upper Makkah, and pelted him with stones."
"10015","Narrated by Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that he and al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam captured al-Walid ibn al-Walid on the day of Badr. Hisham ibn al-Walid came to ransom him, so Sa'd gifted his share while al-Zubayr took his due."
"10016","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared he would that day slay a Qurashi man bound. Uqbah ibn Abi Muayt cried at the top of his voice asking why he alone among them should be killed bound. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied for his disbelief in Allah and his forging lies against Allah's Messenger ﷺ. > **[""I shall today slay a man of Quraysh bound.""]**"
"10017","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called out to the Badr captives, fixing each man's ransom at four thousand, and that Uqbah ibn Abi Muayt was killed before ransom. Ali ibn Abi Talib stood to him and killed him bound. Uqbah asked who would care for his children, and the Prophet ﷺ replied the Fire. > **[""The Fire.""]**"
"10018","Narrated by Masruq: He reported that he told the son of Ibn Abi Muayt that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, who was no liar, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered his father's neck to be struck bound, and when he passed him and asked who would care for his children after him, the Prophet ﷺ replied for them is the Fire, and that Allah's Messenger's decision should suffice him. > **[""For them is the Fire.""]**"
"10019","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Badr killed three men bound: al-Nadr ibn al-Harith of Banu Abd al-Dar, Tuaymah ibn Adi of Banu Nawfal, and Uqbah ibn Abi Muayt."
"10020","Narrated by Al-Nu'man ibn Bashir (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fixed the ransom of the polytheist Badr captives at four thousand for each man."
"10021","Narrated by Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that Quraysh first wailed over their slain then regretted, saying not to wail lest it reach Muhammad and his Companions and they gloat. Among captives was Abu Wada'ah ibn Subayrah al-Sahmi, about whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold his shrewd wealthy merchant son in Makkah would soon come for his father's ransom. When Quraysh spoke about ransom, al-Muttalib agreed they spoke true lest severity follow, then slipped away by night, came to Madinah, and ransomed his father for four thousand dirhams. > **[""He has in Makkah a merchant son, shrewd and wealthy; you will soon see him come for his father's ransom.""]**"
"10022","Narrated by Shaqiq from Ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that the eighteen Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ killed on the day of Badr had their spirits placed by Allah in Paradise within green birds roaming freely. While thus, their Lord looked upon them and asked what they desire. They asked whether anything is beyond this. He repeated asking His servants what they desire, until on the fourth they replied to return their spirits to their bodies to be killed again as they were killed."
"10023","Narrated by Aishah (Radiya Allahu 'anha): She reported that on the day of Badr when the Prophet ﷺ passed the group thrown into the pit, Utbah and Abu Jahl and their companions, he stood over them and declared that Allah recompensed the people of a prophet with evil for their vile driving away and vehement belying. They asked how he addresses corpses already putrid. He replied they do not understand his words better than the slain, or rather the slain understand better than them. > **[""May Allah recompense with evil the people of a prophet for their vile driving away and vehement belying.""]**"
"10024","Narrated by Aishah (Radiya Allahu 'anha): She reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered the slain to be cast into the pit, and they were except Umayyah ibn Khalaf, who swelled in his armor filling it; when they moved him he fell apart, so they left him and covered him with dust and stones. When they had cast them, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood over them asking the people of the pit whether they found what their Lord promised as true, for he found what his Lord promised him as true. His Companions asked if he addresses dead people, and the like of the previous report follows. > **[""O people of the pit, have you found what your Lord promised as true, for I have found what my Lord promised me as true?""]**"
"10025","Narrated by Anas ibn Malik (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered some twenty-odd Quraysh chiefs cast into a foul pit among Badr's pits. It was his custom when victorious to remain on the field three nights, which he did at Badr. On the third day he had his mount saddled, walked forth followed by Companions who thought he went to relieve himself, until he stood at the pit's edge, calling them by their names and fathers' names, asking whether they would now have loved to have obeyed Allah and His Messenger and whether they found what their Lord promised as true. Umar asked why he addresses bodies without souls. He swore by Him in whose hand is Muhammad's soul that they hear no less than the living. Qatadah added Allah revived them to hear his rebuke, belittlement, and disgrace. > **[""O so-and-so son of so-and-so, would it have pleased you to have obeyed Allah and His Messenger? Have you found what your Lord promised as true?""]**"
"10026","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Abbas (Radiya Allahu 'anhuma): He reported that the Prophet ﷺ stood over the Badr slain and declared that Allah recompensed them with evil on his behalf, for they betrayed him though trustworthy and belied him though truthful. He then turned to Abu Jahl ibn Hisham declaring he was more defiant toward Allah than Pharaoh, for Pharaoh affirmed Allah's oneness when certain of ruin, while this one invoked al-Lat and al-Uzza when certain of death. > **[""May Allah recompense you with evil on my behalf, for you betrayed me though trustworthy and belied me though truthful.""]**"
"10027","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood over the people of the pit asking whether they found what their Lord promised as true, for he found what his Lord promised him as true. They asked if the dead hear his words. He replied they hear no less than the living, but today they cannot answer. > **[""O people of the pit, have you found what your Lord promised as true, for I have found what my Lord promised me as true?""]**"
"10028","Narrated by Abdullah ibn Saydan from his father: He reported that the Prophet ﷺ looked over the people of the pit asking whether they found what their Lord promised as true. They asked if they hear. He replied they hear as they hear, but they cannot answer. > **[""O people of the pit, have you found what your Lord promised as true?""]**"
"10029","Narrated by Abu Usayd (Radiya Allahu 'anhu): He reported that on the day of Badr he obtained the sword of Banu Abid ibn al-Marzuban. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered men to return what was in their hands to the spoils, he brought it and cast it into the booty. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never withheld anything he was asked. Al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam al-Makhzumi recognized it, asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for it, and he gave it to him."
"10030","In another narration from Abu Usayd Malik ibn Rabi'ah, may Allah be pleased with him, he reported that on the day of Badr he came upon the sword of the Banu Abid, the Makhzumite Marzuban chief. The report preserves a brief spoil account from Badr without further detail."
"10031","Al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said on the day of Badr that all spoils with the fighters must be returned for collection. Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi, may Allah be pleased with him, then raised the sword of the Banu al-Abid al-Marzuban, which al-Arqam recognized and asked to be granted to him, and the Prophet granted it to him. > **""Return whatever spoils are with you."" > **""Grant it to me, O Messenger of Allah."""
"10032","Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he went out with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and witnessed Badr. Allah routed the enemy, after which one party pursued the fleeing enemy, one party collected the camp and spoils, and one party surrounded the Prophet to guard him from surprise attack. When night came they disputed, the collectors claiming exclusive right, the pursuers claiming they had cleared the enemy from it, and the guards claiming they had been occupied protecting the Prophet. Then the verse was revealed that the spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger and commanding God-consciousness and reconciliation, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divided it equally among the Muslims. He also used to grant an extra fourth at the outset of a raid into enemy land and an extra third on the return, disliked retention of extra grants, and said the strong among the believers should return to the weak. > **""They ask you about the spoils; say, the spoils are for Allah and the Messenger, so fear Allah and set right your mutual relations."" > **""Let the strong among the believers return to the weak."""
"10033","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that on the day of Badr the banner of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was with Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, while the banner of the Ansar was with Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"10034","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say that the people of Badr were three hundred and thirteen men, of whom the Emigrants were seventy-six, and that the rout at Badr occurred on Friday, the seventeenth of Ramadan. In another wording the number is given as three hundred and some-odd, the Ansar as two hundred and thirty-six, and the banner of the Emigrants as with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"10035","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the day of Badr fell on the twenty-seventh of Ramadan."
"10036","Amir ibn Abdullah al-Badri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the morning of Badr was on Monday, the seventeenth of Ramadan."
"10037","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the number of the people of Badr was like the number of Talut's companions on the day of Jalut, three hundred and seventeen."
"10038","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the number of the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at Badr was three hundred."
"10039","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, in a long report said that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, proposed counting themselves against the polytheists. They counted three hundred and thirteen men, the Prophet was pleased, praised Allah, and said it was the number of Talut's companions. > **""Come, let us count ourselves."" > **""The number of Talut's companions."""
"10040","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that twenty men of the clients, the mawali, witnessed Badr with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. The compiler then notes those whose Badr service he already mentioned with chains under their virtues, listing them here without chains: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, Umar ibn al-Khattab, Uthman ibn Affan for whom a share and reward were assigned, Ali ibn Abi Talib, Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, Said ibn Zayd for whom a share was assigned, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, Talhah ibn Ubaydullah for whom a share was assigned, Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, and Hamzah, the uncle of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, may Allah be pleased with them all. He then transmits the extensive roll of those whom Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhri named among the witnesses of Badr, beginning with Aws ibn Thabit ibn al-Mundhir of Banu Awf ibn al-Khazraj who left no offspring, Aws ibn Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Khawli, Unays ibn Qatadah of the Aws, Unaysah the freedman of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, Aswad ibn Zayd ibn Thalabah ibn Ghanm of Banu Salamah, Asad ibn Zayd ibn al-Fakihah of Banu Zurayq, al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam of Quraysh, Bilal the freedman of Abu Bakr and Bishr ibn al-Bara ibn Marur, and continuing through scores of Ansari names from the clans of Khazraj and Aws such as Banu Saidah, Banu Dinar ibn al-Najjar, Banu al-Harith, Banu Salamah, Banu al-Ajlan, Banu al-Najjar, Banu Zurayq, together with Qurashi allies like Rabiah ibn Aktham and Zayd ibn Harithah, Zayd ibn al-Khattab, Abu Talhah Zayd ibn Sahl, Sad ibn Muadh, Sad ibn al-Rabi, and many others recorded at length in the original roster."
"10041","Sahl ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that his brother Thalabah ibn Sad witnessed Badr and was killed on the day of Uhud, leaving no offspring."
"10042","Rifaah ibn Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he and his brother Khallad went out to Badr sharing a weak, emaciated camel of theirs."
"10043","Al-Mughirah ibn Hakim reported that he asked Abdullah ibn Sad ibn Khaythamah, may Allah be pleased with him, whether he had witnessed Badr, and he replied affirmatively, adding that he had also been present at al-Aqabah with his father. > **""Did you witness Badr?"" > **""Yes, and al-Aqabah with my father."""
"10044","Al-Mughirah ibn Hakim reported that he asked Abdullah ibn Sahl, may Allah be pleased with him, whether he had witnessed Badr, and he replied affirmatively, adding that he had also been present at al-Aqabah. > **""Did you witness Badr?"" > **""Yes, and al-Aqabah."""
"10045","Al-Waqidi reported that in that year, meaning 33 AH, Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Asim al-Mazini of Banu Mazin ibn al-Najjar, may Allah be pleased with him, died. He had been in charge of the Prophet's fifth on the day of Badr, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, led his funeral prayer in Madinah."
"10046","Al-Zuhri reported from the son of Amir ibn Rabiah, who was among the chiefs of Banu Adi, that his father had witnessed Badr."
"10047","Abu Idris al-Khawlani reported that Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, was among the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who witnessed Badr and among the naqibs of the night of al-Aqabah."
"10048","Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah reported that he saw Abu Amr, who had been present at Badr, Uhud, and al-Aqabah."
"10049","Unaysah bint Adi, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that she came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said her son Abdullah ibn Salamah, a Badri killed at Uhud, had died and she wished to move him so as to feel near him. The Prophet gave her permission, so she placed him with al-Mujadhdhar ibn Ziyad upon a water camel in a cloak. People wondered at them, the Prophet looked at them and said their deeds had become equal. Abdullah was heavy-bodied while al-Mujadhdhar was slight, and the latter used to recite his verse claiming descent from Bali and thrusting with the spear until it bent. > **""O Messenger of Allah, my son Abdullah ibn Salamah, a Badri, was killed at Uhud and I wish to move him so I may feel near him."" > **""Make their deeds equal between them."""
"10050","Al-Tabarani transmitted in the biography of Hafsah bint Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, through Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Baghawi from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar, that her father Umar ibn al-Khattab, her uncle Zayd, her maternal uncles Uthman, Qudamah, and Abdullah ibn Mazun, and her maternal cousin al-Saib ibn Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, all witnessed Badr."
"10051","Amr ibn Yahya reported from his father from his grandfather Abu Hasan, who had been present at Badr and al-Aqabah, a report which the compiler states he already cited in full in the Book of Prescribed Punishments."
"10052","Makhlad al-Ghifari reported that three slaves belonging to Banu Ghifar witnessed Badr with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"10053","Rafi ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said on the day of Badr that even if a child were born with the understanding of a forty-year-old religious man, obeyed Allah and avoided all disobedience until extreme feeble old age when knowledge leaves after knowledge, he would still not reach the reward of that night. He also said the angels who witnessed Badr excel those among them who stayed back. > **""By Him in whose hand is my soul, even a newborn raised with forty years of religious understanding, working in obedience and avoiding all sin until returned to the feeblest age, would not reach this night of yours."" > **""The angels who witnessed Badr excel those of them who stayed back."""
"10054","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a blind Ansari man sent word to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asking him to mark a mosque in his house for prayer. The Prophet came while his people gathered, one man was absent and was mentioned critically, and the Prophet asked whether he had not witnessed Badr. When they said yes but with such-and-such faults, the Prophet said perhaps Allah had looked upon the people of Badr and forgiven them. > **""What happened to so-and-so?"" > **""Did he not witness Badr?"" > **""Perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said: Do whatever you wish, for I have forgiven you."""
"10055","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said he hoped that, if Allah wills, none who witnessed Badr would enter the Fire. > **""I indeed hope that, if Allah wills, none who witnessed Badr will enter the Fire."""
"10056","Rifaah ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard his father say that Jibril, peace be upon him, said to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that those angels who witnessed Badr excel the rest. > **""Those among the angels who witnessed Badr excel us."""
"10057","Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said he saw in a dream as if he were in a strong coat of mail and saw cattle being slaughtered, interpreting the mail as Madinah and the cattle as men, with Allah being best. His companions first suggested remaining in Madinah to fight any entrants, then others insisted on going out since none had entered upon them even in ignorance, so he said do as you see fit and put on his armor. The Ansar then regretted pressing him against his view and offered to defer to him, but he said it is not for a prophet once he has put on his armor to lay it down before fighting. > **""I saw as if I were in a strong coat of mail, and I saw cattle slaughtered; I interpreted the strong mail as Madinah and the cattle as men, and Allah is best."" > **""Remain as you wish."" > **""It is not for a prophet, once he has put on his armor, to lay it down until he fights."""
"10058","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that when Abu Sufyan and his companions descended upon the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at Uhud, he told his companions he had seen in a dream his sword Dhu al-Faqar broken, a calamity, cattle slaughtered, a calamity, and himself wearing his mail, which was their Madinah, to which the enemy would not reach if Allah wills. > **""I saw in a dream my sword Dhu al-Faqar broken, and it is a calamity; I saw cattle slaughtered, and it is a calamity; and I saw my mail upon me, and it is your Madinah to which they will not reach, if Allah wills."""
"10059","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said he saw as if he had mounted a ram behind him and the hilt of his sword had broken, interpreting it as killing the chief of the enemy and the death of a man of his family. Thus Hamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed, while the Prophet killed Talhah, the enemy standard-bearer. > **""I saw as if I had taken a ram behind me and the hilt of my sword had broken; I interpreted it as killing the ram of the enemy, and the hilt as the killing of a man of my household."""
"10060","Rafi ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he went out on the day of Uhud but the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wished to send him back as young. His uncle said he was an archer and asked that he be allowed, so he was allowed but was struck by an arrow in the chest or throat. His uncle reported the wound, and the Prophet said that if it were left in him and he died, he would die a martyr. Abdullah ibn Husayn added that Rafi's wife used to see it move in his chest when he bathed. > **""O Messenger of Allah, he is an archer, so let him go out."" > **""My nephew has been struck by an arrow."" > **""If you leave it in him and he dies, he will die a martyr."""
"10061","Usayd ibn Zuhayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, deemed Rafi ibn Khadij young on the day of Uhud, whereupon his uncle Usayd said the lad was an archer, so the Prophet permitted him. He was struck in the throat, his uncle brought him to the Prophet and reported the arrow, and the Prophet said that if they wished he would remove it, but if they left it and he died with it in him, he would die a martyr. > **""O Messenger of Allah, he is an archer."" > **""My nephew has been struck by an arrow."" > **""If you wish that I remove it I will remove it, and if you wish to leave it, then if he dies with it in him he will die a martyr."""
"10062","Zayd ibn Jariyah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, deemed several youths too young on the day of Uhud, including Zayd himself, al-Bara ibn Azib, Sad ibn Khaythamah, Abu Said al-Khudri, Abdullah ibn Umar, and Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them."
"10063","Zayd ibn Jariyah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that among those the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, deemed too young on the day of Uhud was Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him."
"10064","Al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he and Ibn Umar were presented to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Badr and were deemed too young, then they witnessed Uhud."
"10065","Muadh of Banu Taym, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wore two coats of mail together on the day of Uhud."
"10066","Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wore two coats of mail together on the day of Uhud."
"10067","Sad, meaning Ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wore two coats of mail together on the day of Uhud."
"10068","Ayyub ibn al-Numan reported from his father from his grandfather that he saw two coats of mail upon the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Uhud."
"10069","Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, offered a sword on the day of Uhud and asked who would take it with its right. Abu Dujanah Simak ibn Kharashah, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and asked its right, took it, and followed destruction through the enemy until he reached women at the mountain foot, among them Hind reciting her rajaz about the daughters of Tariq walking on cushions with musk in their partings, embracing those who advance and parting from those who flee. He charged at her but she cried out in the open and none answered, so he turned away, explaining afterward that he disliked striking a helpless woman with the sword of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. > **""Who will take this sword with its right?"" > **""O Messenger of Allah, I will take it with its right; and what is its right?"" > **""We are the daughters of Tariq, we walk upon cushions; musk is in our partings; if you advance we embrace, if you retreat we part, a parting without love."""
"10070","Qatadah ibn al-Numan, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said on the day of Uhud asking who would take the sword with its right. Ali stood but was told to sit, then at the second call Abu Dujanah stood, and the Prophet handed him his sword Dhu al-Faqar. Abu Dujanah rose with a red band over his eyes lifting his brows from pride, then walked with the sword before the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. > **""Who will take this sword with its right?"" > **""I will, O Messenger of Allah."" > **""Sit down."""
"10071","Khalid ibn Sulayman ibn Abdullah ibn Khalid ibn Simak ibn Kharashah reported from his father from his grandfather that Abu Dujanah, may Allah be pleased with him, marked himself with a red band on the day of Uhud and strutted between the ranks. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said it was a gait Allah hates except in such a place. > **""It is a gait Allah hates except in this place."""
"10072","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the day of Uhud the women were behind the Muslims finishing off the enemy slain, and he would swear that none of them sought this world until Allah revealed that among them were those who sought this world and those who sought the Hereafter, then turned them away to test them. When some companions disobeyed the Prophet's command, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was left alone with nine men, seven of the Ansar and two of Quraysh, himself the tenth. As the enemy closed in he prayed for mercy on whoever would repel them, and Ansari men rose one by one fighting until seven were killed, after which the Prophet said to his two remaining companions that they had not done justice to their fellows. Then Abu Sufyan came shouting Hubal exalted, the Prophet ordered them to answer that Allah is highest and most majestic, and when Abu Sufyan boasted of al-Uzza and of a day for Badr, the Prophet ordered the reply that Allah is their Protector while the disbelievers have no protector, and that the situations are not equal since Muslim slain live rewarded while their slain are punished in the Fire. Abu Sufyan admitted mutilation among the slain without his order yet without dislike, and the Muslims then found Hamzah mutilated. > **""May Allah have mercy on a man who repels them from us."" > **""Say: Allah is highest and most majestic."" > **""Say: Allah is our Protector and the disbelievers have no protector."" > **""They are not equal; our slain are alive receiving provision, while your slain are in the Fire being punished."" > **""Hubal exalted."" > **""We have al-Uzza and you have no Uzza."" > **""A day for the day of Badr; a day for us and a day against us."""
"10073","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said Allah had never aided in a place as He aided on the day of Uhud, citing as proof Allah's words that He fulfilled His promise when they slew them by His permission, where hasa means killing, until weakness and dispute befell the archers. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had stationed them and ordered them to guard the rear whether the Muslims were killed or gained spoils, without aiding or sharing, but when spoils appeared they rushed into the enemy camp to plunder, ranks intermingled, and enemy cavalry entered through their abandoned gap striking in confusion so that many Muslims were killed. Early in the day seven or nine enemy standard-bearers had been killed, the Muslims had not reached the cave but only below al-Mihras, then Satan shouted that Muhammad had been killed and all believed it until the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, appeared walking briskly between the two Sads, bringing joy as if no calamity had struck. He advanced saying Allah's wrath is severe upon those who bloodied the Prophet's face and praying that they not prevail, then Abu Sufyan below the mountain twice shouted Hubal exalted and called for Ibn Abi Kabshah, Ibn Abi Quhafah, and Ibn al-Khattab. Umar asked permission to answer, was told yes, and replied that Allah is highest and most majestic. Abu Sufyan claimed the days alternate and war is give and take, Umar replied they are not equal since Muslim slain are in Paradise and theirs in the Fire, and Abu Sufyan admitted mutilation would be found though not by his chiefs' counsel, then Jahili zeal seized him and he said it had happened and he did not dislike it. > **""Guard our backs; if you see us killed do not aid us, and if you see us gain spoils do not share with us."" > **""Allah's wrath is severe upon a people who bloodied the face of the Messenger of Allah."" > **""O Allah, they have no right to prevail over us."" > **""Hubal exalted."" > **""Yes."" > **""Allah is highest and most majestic."" > **""A day for the day of Badr; days alternate and war is give and take."" > **""They are not equal; our slain are in Paradise and your slain are in the Fire."""
"10074","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he asked Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, his maternal uncle, may Allah be pleased with him, about their story on the day of Uhud. He told him to read after verse one hundred and twenty of Al Imran, from when the Prophet left his household to station the believers for battle through when two parties intended to fail, those who had sought security, through you used to wish for death before meeting it, to when you slew them by His permission. > **""Read after one hundred and twenty of Al Imran and you will find our story."""
"10075","Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the people scattered from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Uhud, he searched among the slain and did not see the Prophet. He reasoned the Prophet would never flee nor lay among the slain, feared Allah had lifted him in anger at their deeds, and resolved that nothing was better than fighting until killed. So he broke his sword sheath, charged until the enemy gave way for him, and there was the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, among them."
"10076","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported from her father that when the people turned away from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at Uhud, he was the first to return. He saw a man fighting before the Prophet and hoped it was Talhah, while another man came from behind swift as a bird, and it was Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with them. Talhah lay fallen before the Prophet, who said to attend to their brother for he had earned it. They turned to the Prophet to find two arrows in his face; when Abu Bakr sought to remove them, Abu Ubaydah insisted until allowed, seized one with his teeth and pulled it out losing a front tooth, then likewise the second, so that Abu Ubaydah became toothless in the front. > **""Look to your brother, for he has earned it."""
"10077","Kab ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the day of Uhud when they reached the mountain pass, he was the first to recognize the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. When he announced it, the Prophet signaled silence, dressed him in his own armor and wore his in exchange. Kab fought until he received twenty or some twenty wounds, every striker thinking him the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"10078","Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the people wheeled away from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Uhud, he resolved to hold fast whether martyred or saved until meeting the Prophet. He saw a veiled man whose face he did not know hurling handfuls of pebbles at the advancing polytheists until they retreated backward toward the mountain, repeatedly. With al-Miqdad between them, before he could ask, al-Miqdad told him the Messenger of Allah was calling him. He rose as if unhurt, was asked where he had been all day, and was seated before the Prophet shooting while praying for an arrow against Allah's enemy as the Prophet prayed for Sad to be answered and steadied, urging him on with his parents as ransom. With every arrow the Prophet repeated the prayer until Sad's quiver emptied, then the Prophet emptied his own quiver for him and handed him a featherless arrow which proved hardest. Al-Zuhri said the arrows Sad shot that day were a thousand. > **""Where have you been all day, O Sad?"" > **""O Allah, it is an arrow with which I strike Your enemy."" > **""O Allah, answer Sad; O Allah, steady his aim; shoot on, O Sad, may my father and mother be your ransom."" > **""O Allah, steady his aim and answer his prayer; shoot on, O Sad."""
"10079","Qatadah ibn al-Numan, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a bow gifted to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was handed to him on the day of Uhud. He shot before the Prophet until its string broke, remaining posted before the Prophet's face meeting arrows with his own face, turning his head to shield the Prophet without shooting back. The last arrow gouged his eyeball into his palm, which he carried running to the Prophet. When the Prophet saw it, his eyes filled with tears and he prayed that since Qatadah had shielded his Prophet with his face, Allah make it his finest eye and sharpest in sight, and so it became. > **""O Allah, Qatadah has shielded Your Prophet with his face, so make it his finest eye and sharpest in sight."""
"10080","Qatadah ibn al-Numan, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the day of Uhud he stood before the face of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, shielding it with his own face, while Abu Dujanah Simak ibn Kharashah, may Allah be pleased with him, shielded the Prophet's back with his own back until his back was filled with arrows."
"10081","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that only four remained with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Uhud, one of them Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him. When asked where Ali was, his father answered that Ali held the banner of the Emigrants."
"10082","Mahmud ibn Labid reported that al-Harith ibn al-Simmah, may Allah be pleased with him, said the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asked him in the mountain pass on the day of Uhud whether he had seen Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf. He had seen him at the mountain base surrounded by enemy troops but turned toward the Prophet instead. The Prophet said the angels were fighting with him. Al-Harith returned to find Abd al-Rahman among seven slain enemies; Abd al-Rahman said he had killed two including Artah ibn Shurahbil, while those he had not seen killed the rest, whereupon al-Harith affirmed the truth of Allah and His Messenger. > **""Have you seen Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf?"" > **""Yes, O Messenger of Allah, I saw him at the mountain base with enemy troops upon him, but I saw you and turned to you."" > **""Indeed the angels are fighting with him."" > **""Your right hand has prospered; did you kill all of these?"" > **""Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth."""
"10083","Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the face of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was wounded on the day of Uhud. Malik ibn Sinan, may Allah be pleased with him, met him, sucked the wound and swallowed it, whereupon the Prophet said whoever wishes to look at one whose blood has mingled with his blood should look at Malik ibn Sinan. > **""Whoever wishes to look at one whose blood has mingled with my blood, let him look at Malik ibn Sinan."""
"10084","Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he saw Hind bint Utbah on the day of Uhud with her shins bared, as if still seeing the mark of leprosy on her shin, while she incited the people."
"10085","Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, killed the standard-bearers, Jibril, peace be upon him, said this was true comradeship. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said Ali was from him and he from Ali, and Jibril said he was from them both. > **""O Messenger of Allah, this is true comradeship."" > **""He is from me and I am from him."" > **""And I am from you both, O Messenger of Allah."""
"10086","Safiyyah bint Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out to the Trench, he placed the women in the fort called Fari with Hassan ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, who would advance and retreat in the fort with the Prophet's movements. Men of the Jews came and one remained until he loomed over them; she told Hassan to kill him but he said it was not in him, for had it been he would have been with the Prophet. So Safiyyah struck his head until she severed it, asked Hassan to throw it to those below the fort, and when he again declined, she threw it herself. The Jews said they knew Muhammad had not left his family unguarded, and dispersed. She also passed Sad ibn Muadh with a trace of yellowness as if recently wed, reciting his verse that shortly the camel would reach the fray and there is no harm in death when the term comes. > **""Get up to him and kill him."" > **""It is not in me; had it been in me I would have been with the Messenger of Allah."" > **""O Hassan, take his head and throw it to them below the fort."" > **""Slowly, soon the camel will reach the fray; there is no harm in death when the term comes."""
"10087","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the day of Uhud Madinah fell into turmoil with cries that Muhammad had been killed. An Ansari woman in pilgrim garb went out and was met with her father, son, husband, and brother, not remembering whom first; at each she asked who it was and was told, yet kept asking what had happened to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. When told he was ahead and she was brought to him, she seized his garment and said with her parents as ransom she cared for no calamity while he was safe. > **""Who is this?"" > **""What happened to the Messenger of Allah?"" > **""He is ahead of you."" > **""May my father and mother be your ransom, O Messenger of Allah, I care for no calamity while you are safe."""
"10088","Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the day of Uhud he joined the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in Madinah when none of the Prophet's companions remained there and the slain multiplied. A crier shouted that Muhammad had been killed and women wept, but one woman said not to hasten weeping until she looked. She went out walking with no concern except the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and asking about him."
"10089","Uqbah, the freedman of Jabr ibn Atik, reported that he witnessed Uhud with other freedmen, struck and killed a polytheist, and said take it from me for I am a Persian man. When it reached the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he asked why he had not said he was an Ansari youth, since the freedman of a people is of themselves. > **""Take it from me, for I am a Persian man."" > **""Why did you not say: Take it, for I am an Ansari youth? For the freedman of a people is of themselves."""
"10090","Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said that in the next year at Uhud they were requited for taking ransom at Badr. Seventy were killed, the companions fled from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, his front tooth was broken, his helmet crushed upon his head, and blood flowed over him. Allah then revealed that when a calamity befell them though they had inflicted twice as much, they asked whence it was, for it was from themselves through their taking ransom, while Allah has power over all things."
"10091","Sahl ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the day of Uhud they told the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, they had never seen one fight like a certain man who never fled while others fled and pursued every stray of the enemy with his sword. After his lineage and description failed to identify him, the man himself appeared, the Prophet asked if it was he, they confirmed, and he said the man was of the Fire. This weighed heavily on the Muslims, so one man vowed to stay with him and watch his end, charging and retreating with him until a wound disabled him. The fighter hastened death by planting his sword hilt in the ground, placing its point between his breasts, and throwing himself upon it until it emerged from his back. The watcher ran back testifying to Allah's oneness and the Prophet's messengership and recounting the suicide, whereupon the Prophet said a man may do the deeds of Paradise as it appears to people yet be of the Fire, and a man may do the deeds of the Fire as it appears yet be of Paradise. > **""O Messenger of Allah, on Uhud we never saw one fight like so-and-so."" > **""Who is he?"" > **""Is this he?"" > **""He is of the people of the Fire."" > **""I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah."" > **""What happened?"" > **""A man may do the deeds of the people of Paradise, as it appears to people, yet he is of the people of the Fire; and a man may do the deeds of the people of the Fire, as it appears to people, yet he is of the people of Paradise."""
"10092","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Amr ibn al-As: peace upon you, your letter mentioning the Roman hosts has reached me; Allah never aided us with His Prophet through numbers or troops, for we raided with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with only a few mares and sharing camels in turn. At Uhud with the Prophet we had but one horse which the Prophet rode, yet He made us prevail and aided us against our opponents. Know, O Amr, that the most obedient to Allah hates sins most, so obey Allah and command your companions to obey Him. > **""Peace upon you; your letter mentioning what hosts Rome has gathered has reached me; Allah never aided us with His Prophet through numbers or troops."" > **""Know, O Amr, that the most obedient of people to Allah is the one who most hates sins; so obey Allah and command your companions to obey Him."""
"10093","Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, commenting on His words that after grief He sent down security in slumber, said that sleep was cast upon them on the day of Uhud."
"10094","Sabrah ibn Mabad, may Allah be pleased with him, was present at Uhud with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when a stone struck his leg, leaving him limping from it until he died."
"10095","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the day the Prophet's face was wounded, they kept carrying water for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in camel skins."
"10096","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that Abdullah ibn Qamiah threw a stone at the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Uhud, wounding his face and breaking his front tooth, boasting take it for I am Ibn Qamiah. The Prophet, wiping blood from his face, asked what was with him and prayed that Allah humble him, after which Allah set upon him a mountain goat that kept goring him until it tore him piece by piece. > **""Take it, for I am Ibn Qamiah."" > **""What is with you? May Allah humble you."""
"10097","Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said in prayer for his people that they did not know. > **""O Allah, forgive my people, for they do not know."""
"10098","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that Allah's wrath is severe upon a people who crushed the helmet upon their Prophet's head while he called them to Allah. > **""Allah's wrath is severe upon a people who crushed the helmet upon the head of their Prophet while he called them to Allah."""
"10099","Al-Zubayr, meaning Ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the day of Uhud a woman came hastening until she nearly overlooked the slain. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, disliked her seeing them and said to hold back the woman. Al-Zubayr recognized her as his mother Safiyyah, may Allah be pleased with her, rushed to her before she reached the slain, and she struck his chest, being a vigorous woman, telling him away. When he said the Messenger of Allah had adjured her, she stopped, produced two garments she had brought to shroud her brother Hamzah whose killing had reached her, and asked that he be shrouded in them. They brought the garments but beside Hamzah lay a slain Ansari treated as Hamzah had been treated, so they felt shame that Hamzah be shrouded in two while the Ansari had none. They allotted one to each, found one larger, drew lots between them, and shrouded each in the garment that fell to him. > **""Hold back the woman, hold back the woman."" > **""Away from me, may you have no ground."" > **""The Messenger of Allah has adjured you."" > **""These are two garments I brought for my brother Hamzah, for his killing has reached me, so shroud him in them."""
"10100","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that when Hamzah was killed on the day of Uhud, Safiyyah, may Allah be pleased with her, came asking what had happened and met Ali and al-Zubayr, asking them what had become of Hamzah. They made her think they did not know, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, smiled and said he feared for her mind, placed his hand on her chest, and she uttered the return to Allah and wept. He then stood over Hamzah and said that were it not for the grief of women he would have left him until gathered from the bellies of beasts and crops of birds. The slain were then brought and he prayed over them, seven with Hamzah each time with seven takbirs, the others lifted while Hamzah remained, then nine likewise, until he finished with them. > **""O Ali, O Zubayr, what happened to Hamzah?"" > **""I fear for her mind."" > **""Were it not for the grief of women, I would have left him until he is gathered from the bellies of beasts and the crops of birds."""
"10101","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the killing of Hamzah reached the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he wept, and when he looked upon him he sobbed deeply."
"10102","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, uncovered Hamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, he wept, and when he saw his mutilation he sobbed deeply."
"10103","Kab ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asked who had seen the place of Hamzah's killing. A man said Allah grant him honor, he had seen it, led him to Hamzah whose belly had been split and who had been mutilated, and reported the mutilation. The Prophet disliked looking at him, stood among the slain, and said he was a witness over them, to wrap them in their blood, for no one wounded in Allah's path comes on Resurrection with his wound bleeding in the color of blood and the scent of musk, to advance the most Quranic, and to place them in the side grave. > **""Who saw the place of Hamzah's killing?"" > **""May Allah honor you, I saw his killing place."" > **""O Messenger of Allah, he has been mutilated."" > **""I am a witness over these; wrap them in their blood, for no one wounded in the path of Allah comes on the Day of Resurrection but his wound bleeds with the color of blood and the scent of musk; advance the most Quranic among them and place him in the side grave."""
"10104","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood over Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib when he was martyred and beheld a sight more heart-rending than any he had seen, with mutilation upon him. He said mercy be upon him, for he had been most joining of kin and most active in good, and swore that were it not for after-grief over him he would have wished to leave him until Allah gathered him from the bellies of beasts, but would instead mutilate seventy like his mutilation. Then Jibril, peace be upon him, descended upon Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with the chapter reciting that if you punish, punish with the like of what you were afflicted with, to the end of the verse, whereupon the Prophet expiated his oath and refrained. > **""May Allah's mercy be upon you, for as far as I knew you were most joining of kin and most active in good deeds."" > **""By Allah, were it not for after-grief over you, it would please me to leave you until Allah gathers you from the bellies of beasts."" > **""By Allah, for that I shall surely mutilate seventy like your mutilation."""
"10105","Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the grave of Hamza ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him. They kept drawing the striped cloak over his face and his feet would become exposed, and drawing it over his feet and his face would become exposed. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Put it over his face, and put some of this foliage over his feet.""** He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his head, and behold his Companions were weeping. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There will come upon people a time when they will go out to the countryside and find there food, dwelling, and mounts — or he said: mounts — and they will write to their families: Come to us, for you are in a barren land of Hijaz. Yet Madina is better for them, if only they knew. No one endures its hardship and severity except that I will be his intercessor — or his witness — on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"10106","'Abdullah ibn Ja'far, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood over Hamza on the day of Uhud while he was being buried. He was wrapped in a striped cloak, and when they covered his head his feet showed. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered harmal shrubs, and they were placed over his feet. And he said: > **""Were it not that women would grieve over that, we would have left Hamza in the open for the birds of prey and the beasts to feed upon.""**"
"10107","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood over Hamza and saw what had been done to him, he said: > **""Were it not that our women would grieve, I would not have buried him, and I would have left him until he ended up in the bellies of beasts and the crops of birds, from whence Allah would raise him.""** He said: What he saw grieved him, so he said: > **""If I prevail over them, I shall surely mutilate thirty men among them.""** Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed concerning that: If you punish, then punish with the like of what you were afflicted with; but if you are patient, it is better for the patient — up to His saying: what they plot. Then he ordered that Hamza be prepared facing the qibla. He pronounced nine takbirs over him. Then the martyrs were gathered to him; whenever a martyr was brought he was placed beside him, and he prayed over him and over the martyrs seventy-two prayers. Then he stood over his Companions until they buried them. And when the Qur'an came down, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pardoned, forbore, and abandoned mutilation."
"10108","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: Hamza was killed on the day of Uhud, and with him a man of the Ansar was killed. Safiyya bint 'Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with her, came with two garments so that Hamza might be shrouded in them, but there was no shroud for the Ansari. So the Prophet ﷺ cast lots between the two garments, then each of the two was shrouded in one garment."
"10109","Ibn 'Umar and Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with them, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from Uhud, he heard the women of the Ansar weeping. So he said: > **""But Hamza has no mourners.""** That reached the women of the Ansar, so they wept for Hamza. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slept, then awoke while they were still weeping, and he said: > **""Woe to them, they have continued weeping since today. Let them weep, but let them not weep over any dead person after today.""**"
"10110","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from Uhud, the women of the Ansar wept over their martyrs. That reached the Prophet ﷺ, so he said: > **""But Hamza has no mourners.""** So the Ansar returned and said to their women: Do not weep over anyone until you first lament Hamza. He said: That practice remained among them until today — they do not weep over a dead person except that they begin with Hamza."
"10111","Wahshi, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When I came to the Prophet ﷺ after the killing of Hamza, he spat in my face three times, then said: > **""Do not show me your face.""**"
"10112","Wahshi, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ and he said to me: > **""Are you Wahshi?""** I said: Yes. He said: > **""Did you kill Hamza?""** I said: Yes. And all praise belongs to Allah who honored him by my hand and did not disgrace me by his hand. Quraysh said to him: Do you love him, though he is the killer of Hamza? So I said: O Messenger of Allah, seek forgiveness for me. So he spat on the ground three times, pushed against my chest three times, and said: > **""O Wahshi, go forth and fight in the path of Allah as you once fought to turn people away from the path of Allah.""**"
"10113","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: When Abu Sufyan and the polytheists turned away from Uhud and reached al-Rawha, Abu Sufyan said: You neither killed Muhammad nor carried off the maidens — evil is what you have done. That reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he called the people and they responded, until they reached Hamra al-Asad, or the well of Banu 'Ayn. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: Those who responded to Allah and the Messenger after injury had touched them. That was because Abu Sufyan had said to the Prophet ﷺ: Your appointment is the fair of Badr, where you killed our companions. As for the coward, he turned back, while the brave took up preparations for fighting and trade. They came to it but found no one there, and they delayed. Then Allah, Glorious is His mention, revealed: So they returned with a blessing from Allah and bounty, untouched by harm."
"10114","'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah al-Zuraqi, from his father — al-Fazari once said: from Ibn Rifa'a al-Zuraqi from his father, while others besides al-Fazari said: from 'Ubayd ibn Rifa'a al-Zuraqi — said: On the day of Uhud, when the polytheists turned back, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Stand straight until I praise my Lord.""** So they formed rows behind him, and he said: > **""O Allah, all praise belongs to You. O Allah, none constricts what You expand, and none expands what You constrict. None guides whom You lead astray, and none leads astray whom You guide. None gives what You withhold, and none withholds what You give. None brings near what You distance, and none distances what You bring near. O Allah, spread over us from Your blessings, Your mercy, Your bounty, and Your provision. O Allah, I ask You for lasting bliss that neither changes nor passes away. O Allah, I ask You for bliss on the day of need and security on the day of fear. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the evil of what You have given us and the evil of what You have withheld from us. O Allah, make faith beloved to us, adorn it in our hearts, make disbelief, wickedness, and disobedience hateful to us, and make us among the rightly guided. O Allah, cause us to die as Muslims, keep us alive as Muslims, and join us with the righteous, neither disgraced nor tested. O Allah, destroy the disbelievers who deny Your messengers and turn people away from Your path, and send upon them Your punishment and Your torment. O Allah, destroy the disbelievers among those given the Book, O God of creation.""** In one narration the wording is: O Allah, destroy the disbelieving People of the Book."
"10115","Burayda, may Allah be pleased with him, said: On the day of Uhud a man said: O Allah, if Muhammad is upon the truth, then swallow me into the earth. He said: So he was swallowed into it."
"10116","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, on the day of Uhud and said: O Fatima, take the sword, free of blame — for I am neither a coward nor base. By my life, I did my utmost in aiding Ahmad and seeking the pleasure of a Lord who knows His servants. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If you fought well, then Sahl ibn Hunayf and Ibn al-Simma fought well.""** And he mentioned another man whom Mu'alla forgot. Then Jibril, peace be upon him, said: O Muhammad, this, by your father, is true solidarity. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Jibril, he is from me.""** So Jibril, peace be upon him, said: And I am from you both."
"10117","Sahl ibn Hunayf, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'Ali came to Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, on the day of Uhud and said: Hold this sword of mine, for I struck well with it today. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If you fought well, then 'Asim ibn Thabit, Sahl ibn Hunayf, and al-Harith ibn al-Simma fought well.""**"
"10118","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: 'Ali ibn Abi Talib entered upon Fatima on the day of Uhud and said: Take this sword, free of blame. So the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""If you fought well, then Sahl ibn Hunayf and Abu Dujana Simak ibn Kharasha fought well.""**"
"10119","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, when the companions of Uhud were mentioned: > **""Indeed, by Allah, I wish I had been left with my companions at the foot of the mountain.""** Meaning the lower slope of the mountain."
"10120","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by Mus'ab ibn 'Umayr when he returned from Uhud, and stopped with his Companions over him. He said: > **""I bear witness that you are alive with Allah. So visit them and greet them with peace. By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, no one greets them with peace but that they return it to him until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"10121","Sa'id ibn Jubayr said: Hamza was struck on the day of Uhud."
"10122","Ibn Ishaq, in naming those of the Muslims martyred on the day of Uhud, then among the Muhajirun, said: Hamza ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib."
"10123","Al-Haythami said: Ibn Shihab named a group martyred on the day of Uhud with a single chain; many of them were already mentioned among those who attended Badr, and I shall mention those who remain. His men up to Ibn Shihab are men of the Sahih. Among them: 1. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj: Aws ibn al-Arqam. 2. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Zurayq: Unays ibn Qatada. 3. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Nabit: Iyas ibn Aws. 4. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'ida: Tha'laba ibn Sa'id ibn Malik. 5. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Zurayq: Hanzala ibn Abi 'Amir, he whom the angels washed. 6. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Nabit: al-Harith ibn Aws ibn Rafi'. 7. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Zurayq: Dhakwan ibn 'Abd Qays. 8. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sawad: Rifa'a ibn 'Amr. 9. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Harith: Sa'd ibn al-Rabi'. 10. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj: Sa'd ibn Suwayd. 11. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sawad: Sa'd ibn Qays ibn Abi Ka'b ibn al-Qayn. 12. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Salima: 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn Haram. He said: 'Urwa ibn al-Zubayr mentioned a group among those martyred on the day of Uhud, some of whom were already mentioned among those who attended Badr, and I shall mention those who remain among them. 13. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Najjar: Aws ibn al-Mundhir. 14. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Mu'awiya ibn 'Amr: Iyas ibn Aws. 15. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'ida: Tha'laba ibn Sa'd ibn Malik ibn Khalid ibn Tha'laba ibn Haritha. 16. Killed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among the Muslims on the day of Uhud, then of Banu Hashim: Hamza ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib — Wahshi ibn Harb killed him. 17. Of the Ansar, then of Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf: al-Harith ibn Aws ibn Rafi'. 18. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Zurayq: Dhakwan ibn 'Abd Qays. 19. Of the Ansar: Rifa'a ibn Aws ibn Za'wra ibn 'Abd al-Ashhal. 20. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Mu'awiya ibn 'Awf: Rabi'a ibn al-Fadl ibn Habib ibn Yazid ibn Tamim. 21. Martyred on the day of Uhud among the Muslims of Quraysh: Rabi'a ibn Aktham, an ally of Banu Asad ibn 'Abd Shams, of Banu Asad. 22. Of the Ansar: Sa'd ibn al-Rabi'. 23. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Nabit: Sulayt ibn Thabit ibn Waqsh. 24. Martyred on the day of Uhud with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, of Banu Umayya ibn 'Abd Shams: 'Abdullah ibn Jahsh, an ally of theirs from Banu Asad ibn Khuzayma — the hadith of Sa'd concerning how he was killed will come in the virtues of 'Abdullah ibn Jahsh, if Allah wills. 25. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Salima: 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn Haram ibn Tha'laba. 26. Al-Tabarani said: Mus'ab ibn 'Umayr ibn Hashim ibn 'Abd Manaf ibn 'Abd al-Dar ibn Qusayy, of the first Muhajirun, was martyred on the day of Uhud."
"10124","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out on Friday after praying Jumu'a, reached the pass of Uhud by morning, and they met on Saturday in the middle of Shawwal."
"10125","'Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Jibril, peace be upon him, came to the Prophet ﷺ when his Companions were exhausted and he was washing his head. He said: O Muhammad, you have laid down your weapons, but the angels have not yet laid down their burdens. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lifted his head before finishing washing it. Then they went to al-Nadir, and Allah granted him victory over them."
"10126","Sahl ibn Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl came to the Prophet ﷺ in Madina. He disputed with the Prophet ﷺ and raised his voice, while Thabit ibn Qays stood over the Prophet ﷺ with his sword. He said: > **""O 'Amir, lower your voice before the Prophet ﷺ.""** He said: What is that to you? Thabit said: > **""By the One who honored him, were it not that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would dislike it, I would strike your head with this sword.""** 'Amir, sitting while Thabit stood, said: By Allah, O Thabit, if you offered yourself to me you would turn away from me. Thabit said: > **""By Allah, O 'Amir, if you offered yourself to my tongue you would hate your life.""** Then a nephew of 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl sneezed and praised Allah, so the Prophet ﷺ blessed him. Then 'Amir sneezed and did not praise Allah, so the Prophet ﷺ did not bless him. 'Amir said: You blessed this boy and left me? The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""He praised Allah.""** He said: By His oath, I shall fill it against you with horses and men. So the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Allah and the two sons of Qayla will suffice me against you.""** Then 'Amir left and gathered men against the Prophet ﷺ; three clans of Banu Sulaym joined him — those whom the Prophet ﷺ used to curse in the dawn prayer: > **""O Allah, curse Lihyan, Ri'l, Dhakwan, and 'Usayya, who disobeyed Allah and His Messenger. Allah is Greatest.""** The Prophet ﷺ supplicated for seventeen nights. When he heard that 'Amir had gathered against him, he sent ten men among them 'Amr ibn Umayya al-Damri of the Ansar, with Mundhir ibn 'Amr as their leader. They camped at Bi'r Ma'una; the enemy came, surrounded them, and killed them all except 'Amr ibn Umayya. Allah revealed to His Prophet ﷺ that very day his best Companions were slain. The Prophet ﷺ said: Look at your companions who were slain. Then the Prophet ﷺ supplicated against 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl, saying: O Allah, suffice me against 'Amir. So Allah sufficed him: he alighted in a courtyard, Allah afflicted his throat with a glandular swelling, while he was in the house of a woman of Sallul. Blood flowed from his wound and he kept saying: O family of 'Amir, a gland like a camel's gland in the house of a Sallul woman. He wished to avoid dying in her house but remained there until he died in it. Lightning struck Arbad ibn Qays and burned him, and all those with them turned back."
"10127","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Haram, the brother of Umm Sulaym, with seventy men who were killed on the day of Bi'r Ma'una — the chief of the polytheists that day being 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl, who had come to the Prophet ﷺ and said: Choose one of three from me: the people of the plain will be yours and the people of the desert mine; or I shall be caliph after you; or I shall raid you with Ghatafan, a thousand sorrel stallions and a thousand sorrel mares. He said: Then he was stricken in the house of a woman of such-and-such tribe. He said: A gland like a camel's gland, in the house of a woman of such-and-such tribe — bring me my horse. It was brought, he mounted it, and died upon its back. Then Haram, the brother of Umm Sulaym, set out with two men: a man of Banu Umayya and a lame man. He said to them: Stay near me until I go to them; if they grant me safety, well and good, otherwise remain near me — if they kill me, inform our companions. So Haram came to them and said: > **""Will you grant me safety so that I may convey to you the message of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?""** They said: Yes. He began speaking to them, and they signaled to one of their men behind him, who stabbed him with a spear clean through. He said: > **""Allah is Greatest. I have succeeded, by the Lord of the Ka'ba.""** He said: Then they killed them all except the lame man, who was on a mountaintop. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"10128","In one narration Hammam said: I think he mentioned another with the lame man on the mountain. Al-Haythami said: It is in the Sahih in abridged form."
"10129","Ka'b ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Mula'ib al-Asinna came to the Prophet ﷺ with a gift, and he offered him Islam, but he refused to accept it. So the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""I do not accept the gift of a polytheist.""** He said: Then send whomever you wish to the people of Najd, and I shall grant them protection. So he sent to them a party among whom was al-Mundhir ibn 'Amr, called al-Mu'taq li-yamut. 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl rallied Banu 'Amir against them, but they refused to obey him and refused to violate the protection of Mula'ib al-Asinna. So he rallied Banu Sulaym against them and they obeyed him; nearly a hundred archers followed them, caught up with them at Bi'r Ma'una, and killed them all except 'Amr ibn Umayya."
"10130","'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik and others said: 'Amir ibn Malik, called Mula'ib al-Asinna, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while a polytheist. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered him Islam, and said: > **""I do not accept the gift of a polytheist.""** So 'Amir ibn Malik said: O Messenger of Allah, send whomever of your messengers you wish, and I shall grant them protection. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a party among them al-Mundhir ibn 'Amr al-Sa'idi, called U'tiq li-yamut, as scouts among the people of Najd. 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl heard of them, sought help from Banu Sulaym, and they marched with him and killed them at Bi'r Ma'una except 'Amr ibn Umayya al-Damri, whom 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl seized and released. He alone came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from among them. Among them was 'Amir ibn Fuhayra. 'Urwa told me: He was killed that day and his body was not found when they buried them. 'Urwa says: They used to think the angels buried him. Then Hassan lampooned 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl, saying: O sons of Umm al-Banin, did it not alarm you, though you are of the noblest of Najd — the mockery of 'Amir toward Abu Bara, violating his protection, and a mistake is not like an intentional act. Then Rabi'a ibn 'Amir ibn Malik reproached 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl for violating the protection of 'Amir ibn Malik, stabbing him in the thigh with a thrust that laid it open."
"10131","'Urwa ibn al-Zubayr said: Then came the expedition of al-Mundhir ibn 'Amr, brother of Banu Sa'ida, to Bi'r Ma'una. He sent al-Muttalib al-Sulami with them to guide them on the road. The enemies of Allah sent to 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl seeking his aid, and he aided them against the Muslims. Al-Mundhir ibn 'Amr and his companions were killed except 'Amr ibn Umayya al-Damri, whom they captured and spared until they brought him to Makka — he is the one who buried Khubayb ibn 'Adi. The polytheists offered 'Urwa ibn al-Salt on the day of Bi'r Ma'una that they would grant him safety, but he refused, so they killed him. It was mentioned to us that the Muslims said on the day of Bi'r Ma'una, when the enemy surrounded them: > **""O Allah, we find none to convey from us to Your Messenger but You. O Allah, convey our greetings of peace to him from us, and inform him of our news.""**"
"10132","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained after Uhud through the rest of Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'da, Dhu al-Hijja, and Muharram, then sent his Companions to Bi'r Ma'una in Safar, four months after Uhud. Ishaq narrated to me from al-Mughira ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, and other scholars. They said: Abu Bara 'Amir ibn Malik ibn Ja'far, Mula'ib al-Asinna, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madina. He offered him Islam; he did not accept, yet was not far from Islam. He said: > **""O Muhammad, if you sent a man of your Companions to call them to your cause, I hope they would respond to you.""** The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I fear the people of Najd.""** Abu Bara said: I grant them protection — send them to call people to your cause. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent al-Mundhir ibn 'Amr, brother of Banu Sa'ida of Khazraj, called al-Mu'taq li-yamut, with forty of the best Muslims, among them al-Harith ibn al-Simma, Haram ibn Milhan brother of Banu 'Adi ibn al-Najjar, 'Urwa ibn Asma ibn al-Salt al-Sulami, Nafi' ibn Budayl ibn Warqa al-Khuza'i, 'Amir ibn Fuhayra freedman of Abu Bakr, and other named best Muslims. They marched until they camped at Bi'r Ma'una, a well in Banu 'Amir land near the lava field of Banu Sulaym, closer to Sulaym. When they camped they sent Haram ibn Milhan with the letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl. He did not even look at the letter but rushed at him and killed him. Then he called Banu 'Amir for help, but they refused, saying: We will not violate the protection of Abu Bara, for he contracted and guaranteed them. So he called the clans of Sulaym — 'Usayya, Ri'l, and Dhakwan — and they obeyed. They set out, surrounded the Muslims at their camp, and when the Muslims saw them they drew their swords and fought until the last of them was slain, except Ka'b ibn Zayd of Banu Dinar ibn al-Najjar, whom they left for dead among the slain with a spark of life; he lived on until he was martyred at al-Khandaq. 'Amr ibn Umayya al-Damri and an Ansari of Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf learned of their companions' fate from a bird circling the army. They came to see, found the men weltering in blood and their horses standing by. The Ansari said to 'Amr ibn Umayya: What is your view? He said: I think we should go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and inform him. The Ansari said: But I have no desire to leave the place where Mundhir ibn 'Amr was slain. So he fought them until he was slain. Then they captured 'Amr ibn Umayya, and when he told them he was of Mudar, 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl released him, cut his forelock, and freed him in place of a slave his mother owed. 'Amr set out until at Qarqara in Quba two men of Banu 'Amir camped with him in one shade. When they slept he fell upon them and killed them, thinking he had taken revenge. When he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him, the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""You have killed two men whose blood money I shall surely pay. This is the deed of Abu Bara; I disliked it and feared it.""** When Abu Bara heard, it weighed heavily on him that 'Amir violated his protection and thereby harmed the Companions of Muhammad ﷺ. So Hassan ibn Thabit incited the son of Abu Bara against 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl, saying: O sons of Umm al-Banin, did it not alarm you, though you are of the noblest of Najd — the mockery of 'Amir toward Abu Bara, violating him, and a mistake is not like an intentional act. Convey to Rabi'a of lofty deeds what happened after me: your father is the father of wars, Abu Bara, and your uncle is the noble Hakam ibn Sa'd. So Rabi'a ibn 'Amir charged 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl and stabbed him with a spear in the thigh, laying it open; he fell from his horse and said: This is the deed of Abu Bara — if I die, my blood is for my uncle and not to be pursued, and if I live I shall decide what to do about what was done to me."
"10133","'Urwa, in naming those of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ martyred on the day of Bi'r Ma'una, said: Aws ibn Mu'adh al-Ansari, al-Hakam ibn Kaysan al-Makhzumi, al-Harith ibn al-Simma, Sahl ibn 'Amir ibn Sa'd ibn 'Amr ibn Thaqab al-Ansari, and of Quraysh then of Banu Taym ibn Murra: 'Amir ibn Fuhayra."
"10134","Ibn Shihab, in naming those of the Muslims martyred on the day of Bi'r Ma'una, said: al-Harith ibn al-Simma."
"10135","Muhammad ibn Ishaq, in naming those of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ martyred on the day of Bi'r Ma'una, said: Nafi' ibn Yazid ibn Warqa al-Khuza'i."
"10136","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Beware of testifying; if you must do so, then testify for the expedition whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent and who were struck down. Concerning them the Qur'an came down — convey from us to our people: > **""That we have met our Lord; He is pleased with us and has made us pleased.""**"
"10137","'Amr ibn 'Awf al-Muzani, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ marked the trench from the red saline ground at the edge of Banu Haritha in the year of the Confederates until it reached al-Madhahij. He allotted forty cubits to every ten men. The Muhajirun and Ansar disputed over Salman al-Farisi, who was a strong man. The Muhajirun said: Salman is from us. The Ansar said: From us. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Salman is from us, the Household.""**"
"10138","Al-Bara ibn 'Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered us to dig the trench. A rock appeared in one part of the trench that pickaxes could not cut, so they complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He came — I think he said: he laid aside his garment — then descended to the rock, took the pickaxe and said: > **""In the Name of Allah.""** He struck once and broke a third of the stone, and said: > **""Allah is Greatest. I have been given the keys of Sham. By Allah, I surely see its red palaces from this place of mine.""** Then he said: > **""In the Name of Allah.""** And struck again, breaking another third, and said: > **""Allah is Greatest. I have been given the keys of Persia. By Allah, I surely see al-Mada'in, and I see its white palace from this place of mine.""** Then he said: > **""In the Name of Allah.""** And struck another blow, cutting the rest of the stone, and said: > **""Allah is Greatest. I have been given the keys of Yemen. By Allah, I surely see the gates of San'a from this place of mine.""**"
"10139","'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered the trench and it was dug around Madina. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we found a smooth rock we cannot dig. The Prophet ﷺ stood, and we stood with him. When he came he took the pickaxe, struck once and pronounced takbir. I heard a tremor the like of which I never heard, and he said: > **""Persia has been opened.""** Then he struck again and pronounced takbir. I heard a tremor the like of which I never heard, and he said: > **""Rome has been opened.""** Then he struck again and pronounced takbir. I heard a tremor the like of which I never heard, and he said: > **""Allah has brought Himyar as helpers and supporters.""**"
"10140","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dug the trench while he and his Companions had bound stones upon their bellies from hunger. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw that, he said: > **""Have you been guided to anyone who can feed us a meal?""** A man said: Yes. He said: > **""Then go forward and guide us to him.""** They went to a man who was in the trench working his share. His wife sent word: Come, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has come to us. The man came hurrying and said: May my father and mother be ransomed for you. He had a goat with her kid. He leapt toward it, and the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The kid is for those behind us.""** So he slaughtered the kid. His wife took her flour, kneaded it, baked it, the pot became ready, and she prepared tharid in her bowl. She brought it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions. The Prophet ﷺ placed his finger in it and said: > **""In the Name of Allah. O Allah, bless it. O Allah, bless it. Feed the people.""** They ate from it until they left satisfied, yet they had eaten only a third of it and two thirds remained. He dismissed those ten who were with him, saying that they should go and send others to be fed lunch. They went, and another ten came in their place and ate until full. Then he stood, supplicated for the lady of the house, praised her and her family, then they walked to the trench. He said: > **""Take us to Salman.""** And behold a rock before him that had defeated him. The Prophet ﷺ said to his Companions: > **""Let me be the first to strike it.""** He said: > **""In the Name of Allah.""** And struck it; a third of it split off. He said: > **""Allah is Greatest — the palaces of Rome, by the Lord of the Ka'ba.""** Then he struck again and a piece split off. He said: > **""Allah is Greatest — the palaces of Persia, by the Lord of the Ka'ba.""** At that the hypocrites said: We are digging a trench, yet he promises us the palaces of Persia and Rome."
"10141","Abu Huraira, may Allah be pleased with him, said: al-Harith came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: Halve the dates of Madina with us, or I shall fill it against you with horses and men. He said: > **""Until I consult the Sa'ds — Sa'd ibn 'Ubada and Sa'd ibn Mu'adh.""** Meaning he would consult them. They said: No, by Allah. We never accepted humiliation from ourselves in ignorance — how then, now that Allah has brought Islam? So he returned to al-Harith and informed him. He said: You have betrayed us, O Muhammad. Then Hassan said: O Harith, whoever among you betrays the covenant of his neighbor — Muhammad never betrays. If you betray, then betrayal is of your customs, and baseness grows in the roots of the sakhabar tree. And the trust of al-Nahdi, when I met it, was like glass whose crack is never mended. He said: So al-Harith said: Withhold the tongue of Hassan from us, O Muhammad — if it were mixed with seawater, it would taint it. In the wording of al-Bazzar and al-Tabarani: Abu Huraira said: al-Harith al-Ghatafani came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Muhammad, share the dates of Madina with us. He said: > **""Until I consult the Sa'ds.""** So he sent for Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, Sa'd ibn 'Ubada, Sa'd ibn al-Rabi', Sa'd ibn Khaythama, and Sa'd ibn Mas'ud, and said: > **""I know the Arabs have shot at you with a single bow. Al-Harith has asked you to share the dates of Madina with him. If you wish to pay him off this year, your affair thereafter remains yours.""** They said: O Messenger of Allah, is it revelation from heaven, so submission is to Allah's command? Or is it from your own opinion and desire, so our view follows your desire and opinion? If you only intend preserving us, then by Allah you have seen us and them as equals — they take not a single date from us except by purchase or hospitality. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Here you are — you hear what they say?""** They said: You have betrayed us, O Muhammad. Then Hassan ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Harith, whoever betrays the covenant of his neighbor — Muhammad never betrays. And the trust of al-Murri, when I met it, was like broken glass whose crack is never mended. If you betray, then betrayal is of your customs, and baseness grows in the roots of the sakhabar tree."
"10142","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said: On the day of the Trench the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say: > **""By Allah, were it not for Allah we would not be guided, nor would we fast, nor would we pray. So send down tranquility upon us.""**"
"10143","Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I never forgot his words on the day of the Trench while he handed them bricks, the hair of his chest covered with dust, as he said: > **""O Allah, indeed good is the good of the Hereafter, so forgive the Ansar and the Muhajira.""**"
"10144","Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, said: There was no fortress stronger than the fortress of Banu Haritha. The Prophet ﷺ placed the women, children, and offspring in it, and said: > **""If anyone comes upon you, ward him off with the sword.""** A man of Banu Tha'laba ibn Sa'd called Najdan, of Banu Hashshash, came on horseback until he was at the base of the fortress, then kept saying to the women: Come down to me; it is better for you. They brandished the sword; the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him, and a party hastened to the fortress, among them a man of Banu Haritha called Zuhayr ibn Rafi'. He said: > **""O Najdan, come out.""** So he came out to him. He charged with his horse, killed him, took his head, and brought it to the Prophet ﷺ."
"10145","Al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to the Trench and placed his wives and his aunt Safiyya in a fortress called Fari', with Hassan ibn Thabit among them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out toward Uhud. A Jew climbed until he looked down upon the wives of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his aunt. Safiyya said: O Hassan, stand to him and kill him. He said: By Allah, that is not in me — had it been in me, I would have gone out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Safiyya said: Then fasten the sword to my arm. Then she advanced to him until she killed him and cut off his head. She said to him: Take the head and throw it at the Jews. He said: That is not in me. So she herself took the head and threw it at the Jews. The Jews said: We knew Muhammad would never leave his family behind with no one among them. So they dispersed and left. 'A'isha said: Then Sa'd ibn Mu'adh passed by, saying: Wait a little — the camel will reach the battle. There is no harm in death when the appointed time comes. She said: I never saw anyone more handsome than him that day; upon him was a trace of yellow, a short coat of mail, and he had married and consummated before that, so upon him was a trace of saffron. He said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ charged the disbelievers, Hassan would open the fortress, and when they retreated he would return with them. In the abridgment of Abu Ya'la: When that was reported to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he struck a share for Safiyya as he struck for men."
"10146","'Urwa said: On the day of the Confederates the Prophet ﷺ lodged his wives in a fortress of Madina's fortresses. Hassan ibn Thabit was a cowardly man, so he lodged him with the women and locked the door. A Jew came and sat at the door of the fortress. Safiyya bint 'Abd al-Muttalib said: Go down, O Hassan, to this brute and kill him. He said: I would never risk myself for this brute. So she wrapped herself in a cloak, took a millstone, went down to him, and severed his head."
"10147","Mu'awiya ibn al-Hakam, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when my brother 'Ali ibn al-Hakam made his horse leap a trench. The horse struck him; the wall of the trench broke his leg. We brought him on his horse to the Prophet ﷺ. He wiped his leg, and he was healed before he dismounted from it. Mu'awiya ibn al-Hakam said in a poem of his: He made her leap toward me as she plunged — like a brimming bucket lowered by a rope — across the two trenches, but the plunge of a dark-eyed, dim-sighted one spilled him. Then he bound his leg and walked upon it like a falcon swooping on a dewy day. Then Muhammad, blessings upon him, King of mankind, said: > **""Rise for him and walk on evenly with it.""** And thereafter it was the soundest of legs. Muhammad ibn 'Ubada said: When a she-camel stumbles it is said to her: la'an laki — rise and stand. Al-A'sha said: With a weak one we hamstrung when she stumbled — a swift death was nearer to her than saying: rise."
"10148","'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Bakr ibn Malik ibn Wahb al-Khuza'i, from his father, from his grandfather, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Salit and Sufyan ibn 'Awf al-Aslami as scouts on the day of the Confederates. They went out until at al-Bayda horsemen of Abu Sufyan surrounded them. They fought until both were killed. They were brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and buried in a single grave — they are the two neighboring martyrs."
"10149","Nafi' said: It was said to Ibn 'Umar: Where did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray on the day of the Confederates? He said: He used to pray in the belly of the pass beside a ruin there. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had given the people permission to leave, then ordered me to call them back, so I called them."
"10150","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: My maternal uncle 'Uthman ibn Maz'un sent me to his father with a quilt. I came to the Prophet ﷺ and sought permission while he was at the Trench. He permitted me and said: > **""Whomever you meet, tell them: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ orders you to return.""** That was in severe cold. I went out, met the people, and said to them: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ orders you to return. He said: By Allah, not two of them, nor one, turned away from me in refusal."
"10151","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was concealed on the day of the Trench except from six men: four of the Muhajirun — Talha, al-Zubayr, 'Ali, and Sa'd — and of the Ansar: Abu Dujana and al-Harith ibn al-Simma."
"10152","'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at the Trench. He would tend a breach in the mountain that he feared, come, recline in my lap, then rise and listen. He heard the sound of a man in iron moving into the mountain. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Who is this?""** He said: I am Sa'd. I came so that you may command me with your order. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered him to spend the night at that breach. 'A'isha said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ slept in my lap until I heard his snoring. 'A'isha said: I never forget it in favor of Sa'd."
"10153","Sa'd — meaning Ibn Abi Waqqas — may Allah be pleased with him, said: On the day of the Trench a man shielding himself with a buckler kept moving it thus — placing it above his nose, then lowering it down. So I reached for my quiver, took out a blood-stained arrow, placed it in the middle of the bow, and when he did thus — lowering the shield — I shot. I never forgot the striking of the arrowhead upon such-and-such part of the shield. He said: He fell and moved his leg thus, and the Prophet of Allah ﷺ laughed — I think he said: until his molars showed. He said: I said: Why did he do that? He said: Because of what the man did. In the wording of al-Bazzar: There was a man with two shields, and Sa'd was an archer. He kept moving the two shields thus, covering his forehead. Sa'd drew an arrow for him; when he raised his head he shot him and did not miss this part of him — meaning his forehead. The rest is similar."
"10154","Hudhayfa, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The people dispersed from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the night of the Confederates, and only twelve men remained with him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me while I was crouching from sleep, and said: > **""O son of al-Yaman, rise and go to the camp of the Confederates and observe their condition.""** I said: O Messenger of Allah, by the One who sent you with the truth, I only rose for you out of modesty despite the cold. He said: > **""Go, O son of al-Yaman — no harm of cold or heat shall touch you until you return to me.""** So I went until I reached their camp and found Abu Sufyan kindling a fire among a small band around him; the Confederates had dispersed from him. I went until I sat among them. Abu Sufyan sensed that one not of them had entered among them, so he said: Let each man among you take the hand of his companion. So I struck my hand upon the one on my right and took his hand, then struck my hand upon the one on my left and took his hand. I remained among them a while, then rose and came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was standing in prayer. He motioned to me to draw near, so I drew near until he cast over me part of the garment upon him to warm me. When he finished his prayer he said: > **""O son of al-Yaman, sit. What is the news of the people?""** So I said: O Messenger of Allah, the people have dispersed from Abu Sufyan; none remains but a small band kindling a fire. Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has poured upon them cold like that He poured upon us — but we hope from Allah what they do not hope for."
"10155","'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I went out on the day of the Trench following the tracks of the people, and heard the thud of the earth behind me — the tread of the ground. Behold, it was Sa'd ibn Mu'adh with his nephew al-Harith ibn Aws carrying his shield. She said: I sat down to the ground. Sa'd passed wearing an iron coat of mail from which his limbs protruded, and I feared for Sa'd's limbs. She said: Sa'd was among the greatest and tallest of people. She said: He passed chanting: Wait a little — the camel will reach the battle. How excellent is death when the appointed time comes. She said: I rose and plunged into a garden; in it were a group of Muslims, among them 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, and among them a man wearing a head covering — the helmet. 'Umar said: What brought you? By my life, indeed you are bold — what assures you against calamity or shifting ground? She said: He kept blaming me until I wished the earth had split open for me that very hour and I had entered it. He said: The man lifted the covering from his face — behold, it was Talha ibn 'Ubaydullah. He said: Woe to you, O 'Umar, you have said much today — where is shifting ground or flight except to Allah, Exalted? She said: A man of the polytheists of Quraysh called Ibn al-'Ariqa shot Sa'd with an arrow of his, saying: Take it — I am Ibn al-'Ariqa. It struck his median vein and severed it. So Sa'd supplicated to Allah and said: > **""O Allah, do not cause me to die until You cool my eye with Banu Qurayza.""** She said: They had been his allies and clients in ignorance. She said: Then his wound subsided. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent the wind upon the polytheists, and Allah sufficed the believers in fighting — and Allah is Strong, Mighty. Abu Sufyan and those with him joined Tihama, 'Uyayna ibn Badr and those with him joined Najd, and Banu Qurayza returned and fortified themselves in their fortresses. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned to Madina and ordered a tent of leather pitched over Sa'd in the mosque. She said: Then Jibril, peace be upon him, came to him, dust upon his front teeth, and said: > **""You have laid down your weapons! No, by Allah, the angels have not yet laid down their weapons. Go out to Banu Qurayza and fight them.""** He said: So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ donned his armor and announced to the people the departure. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out and passed by Banu Ghanm, neighbors of the mosque, and said: > **""Who passed by you?""** They said: Dihya al-Kalbi passed by us — and Dihya's beard, age, and face resembled Jibril, peace be upon him. She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them and besieged them twenty-five nights. When the siege and trial grew severe, it was said to them: Come down to the judgment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They consulted Abu Lubaba ibn 'Abd al-Mundhir, and he signaled to them that it meant slaughter. So they said: We shall come down to the judgment of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Come down to the judgment of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh.""** So they came down. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for Sa'd ibn Mu'adh; he was brought on a donkey with a saddle of palm fiber, carried upon it, surrounded by his people. They said to him: O Abu 'Amr, your allies, clients, people of valor, and those you know. He returned nothing to them and paid them no attention until, when he neared their dwellings, he turned to his people and said: It is time that no blame of a blamer seizes me concerning Allah."
"10156","'Urwa — meaning Ibn al-Zubayr — said: Sa'd ibn Mu'adh was shot on the day of the Trench with a shot that severed the median vein of his upper arm. They claim Hibban ibn Qays of Banu 'Amir ibn Lu'ayy of Banu al-'Ariqa shot him; others said Abu Usama al-Jushami shot him. So Sa'd ibn Mu'adh said: > **""My Lord, heal me regarding Banu Qurayza before death.""** So the wound subsided after bursting. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained over Banu Qurayza until they asked him to place an arbitrator between him and them, upon whose judgment they would come down. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Choose whomever of my Companions you wish; let his word be heard.""** So they chose Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was pleased with him, and they submitted. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered their weapons placed in a house, ordered them bound and tied, and placed them in the house of Usama ibn Zayd. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for Sa'd ibn Mu'adh; he came on a Bedouin donkey — they claim the padding of its saddle was of fiber — followed by a man of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal walking with him, magnifying the right of Banu Qurayza, reminding him of their alliance and what they did on the day of Bu'ath, that they chose him over all others hoping for his pardon and tenderness toward them: spare them, for they are your beauty and number. The man said much, but Sa'd returned nothing to him until they drew near. The man said to him: Will you not return anything to me? He said: > **""By Allah, I do not care concerning Allah for the blame of any blamer.""** So the man left him and went to his people, despairing that he would spare them, and informed them of what he had said to him and what Sa'd had answered. Sa'd went on until he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: > **""O Sa'd, judge between us and them.""** So Sa'd said: I judge concerning them that their fighters be killed, their captives divided, their wealth taken, and their offspring and women enslaved. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Sa'd has judged concerning them with the judgment of Allah.""** Some people claim they came down to the judgment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he referred judgment concerning them to Sa'd ibn Mu'adh. They were brought out group by group and their necks were struck. Huyayy ibn Akhtab was brought out, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Has Allah disgraced you?""** He said: You have prevailed over me, and I do not blame myself concerning you. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered concerning him; he was taken out to the stones of oil in the market and his neck was struck — all of it before the eyes of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh. They claim Sa'd's wound had healed and scabbed with dust. Then he supplicated, saying: > **""O Allah, Lord of the heavens and the earth, no people were more hateful to me than a people who denied Your Messenger and expelled him. I think the war between us and them has been laid down. If any fighting between us and them remains, then keep me alive to fight them for You. And if You have laid down the war between us and them, then cause this place to burst open and make my death in it.""** So Allah, Blessed and Exalted, caused it to burst. He lay sleeping in the depth of night and they knew not until he died — the wound never subsiding until he died."
"10157","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the day of the Confederates, when they had gathered great hosts against him: > **""After this, they will never raid you — rather, you will raid them.""**"
"10158","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The east wind came to the north wind on the night of the Confederates and said: Come, that we may aid the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The north wind said: The free woman does not travel by night. So the wind by which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was aided was the east wind."
"10159","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, may Allah be pleased with him, shot at Qurayza and al-Nadir and his median vein was cut. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cauterized him, so it festered and reopened. He cauterized him a second time. So Sa'd said: > **""O Allah, do not take my soul until You cool my eye with Banu Qurayza and al-Nadir.""**"
"10160","Muhammad ibn Maslama, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rendered judgment concerning Banu Qurayza, the Aws felt distress at that. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to every house of Aws two captives each, and sent to Banu Haritha two captives."
"10161","Hudhayfa, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""They distracted us from the afternoon prayer — and he had not prayed it that day until the sun set — may Allah fill their graves with fire.""** Or he said: their hearts with fire. Or he said: their houses with fire."
"10162","Al-Bara ibn 'Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Abu Sufyan passed with Mu'awiya behind him — a conspicuous man — so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Allah, deal with the rider of the humps.""**"
"10163","Ka'b ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from pursuing the Confederates and alighted at Madina, he laid down his armor, washed, and used stones for cleaning."
"10164","Ka'b ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from pursuing the Confederates, he took off his armor, washed, and used stones for cleaning. Duhaym added in his hadith: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Jibril, peace be upon him, descended and said: May you be excused from a warrior — do I see you have laid down your armor, yet we have not laid ours down?""** So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ leapt up alarmed and charged the people not to pray Asr except at Banu Qurayza. They donned weapons and went out, but did not reach Banu Qurayza until the sun set. The people disputed over the Asr prayer. Some said: Pray — the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not intend that you abandon prayer. Others said: He charged us not to pray until we reach Banu Qurayza; we are under the charge of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so there is no sin upon us. So one party prayed Asr in faith seeking reward, and one party did not pray until they reached Banu Qurayza after sunset, then prayed it in faith seeking reward. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ blamed neither of the two parties."
"10165","'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard the voice of a man, leapt up sharply, and went out to him. I followed him — behold, he was a reclining man, turbaned, his turban hanging loose between his shoulders. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered I said: You leapt up and went out — yet he was Dihya al-Kalbi. He said: > **""Did you see him?""** I said: Yes. He said: > **""That was Jibril, peace be upon him — he ordered me to go out to Banu Qurayza.""**"
"10166","Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went early to Banu Qurayza upon a bare donkey called Ya'fur."
"10167","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to Banu Qurayza upon a donkey, with Jibril, peace be upon him, upon a white mule bearing a carpet of brocade whose pile was pearls. He said: > **""O Muhammad, by the One who sent you with the truth, I shall not dismount from it until it is opened for you — and I shall crush them as an egg is crushed upon smooth rock.""** Ibn 'Abbas said: He did not return until it was opened for him."
"10168","Aslam al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed me over the captives of Qurayza. I would look at the private part of a boy: if I saw pubic hair had grown, I struck his neck; if I saw it had not grown, I placed him among the spoils of the Muslims."
"10169","'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Al-Zubayr was a blind man. Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Al-Zubayr favored me on the day of Bu'ath and freed me — grant him to me that I may repay him. He said: > **""He is yours.""** He said to al-Zubayr: Do you know me? He said: Yes — you are Thabit ibn Qays. He said: I shall favor you as you favored me on the day of Bu'ath. He said: Will you benefit me? Where is my family? So he returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: Grant me his family. So he granted him his family. He came to him and informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had returned his family to him. He said: O son of my brother, what good is it to live on as bodies? Where is the wealth? So he returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, grant me his wealth. He said: > **""And his wealth is yours.""** So he returned to him and said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has returned your wealth to you — Allah, Exalted, intended good for you. He said: O son of my brother, what became of Huyayy ibn Akhtab, chief of settler and nomad? He said: He was killed. He said: O son of my brother, what became of Zayd ibn Rawta, protector of the Jews? He said: He was killed. He said: What became of Ka'b ibn Ashta, at whose girth the virgins of the quarter would pinch? He said: He was killed. He said: What became of the two Muhammasans? He said: They are as yesterday gone. He said: Then between me and meeting the beloved ones is only as the emptying of a bucket. I ask you, by my hand upon you, to join me with the company. So he killed him."
"10170","Ibn Shihab said: Martyred on the day of the Trench, of the Ansar: Anas ibn Mu'adh ibn Aws ibn 'Abd 'Amr. And of the Ansar, then of Banu Salima: Tha'laba ibn 'Anama. The hadith of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh and the two neighboring ones already preceded."
"10171","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: The Trench was in Shawwal, year five. In it Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, may Allah be pleased with him, died."
"10172","Sinan ibn Wabra, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the expedition of al-Muraysi' — the expedition of Banu al-Mustaliq — and their watchword was: > **""O Mansur — kill, kill.""**"
"10173","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: 'Asim ibn 'Umar ibn Qatada, 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, and Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban each narrated to me part of the hadith of Banu al-Mustaliq. He said: It reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Banu al-Mustaliq were gathering against him, led by al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar, father of Juwayriya bint al-Harith, wife of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard of them, he went out to them until he met them at a water of theirs called al-Muraysi', toward Qudayd by the coast. The people advanced and fought. Allah routed Banu al-Mustaliq; al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar, father of Juwayriya, was killed, those of them killed were killed, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed their sons and women as spoils. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took many captives from them that day and divided them among the Muslims. Among the women taken that day was Juwayriya bint Abi Dirar, the leading lady of her people."
"10174","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: The expedition of Banu al-Mustaliq was in Sha'ban, year six. He went out in that expedition with 'A'isha with him — he cast lots among his wives and her lot came out. In that expedition the people of the slander said what they said, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down her vindication."
"10175","Shabab al-'Usfuri, may Allah be pleased with him, said: In the sixth year of the Hijra was the expedition of Banu al-Mustaliq. In this expedition the people of the slander said what they said, and concerning it the Qur'an came down: Indeed, those who brought the slander are a band among you — the verse."
"10176","Salama ibn al-Akwa', may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'Uyayna ibn Hisn ibn Hudhayfa raided the milch camels of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the morning and drove them off. Salama said: I went out with my bow and arrows — I used to hunt — until at Thaniyyat al-Wada' I saw them driving them. I hastened among the horses at Sila', then shouted: O morning raid! My cry reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and it was announced among the people: Alarm, alarm! I went out shooting at them, saying: > **""Take it — I am Ibn al-Akwa'.""** It was not long before I saw the horsemen of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ threading through the trees — eight horsemen caught up with them. The first to reach them was Abu Qatada ibn Rib'i. He stabbed a man of Banu Fazara called Sa'd, stripped his cloak, and wrapped him in it. Then he went on in pursuit of the enemy with the horsemen. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed while the people were alarmed, saying: Abu Qatada is slain. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It is not Abu Qatada — rather, it is the slain of Abu Qatada. Leave him and his spoils. Press on in pursuit of the people.""** So they pressed on and recovered what they recovered of the milch camels; the rest they carried off. Muhammad ibn Talha said: In the hadith he named those who went out seeking the camels: 'Ukkasha ibn Mihsan, al-Miqdad ibn 'Amr — called Ibn al-Aswad, ally of Banu Zuhra — and Mahraz ibn Nadla al-Asadi, ally of Banu 'Abd Shams; it is said none of the enemy was killed but him. And of the Ansar: Sa'd ibn Zayd al-Ashhali, their leader, 'Abbad ibn Bishr al-Ashhali, Zuhayr ibn 'Amr al-Harithi, Abu Qatada ibn Rib'i, and Mu'adh ibn Ma'is al-Zuraqi. Abu 'Ayyash al-Zuraqi was one of the five. He said: I came forward on a horse of mine, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Abu 'Ayyash, if only you gave this horse to one more skilled at horsemanship than you.""** He said: I said: I am the most skilled horseman of the Arabs. The horse had not gone fifty cubits before it threw me and broke my leg. So I said: Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth. Then I mounted my cousin Mu'adh ibn Ma'is al-Zuraqi upon my horse."
"10177","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until at 'Usfan the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to us: > **""The eyes of the polytheists are now upon Dajnan. Which of you knows the road of Dhat al-Hanzal?""** When evening came the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Is there a man to dismount and run before the mounts?""** A man said: I, O Messenger of Allah. So I dismounted; stones struck his feet and trees caught his clothes. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Mount.""** Then another dismounted; stones struck him and trees caught his clothes. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Mount.""** Then we found the road and marched through a pass called al-Hanzal. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The likeness of this pass is but the likeness of the gate through which the Children of Israel entered — Enter the gate prostrating and say: Hittah; We shall forgive you your sins — none passes this pass tonight but that he is forgiven.""** So the people hastened and passed through, the last to pass being Qatada ibn al-Nu'man at the rear of the people. He said: The people mounted one another in turn until we caught up together. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ alighted, and we alighted."
"10178","Jundub ibn Najiya — or Najiya ibn Jundub — may Allah be pleased with him, said: When we were at al-Ghamim, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ received news of Quraysh: they had sent Khalid ibn al-Walid with a squadron of cavalry to meet him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ disliked meeting them, and was merciful toward them. So he said: > **""Is there a man to turn us aside from the road?""** I said: I, may my father be for you. So I took them by a road of rugged hollows and defiles until the land leveled with us and he alighted them at al-Hudaybiyya — a well nearly dry. He cast an arrow or two from his quiver, spat into it, then supplicated, and springs gushed forth — I say, or we say: had we wished, we could have scooped with our hands."
"10179","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: The one who descended into the well with the arrow of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of al-Hudaybiyya was Najiya ibn Jundub ibn 'Umayr ibn Ma'mar ibn Hazim ibn 'Amr ibn Wathila ibn Sahm ibn Mazin ibn Salaman ibn Aslam ibn Afsa ibn Haritha — driver of the sacrificial camels of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"10180","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: On the day of al-Hudaybiyah the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade lighting fires at night for concealment. Afterwards he permitted it, saying none after them would attain their measure in virtue and precedence. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Do not light a fire at night.""** He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) later said: > **""Light fires and prepare what you need, for no people after you will reach your sa' nor your mudd.""**"
"10181","Yazid ibn Malik, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), reported: He was present with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of the Tree and on the day when the sacrificial animals were detained before reaching their place. A man from the polytheists said, ""O Muhammad, what moves you to bring these people against us when we dislike it?"" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""These are better than you and your forefathers. They believe in Allah and the Last Day. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, Allah is indeed pleased with them.""**"
"10182","'Abdullah ibn Mughaffal al-Muzani (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Hudaybiyah beneath the tree mentioned in the Qur'an. Branches fell upon the back of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah honor him) and Suhayl ibn 'Amr sat before him for the treaty. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Write: In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate.""** Suhayl seized his hand and said, ""We do not know al-Rahman al-Rahim. Write as we know."" So he said: > **""Write: In Thy Name, O Allah.""** Then he dictated, ""This is what Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, has made peace upon with the people of Makkah."" Suhayl seized his hand and said, ""If you were His Messenger we would not have wronged you. Write as we know."" So he said: > **""Write: This is what Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib has made peace upon, and I am the Messenger of Allah.""** While thus engaged, thirty armed youths came upon them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated against them, Allah took away their sight, and the Companions seized them. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Did you come under the covenant of anyone? Did anyone grant you safe-conduct?""** They said, ""No."" So he released them. Then Allah revealed: ""And He it is Who withheld their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah, after He had made you victorious over them, and Allah sees what you do"" [al-Fath 24]."
"10183","'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Suspect personal opinion against the religion. Then he recalled al-Hudaybiyah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was writing between himself and the people of Makkah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Write: In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate.""** They said, ""If we regarded that as true we would have believed. But write as you used to write: In Thy Name, O Allah."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted it, but I refused, until he said to me: > **""O 'Umar, do you see that I am satisfied while you refuse?""** 'Umar said, ""So I became satisfied."""
"10184","'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: On the day of al-Hudaybiyah the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the people to the pledge. Abu Sinan ibn Mihsan stood and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I pledge to you upon what is in your heart."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""And what is in your heart?""** He said, ""That I strike with my sword before you until Allah grants you victory or I am killed."" So he pledged to him upon that, and the people pledged following the pledge of Abu Sinan."
"10185","'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah reported: I asked Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), ""Did you witness the Pledge of Ridwan with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"" He said, ""Yes."" I asked, ""What was he wearing?"" He said, ""A cotton shirt, a stuffed cloak, a mantle, and a sword. And I saw al-Nu'man ibn Muqrin al-Muzani standing at his head, lifting the branches of the tree from above his head while the people pledged to him."""
"10186","'Abdullah ibn Mughaffal (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I am one of the group whom Allah, Exalted is His praise, mentioned: ""I find not whereon to mount you"" [al-Tawbah 92]. I was holding some branches of the tree under which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took the pledge from the people, shading him. We pledged to him that we would not flee."
"10187","'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: In the year of al-Hudaybiyah, when 'Uthman informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that Suhayl had been sent to him by his people to make peace that he return from them that year and they would leave it for him the next year for three days, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Suhayl - the matter is made easy for you.""**"
"10188","Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: The truce between the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people of Makkah at al-Hudaybiyah was for four years."
"10189","Ibn Shihab reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered 'Umrat al-Qada', he commanded his Companions: > **""Uncover your shoulders and hasten in the circumambulation.""** He intended that the polytheists see their strength and vigor. The men, women, and children of Makkah gathered to look at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions while they circumambulated the House. 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah walked before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his sword slung over him, reciting: ""Clear the way, O sons of disbelievers, from his path. I bear witness that he is His Messenger. The Most Merciful has sent down in His revelation, in scriptures recited upon His Messenger: today we shall strike you for its interpretation as we struck you for its revelation, a blow that removes heads from their resting place and makes the beloved forget his beloved."" Some nobles of the polytheists absented themselves, unwilling out of rage, spite, and envy to look at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and went to the outskirts of Makkah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed his rites and remained three days."
"10190","Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent 'Amr ibn al-Tufayl to Khaybar to seek reinforcements from his people. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""O 'Amr, go and seek reinforcements for us from your people.""** 'Amr said, ""O Messenger of Allah, you send me when fighting has already broken out?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Are you not pleased to be the messenger of the Messenger of Allah?""**"
"10191","Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Prepare yourselves for this town whose people are unjust - meaning Khaybar - for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will conquer it for you, if Allah wills. Let no one with a refractory mount or one who is weak go out with me.""** Abu Hurayrah went to his mother and said, ""Equip me, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has ordered us to prepare for the expedition."" She said, ""You go, though you know I admit none to help me except you with me?"" He said, ""I would never stay behind from the Messenger of Allah."" She bared her breast and adjured him by the milk he had suckled. Then she went secretly to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Go, you have been sufficed.""** Later Abu Hurayrah came and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away from him. He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I see you turn away from me only for something that reached you."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""You are the one whom your mother adjured, baring her breast and adjuring you by what you suckled of her milk. Does one of you think that when he is with his parents, or one of them, that he is not in the path of Allah? Rather he is in the path of Allah when he treats them kindly and fulfills their right.""** Abu Hurayrah said, ""I remained years after this without raiding until she died."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out from Madinah. A youth of Banu 'Amir on a refractory young camel rode apart from the road. His camel fell into a hollow, he cried out, ""O family of 'Amir,"" and he and his camel trembled. His people came and carried him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached Khaybar he called al-Tufayl ibn al-Harith al-Khuza'i and said: > **""Go to your people and seek their aid against this town whose people are unjust, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will conquer it for you, if Allah wills.""** Al-Tufayl said, ""O Messenger of Allah, you distance me from you. By Allah, to die while near you is dearer to me than life while far from you."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""What must be must be, so go.""** He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, perhaps I shall not meet you again, so give me provision to live by."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Do you control your tongue?""** He said, ""What do I control if I do not control my tongue?"" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Do you control your hand?""** He said, ""What do I control if I do not control my hand?"" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Say nothing with your tongue except what is good, and stretch your hand only toward good. Spread peace, offer food, be shy before Allah as you are shy before a revered man of your kin. Let your character be good. When you do evil, do good after it, for good deeds remove evil deeds.""**"
"10192","Husayl ibn Kharijah al-Ashja'i (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I came to Madinah with merchandise to sell. I was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""I will give you twenty sa' of dates if you guide my Companions to the road to Khaybar.""** I did so. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached Khaybar and conquered it, I came and he gave me the twenty. Then I embraced Islam."
"10193","Nasr ibn Dahr al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: He heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say on the march to Khaybar to 'Amir ibn al-Akwa', the uncle of Salamah ibn 'Amr ibn al-Akwa' - al-Akwa' being Sinan: > **""Dismount, O son of al-Akwa', and recite for us some of your verses.""** He dismounted and recited for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""By Allah, were it not for Allah we would not have been guided, nor given charity, nor prayed. When a people transgress against us, and desire discord, we refuse. So send down tranquility upon us, and make firm our feet when we meet."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""May Allah have mercy on you.""** 'Umar said, ""It has become due, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah. Would that you had let us benefit from him."" He was martyred on the day of Khaybar."
"10194","Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Khaybar in the morning while they had taken their spades and baskets and gone out to their fields and lands. When they saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the army, they turned back fleeing. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest. Khaybar is ruined. When we descend upon the courtyard of a people, evil is the morning of those who were warned.""**"
"10195","Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), my knee touching his knee. He kept silent about them until near dawn, when those with livestock went to their livestock and those with crops to their crops. Then he raided them and said: > **""When we descend upon the courtyard of a people, evil is the morning of those who were warned.""**"
"10196","'Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided Khaybar while they were going out in the morning. They said, ""Muhammad and the khamis (army)."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Allah is Greatest. Khaybar is ruined. When we descend upon the courtyard of a people, evil is the morning of those who were warned.""**"
"10197","Abu al-Yusr Ka'b ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: By Allah, I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Khaybar in the evening when sheep belonging to a Jewish man came toward their fortress while we were besieging them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Who will feed us from these sheep?""** Abu al-Yusr said, ""I will, O Messenger of Allah."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Then do so.""** So I ran like an ostrich. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me departing he said: > **""O Allah, grant us enjoyment through him.""** I reached the sheep after their front had entered the fortress. I seized two sheep from the rear, clasped them under my arms, and ran as if carrying nothing, until I laid them before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They slaughtered them and ate. Abu al-Yusr was among the last of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to die. When he narrated this hadith he wept and said, ""Enjoy me while you may, for by my life I am the last of them."""
"10198","Salamah ibn al-Akwa' (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: His uncle struck a man from the polytheists, killed him, and wounded himself. He began saying, ""I have killed myself."" That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""For him are two rewards.""**"
"10199","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: Marhab the Jew came out of their fortress with his weapons gathered, reciting rajaz: ""Khaybar knows that I am Marhab, armed, a tried champion. At times I thrust and at times I strike, when lions advance raging."" He called, ""Who will duel?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Who is for him?""** Muhammad ibn Maslamah said, ""I am for him, O Messenger of Allah, the wronged avenger. They killed my brother yesterday."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Then stand to him. O Allah, aid him against him.""** When the two drew near, a tree of 'ushr came between them. Each sought shelter by it from the other, cutting with his sword what was before him, until each stood exposed to his fellow like an upright man with no branches. Marhab charged Muhammad and struck; he shielded himself with his buckler, the sword lodged in it, he held it fast, and Muhammad ibn Maslamah struck him until he killed him."
"10200","Buraydah al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) encamped before the people of Khaybar, he gave the banner to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab. Those Muslims who rose went and met the people of Khaybar, then returned. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Tomorrow I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and whom Allah and His Messenger love.""** Next day he called 'Ali while his eyes were inflamed. He spat in his eyes, gave him the banner, and the people rose with him and met the people of Khaybar. Marhab recited before them: ""Khaybar knows that I am Marhab, armed, a tried champion. At times I thrust and at times I strike, when lions advance raging."" They exchanged two blows. 'Ali struck him on his head until the sword bit to his molars. The camp heard the sound of his blow. The last men had not yet joined 'Ali before victory was granted to him and them."
"10201","Buraydah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: We besieged Khaybar. Abu Bakr took the banner, returned, and no victory was granted him. Next day 'Umar took it, went out, returned, and no victory was granted him. The people suffered hardship and strain that day. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""I shall give the banner tomorrow to a man whom Allah and His Messenger love, and who loves Allah and His Messenger. He will not return until victory is granted him.""** We spent the night hopeful that victory would come morrow. In the morning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the dawn prayer, stood, called for the banner while the people stood in ranks, called 'Ali while his eyes were inflamed, spat in his eyes, handed him the banner, and victory was granted him. Buraydah said, ""I was among those who craned for it."""
"10202","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took the standard, shook it, then said: > **""Who will take it by its right?""** A certain man came and said, ""I."" He said: > **""Turn away.""** Then another man came. He said: > **""Turn away.""** Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""By Him Who honored the face of Muhammad, I shall give it to a man who does not flee. Come, O 'Ali.""** He went until Allah granted him victory over Khaybar and Fadak, and he brought their 'ajwah dates and dried meat."
"10203","'Ali (peace be upon him) reported: We came to Khaybar. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached them, he sent 'Umar with the people. They soon fled before 'Umar and his companions, defeated. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""I shall send against them a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and whom Allah and His Messenger love. He will fight them until Allah grants him victory.""** The people craned their necks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) waited a while, then said: > **""Where is 'Ali?""** They said, ""His eyes are inflamed."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Call him to me.""** When I came, he opened my eyes, spat in them, and gave me the banner. I went until I reached them. Marhab was reciting rajaz until we met. Allah routed him, his companions fled and fortified themselves, locking the gate. We came to the gate and I kept working it until Allah opened it."
"10204","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: On the day of Khaybar the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man who showed cowardice. Muhammad ibn Maslamah came and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I have never seen the like of today. Mahmud ibn Maslamah is killed."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Do not wish to meet the enemy. Ask Allah for well-being, for you know not what you will be tried with from them. When you meet them say: O Allah, You are our Lord and their Lord, our forelocks and their forelocks are in Your Hand, and it is You alone Who slay them. Then keep to the ground sitting. When they overwhelm you, rise and magnify Allah.""** Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Tomorrow I shall send a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and they both love him. He will not turn his back.""** Next day he sent 'Ali, who suffered severe ophthalmia. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Go.""** He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I cannot see where to place my foot."" He spat in his eyes, tied the banner for him, and handed him the standard. 'Ali said, ""Upon what shall I fight them, O Messenger of Allah?"" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Upon their testifying that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah. If they do that, they have protected their blood and wealth except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah, Exalted is He.""**"
"10205","'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When I killed Marhab I brought his head to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"10206","Abu Rafi', freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), reported: We went out with 'Ali when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him with his standard. When he neared the fortress its people came out and fought him. A Jewish man struck him and knocked his shield from his hand. 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) took a door by the fortress and shielded himself with it. It remained in his hand while he fought until Allah granted him victory. Then he cast it down when finished. I saw myself with seven men, I the eighth, striving to overturn that door and we could not overturn it."
"10207","Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), who was in the expedition of Khaybar, said: I heard the clash of the sword upon the teeth of Marhab."
"10208","Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made peace with the people of Khaybar over all gold and silver and everything except their lives and offspring. Al-Rabi' and Kinanah, sons of Abu al-Huqayq, were brought, one of them newly wed to Safiyyah bint Huyayy. When they were brought he said: > **""Where are your vessels that were lent in Madinah?""** They said, ""You expelled and exiled us, so we spent them."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Consider what you say, for if you conceal from me I shall thereby make lawful your blood and your offspring.""** He called a man of the Ansar and said: > **""Go to such and such place, look at a palm with a rag on its top, remove the rag, take out those vessels, and bring them.""** He went and brought them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had them executed. Their offspring were brought, among them Safiyyah bint Huyayy as a bride. He ordered Bilal, who took her to the dwelling of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Bilal passed with her by her husband and brother, both slain. When he returned the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Glory be to Allah! What did you intend, O Bilal, toward a young maiden, passing her by two slain men and showing them to her? Have you no mercy?""** He said, ""I wished to burn her heart."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and spent the night with her. Abu Ayyub came with his sword and sat beside the tent, saying, ""If I hear a cry or suspect anything, I shall be near the Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out at Bilal's call to prayer and said: > **""Who is this?""** He said, ""I am Abu Ayyub."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""What is the matter with you here at this hour?""** He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, you entered with a young maiden whose husband and brother you have killed, so I feared for you and stayed near."" He said: > **""May Allah have mercy on you, O Abu Ayyub.""** Three times. The people spoke much about her, one saying his concubine, another his wife. At departure they said, ""Look: if he veils her she is his wife, if not she is his concubine."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought her out veiled. He placed his knee for her, she placed her knee upon his thigh and mounted. He had earlier offered her to take her as concubine or free and marry her. She said, ""Rather free me and marry me."" So he did, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"10209","'Urwah reported: When Allah, Mighty and Majestic, granted Khaybar to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and those of them were killed who were killed, Zaynab bint al-Harith the Jewess, niece of Marhab, gifted a roasted sheep, poisoning it, putting most in the shoulder and arm, having been told those were the parts of the sheep dearest to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered with Bishr ibn al-Bara' ibn al-Ma'rur, brother of Banu Salamah, it was presented. He took the shoulder and arm and bit from it; Bishr took another bone and bit from it. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) swallowed his mouthful, Bishr swallowed his. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Lift your hands, for the shoulder of the sheep informs me that treachery has been done in it.""** Bishr ibn al-Bara' said, ""By Him Who honored you, I felt that in the bite I ate, but only my dislike of spoiling your meal prevented me from spitting it. When you swallowed what was in your mouth, I did not wish to be sparing of myself apart from you, and I hoped you had not swallowed it with treachery in it."" Bishr did not rise from his place before his color turned like dark veils, his pain lingered until he could not move except as moved. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained after three years until his illness in which he died."
"10210","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Khaybar, al-Hajjaj ibn 'Ilat said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I have wealth and family in Makkah, and I wish to go to them. Am I free if I disparage you or say something?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him to say what he wished. He came to his wife and said, ""Gather what you have, for I wish to buy from the spoils of Muhammad and his Companions, for they have been plundered and their wealth seized."" The report spread in Makkah. The Muslims were downcast; the polytheists showed joy. The news reached al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib, who was stricken and could not stand. He took his son Qutham, lay down, placed him on his chest, saying, ""My beloved Qutham, like the high-nosed one, the prophet of blessings, despite the spiteful."" He sent his slave to al-Hajjaj: ""Woe to you, what have you brought, what do you say? What Allah has promised is better than what you brought."" Al-Hajjaj told his slave, ""Convey peace to Abu al-Fadl, and tell him to empty a house for me to visit him, for the news is as he will love."" The slave came to the door saying, ""Glad tidings, Abu al-Fadl."" Al-'Abbas leapt joyfully and kissed between his eyes, then freed him. Al-Hajjaj came and told him the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had conquered Khaybar, spoils were allotted, and he had chosen Safiyyah bint Huyayy for himself, offering her freedom and marriage or return to her kin, and she chose freedom and marriage. ""But I came for wealth of mine here to collect. I sought permission of the Messenger of Allah and he permitted me to say what I wished. Conceal me for three days, then mention as you will."" His wife gathered her jewelry and goods and gave them to him, and he departed. After three days al-'Abbas visited the wife of al-Hajjaj and asked about her husband. She said he left on such a day, adding, ""May Allah not disgrace you, Abu al-Fadl, we were grieved by what reached you."" He said, ""Indeed may Allah not disgrace me. By praise of Allah it was only what we love. Allah granted Khaybar to His Messenger, the shares were cast, he chose Safiyyah for himself. If you need your husband, join him."" She said, ""I think by Allah you speak truth."" He said, ""I do, and the matter is as I told you."" Then he went to the gatherings of Quraysh, who said as he passed, ""Nothing but good befalls you, Abu al-Fadl."" He said, ""Nothing but good befell me, by praise of Allah, Blessed and Exalted. Al-Hajjaj told me Allah granted Khaybar to His Messenger, the shares were cast, he chose Safiyyah for himself. He asked me to conceal him three days. He came only to take his wealth here, then go."" So Allah turned the gloom of the Muslims upon the polytheists. The Muslims, even those secluded in gloom, came to al-'Abbas, he told them the news, they rejoiced, and Allah returned gloom, rage, and grief upon the polytheists."
"10211","'Urwah reported: There were killed on the day of Khaybar, of Quraysh then of Banu 'Abd Manaf: Thaqif ibn 'Amr, an ally of Banu Asad ibn Khuzaymah. Of the Ansar, of Banu Zurayq: Mas'ud ibn Sa'd ibn Khalid; of Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf: Abu al-Sayyah, or Abu Dayyah."
"10212","Ibn Shihab, naming those martyred on the day of Khaybar with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), reported: Of the Ansar, of Banu Harithah: Mahmud ibn Maslamah. They mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Muhammad ibn Maslamah: > **""Your brother has the reward of two martyrs.""** And of Banu Zurayq: Mas'ud ibn Sa'd ibn Qays."
"10213","Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I never witnessed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) any spoils but he allotted to me, except Khaybar, for it belonged to the people of al-Hudaybiyah exclusively. Abu Hurayrah and Abu Musa came between al-Hudaybiyah and Khaybar."
"10214","'Uqbah ibn Suwayd al-Ansari, who heard his father, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), report: We returned with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the expedition of Khaybar. When Uhud appeared to him, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Allah is Greatest. A mountain that loves us and we love it.""**"
"10215","Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an expedition to Mu'tah and placed Zayd in command over them: if Zayd is killed, then Ja'far; if Ja'far is killed, then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah."
"10216","Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, horseman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dispatched the army of commanders and said: > **""Over you is Zayd ibn Harithah. If Zayd is stricken, then Ja'far ibn Abi Talib. If Ja'far is stricken, then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah al-Ansari.""** Ja'far leapt and said, ""May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah, I would not have feared your placing Zayd over me."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Proceed, for you know not which of that is better.""** They set out and stayed as Allah willed. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended the pulpit, ordered the call ""Prayer in congregation,"" and said: > **""Good has befallen - or good has come - shall I not inform you of this raiding army of yours? They set out and met the enemy. Zayd was stricken as a martyr, so seek forgiveness for him.""** The people sought forgiveness for him. > **""Then Ja'far ibn Abi Talib took the banner and charged the enemy until he was martyred. I bear witness to him for martyrdom, so seek forgiveness for him. Then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah took the banner and stood firm until he was killed as a martyr, so seek forgiveness for him. Then Khalid ibn al-Walid took the banner - he was not among the appointed commanders, he appointed himself.""** Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his finger and said: > **""O Allah, he is a sword of Your swords, so grant him victory.""** From that day Khalid was called the Sword of Allah. Then he said: > **""Go forth and reinforce your brothers, and let none stay behind.""** The people set out in intense heat, on foot and mounted."
"10217","Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Zayd, Ja'far, and 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah, and handed the standard to Zayd."
"10218","'Abdullah ibn Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an army and placed Zayd ibn Harithah in command over them: > **""If Zayd is killed or martyred, your commander is Ja'far. If he is killed or martyred, your commander is 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah.""** Zayd took the banner and fought until killed. Then Ja'far took it and fought until killed. Then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah took it and fought until killed. Then Khalid ibn al-Walid took the banner and Allah granted him victory. Their news reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He went out to the people, praised Allah, and said: > **""Your brothers met the enemy. Zayd took the banner and fought until he was killed or martyred. Then Ja'far ibn Abi Talib took it after him and fought until he was killed or martyred. Then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah took it and fought until he was killed or martyred. Then a sword of the swords of Allah, Khalid ibn al-Walid, took it and Allah granted him victory.""** He gave the family of Ja'far three days respite before visiting them, then came and said: > **""Weep not for my brother after today. Call me the sons of my brother.""** We were brought like chicks. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Call me the barber.""** The barber came and shaved our heads. Then he said: > **""As for Muhammad, he resembles our uncle Abu Talib. As for 'Abdullah, he resembles my creation and character.""** Then he took my hand, raised it, and said: > **""O Allah, succeed Ja'far in his family, and bless 'Abdullah in his dealings.""** Three times. Our mother came, mentioning our orphanhood, fearing poverty. He said: > **""Do you fear poverty for them while I am their guardian in this world and the Hereafter?""**"
"10219","Abu al-Yusr ibn 'Amr al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I handed the banner to 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah. He was stricken, so I handed it to Thabit ibn Aqram al-Ansari, who handed it to Khalid ibn al-Walid. Khalid said to him, ""Why hand it to me?"" He said, ""You know warfare better than I."""
"10220","'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr reported: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an expedition to Mu'tah in Jumada al-Ula of year eight, placing Zayd ibn Harithah in command over them. He said to them: > **""If Zayd is stricken, then Ja'far ibn Abi Talib is over the people. If Ja'far is stricken, then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah is over the people.""** The people equipped themselves, three thousand, and prepared to depart. At departure the people bade farewell to the commanders of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah was bidden farewell among them, he wept. It was said, ""What makes you weep, O son of Rawahah?"" He said, ""By Allah, not love of the world. But I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite a verse of the Book of Allah mentioning the Fire: 'There is none of you but will pass over it; this is with your Lord a decree determined' [Maryam 71]. I know not how I shall fare in the return after the passing."" The Muslims said to them, ""May Allah accompany you, defend you, and return you to us righteous."" 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah said: ""But I ask the Most Merciful for forgiveness, and a violent blow that casts froth, or a thrust by a heated hand with a spear piercing bowels and liver, until they say when they pass my grave: Allah guided him as a raider, and he is guided."" Then the people prepared to depart. 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah came to bid farewell to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: ""May Allah make firm what good He gave you, as Musa was made firm, with victory like theirs. I discerned good in you as a favor, a discernment unlike what they perceived. You are the Messenger; whoever is deprived of his favors and his face, fate has debased him."" Then the company left, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) escorting them until he bade them farewell. 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah said: ""Peace left behind upon one I bade farewell among the palms, unbade yet entrusted."" They marched until they camped at Ma'an in Sham. News reached them that Heraclius had camped at Ma'ab in the Balqa' with a hundred thousand Romans, joined by Arab tribes of Lakhm, Judham, Balqayn, Bahram, and Bali, a hundred thousand under Malik ibn Zanah. The Muslims halted at Ma'an two nights, considering writing to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to report the enemy's number, whether he reinforce them or command them. 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah encouraged them: ""O people, by Allah, what you dislike is what you set out seeking - martyrdom. We fight people not by number, strength, or multitude, but by this religion by which Allah honored us. Go forth, for it is one of two goods: either triumph or martyrdom."" In that halt, as Ibn Ishaq related from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr from Zayd ibn Arqam: ""I was an orphan of 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah in his care. He set out on that journey with me behind him on his saddle. One night we marched and I heard him recite: 'When you deliver me and carry my baggage four stages beyond al-Hasa'. When I heard it I wept; he struck me with his stick and said, 'What is it to you, wretch, that Allah grant me martyrdom and you return with the saddle's fork.'"" They marched until at the borders of Balqa' the hosts of Heraclius, Romans and Arabs, met them at Mashariq village, then the Muslims withdrew to Mu'tah village and met there. The Muslims arrayed: on the right Qutbah ibn Qatadah of Banu 'Udhrah, on the left 'Ubadah ibn Malik of the Ansar. They met and fought. Zayd ibn Harithah fought with the banner of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he fell amid the spears. Then Ja'far took it and fought until, pressed by fighting, he hamstrung his reddish horse and fought on foot until killed. Ja'far was the first Muslim to hamstring in Islam."
"10221","'Abbad ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr reported: My foster father, of Banu Murrah ibn 'Awf, who was in that expedition of Mu'tah, told me: By Allah, it is as if I see Ja'far ibn Abi Talib when he hamstrung his reddish horse, then fought until killed. When Ja'far was killed, 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah took the banner, advanced on his horse, urging himself down, hesitating somewhat, then said: ""I swore, O soul, you shall descend, willingly or compelled. Why do I see you averse to Paradise? Long have you been at ease. You are but a drop in a waterskin."" And 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah said: ""O soul, if you are not killed you shall die. This is the death you have faced. What you wished you have met. If you do as they did, you are guided."" Then he dismounted. His cousin brought him a bone of meat: ""Strengthen your back with this, for you have met much these days."" He took it and bit a bite, then heard the tumult among the people and said, ""And you still in the world?"" He cast it down, took his sword, advanced, and fought until killed. Then Thabit ibn Aqram of Bal-'Ajlan took the banner and said, ""O people, agree upon a man among you."" They said, ""You."" He said, ""I shall not."" So the people agreed upon Khalid ibn al-Walid. When he took the banner he held the enemy off, then withdrew and departed. When they were stricken, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Zayd ibn Harithah took the banner and fought with it until he was killed as a martyr. Then Ja'far took it and fought with it until he was killed as a martyr.""** Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent until the faces of the Ansar changed, thinking something displeasing about 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah. He said: > **""Then 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah took it and fought with it until he was killed as a martyr. They were raised to me in Paradise - as seen in sleep - upon thrones of gold. I saw in the throne of 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah a turning away from the thrones of his two companions. I asked, Why is this? It was said to me: They went forth, while 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah hesitated somewhat, then went forth.""**"
"10222","Ibn Shihab reported: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an army to Mu'tah, placing Zayd ibn Harithah over them: if Zayd is stricken, Ja'far ibn Abi Talib is their commander; if Ja'far is stricken, 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah is their commander. They marched until they met Ibn Abi Sabrah al-Ghassani at Mu'tah, with gatherings of Arab Christians, Romans, Tanukh, and Bahram. Ibn Abi Sabrah shut the fortress against the Muslims three days, then they came out. They met upon green crops and fought fiercely. Zayd ibn Harithah took the banner and was killed, then Ja'far took it and was killed, then Ibn Rawahah took it and was killed. Then after the commanders of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the people agreed upon Khalid ibn al-Walid. Allah routed the enemy and made the Muslims victorious. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had sent them in Jumada al-Ula."
"10223","Ibn al-Musayyab reported: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""They were shown to me in Paradise in a tent of pearl, each upon a throne. I saw Zayd and Ibn Rawahah with their necks turned away.""** He said, ""I asked,"" or ""It was said to me: When death overwhelmed them, it is as if they turned aside or averted their faces. As for Ja'far, he did not."" Ibn 'Uyaynah said: That is when Ibn Rawahah says: ""I swore, O soul, you shall descend, with obedience from you or compelled. Long have you been at ease."" Ja'far said, ""How sweet is the scent of Paradise."""
"10224","Abu al-Yusr reported: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Abu 'Amir al-Ash'ari came and said, ""You sent me on such and such. I came to Mu'tah. When the ranks were arrayed, Ja'far mounted his horse, donned his armor, took the banner, walked to the enemy, then called, 'Who will deliver this to its owner?' A man said, 'I,' and he sent it. Then he advanced and struck with his sword until killed."" The eyes of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flowed with tears. He led us in Zuhr, entered, and did not speak to us. Prayer was called, he came out, prayed, and did not speak. He did likewise at Maghrib and 'Isha, entering and not speaking. When he prayed he would turn to us. At dawn, at his usual hour, I and Abu 'Amir al-Ash'ari sat. He sat between us and said: > **""Shall I not tell you of a vision I saw? I entered Paradise and saw Ja'far with two wings stained with blood, Zayd facing him, and Ibn Rawahah with them as if turning from them. I shall tell you why: when Ja'far advanced and saw death, he did not turn his face, nor did Zayd. Ibn Rawahah turned his face.""**"
"10225","Asma' bint 'Umays (may Allah be pleased with her) reported: When Ja'far and his companions were stricken, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me while I had tanned forty skins, kneaded dough, washed my children, oiled and cleaned them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Bring me the sons of Ja'far.""** I brought them. He kissed them, his eyes overflowing. I said, ""May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, what makes you weep? Has anything reached you of Ja'far and his companions?"" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Yes, they were stricken today.""** I rose crying, and women gathered to me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to his family and said: > **""Do not neglect the family of Ja'far by preparing food for them, for they are occupied with the affair of their companion.""**"
"10226","'Urwah reported: There were killed on the day of Mu'tah, of the Ansar: al-Harith ibn al-Nu'man ibn Yasaf ibn Nadlah ibn 'Abd 'Awf ibn Ghanm, Zayd ibn Harithah ibn Ghanm, and Suraqah ibn 'Amr ibn 'Atiyyah ibn Khansa'."
"10227","'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) reported: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) angry over the affair of Banu Ka'b with an anger I had not seen for a long time. He said: > **""May Allah not grant me victory if I do not aid Banu Ka'b.""** She said: He said to me: > **""Tell Abu Bakr and 'Umar to prepare for this expedition.""** They came to 'A'ishah and asked, ""Where does the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intend?"" She said, ""I saw him angry over the affair of Banu Ka'b with an anger I had not seen for a long time."""
"10228","Dhu al-Jawshan al-Dababi reported: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after he finished with the people of Badr, with a filly of mine called al-Qarha'. I said, ""O Muhammad, I brought you the son of al-Qarha' to take."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""I have no need of it. If you wish, I will exchange it for the choicest coats of mail of Badr.""** He said, ""I would not exchange it today for a bright price."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""I have no need of it.""** Then he said: > **""O Dhu al-Jawshan, will you not embrace Islam and be among the first of this matter?""** I said, ""No."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Why?""** I said, ""I saw your people infatuated with you."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""How did news of their slain at Badr reach you?""** I said, ""It reached me."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Then we shall guide you.""** I said, ""If you conquer the Ka'bah and settle it."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Perhaps if you live you will see that.""** Then he said: > **""O so-and-so, take the man's bag and provision him with 'ajwah dates.""** When I turned away he said: > **""He is among the best horsemen of Banu 'Amir.""** He said: By Allah, while I was with my family in al-Ghawr, a rider came. I asked, ""What did the people do?"" He said, ""By Allah, Muhammad has conquered the Ka'bah and settled it."" I said, ""May my mother be bereaved of me. Had I embraced Islam that day and asked him for al-Hirah, he would have granted it to me."""
"10229","In a narration: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: > **""What prevents you from that?""** He said, ""I saw your people deny you, expel you, and fight you. I wait to see what you do. If you prevail over them, I shall believe in you and follow you. If they prevail over you, I shall not follow you."""
"10230","Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the poet of Khuza'ah said: ""O Allah, I adjure Muhammad by the covenant of our father and his ancient father. Aid, may Allah guide you, a mighty aid. Call the servants of Allah, let them come as madad (reinforcement)."""
"10231","'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended Makkah, he sent to men among his Companions that he intended Makkah, among them Hatib ibn Abi Balta'ah. It spread among the people that he intended Hunayn. Hatib wrote to the people of Makkah: ""The Messenger of Allah intends you."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed. He sent me with Abu Marthad al-Ghanawi, each of us with a horse, saying: > **""Go to Rawdat al-Khakh, for you will meet there a woman with a letter. Take it from her.""** We rode until we saw her at the place the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named. We said, ""Bring the letter."" She said, ""I have no letter."" We set down her baggage and searched it, finding nothing. Abu Marthad said, ""Perhaps she has no letter."" We said, ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) neither lies nor is lied to. You shall produce it or we shall strip you."" She said, ""Do you not fear Allah? Are you not Muslims?"" We said, ""You shall produce it or we shall strip you."" She produced it from her waist-wrapper."
"10232","Maymunah bint al-Harith, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with her), reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spent the night with her. He rose to ablute for prayer. She heard him say in his ablution place: > **""At Thy service, at Thy service. You are aided, you are aided.""** Three times each. When he came out she asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, I heard you say in your ablution place, 'At Thy service, at Thy service, you are aided, you are aided,' three times, as if speaking to a person. Was anyone with you?"" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""This is the rajiz of Banu Ka'b crying to me for help, claiming Quraysh aided Bakr ibn Wa'il against them.""** Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered 'A'ishah to equip him without informing anyone. Abu Bakr entered upon her: ""O daughter, what is this equipment?"" She said, ""By Allah, I know not."" He said, ""This is not the time of the expedition to Banu al-Asfar. Where does the Messenger of Allah intend?"" She said, ""By Allah, I have no knowledge."" They waited three days. Then he prayed dawn with the people, and the rajiz was heard reciting: ""O Lord, I adjure Muhammad by the covenant of our father and his ancient father. We bore you and you were a son, then we embraced Islam and did not withdraw. Quraysh broke their promise and violated your confirmed pact, claiming you call none. Aid, may Allah guide you, a mighty aid. Call the servants of Allah, let them come as madad (reinforcement), among them the Messenger of Allah stripped for battle."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""At Thy service, at Thy service. You are aided, you are aided.""** Three times each. Then he set out. At al-Rawha' he looked at a towering cloud and said: > **""This cloud pours with the aid of Banu Ka'b.""** A man of Banu 'Adi ibn 'Amr, brother of Banu Ka'b ibn 'Amr, asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, and the aid of Banu 'Adi?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""May your throat be dusty - is 'Adi but Ka'b, and Ka'b but 'Adi?""** That man was martyred in that journey. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""O Allah, blind them to our news until we take them by surprise.""** Then he camped at Marr. Abu Sufyan, Hakim ibn Hizam, and Budayl ibn Warqa' went out that night overlooking Marr. Abu Sufyan saw the fires: ""O Budayl, this is the fire of Banu Ka'b your kin."" He said, ""War has driven it to you."" Muzaynah, on guard that night, seized them. They asked to be taken to al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib. Abu Sufyan asked him to seek permission from the Messenger of Allah. He brought them to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He asked him to grant security to whom he granted. He said: > **""I have granted security to whom you granted, save Abu Sufyan.""** He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, do not bar me."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Whom you granted is secure.""** Al-'Abbas took them to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then out. Abu Sufyan said, ""We wish to depart."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Depart.""** The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose to ablute; the Muslims hastened to his ablution water, sprinkling it on their faces. Abu Sufyan said, ""O Abu al-Fadl, the sovereignty of your nephew has become great."" He said, ""It is not sovereignty, it is prophethood, and in that they delight."""
"10233","Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proceeded on his journey, leaving Abu Ruhm Kulthum ibn al-Husayn ibn 'Utbah ibn Khalaf al-Ghifari over Madinah. He set out on the tenth of Ramadan, fasting, the people fasting with him, until at al-Kadid - a water between 'Usfan and Amaj - he broke fast. Then he marched until he camped at Marr al-Zahran with ten thousand Muslims."
"10234","Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proceeded, leaving Abu Ruhm Kulthum ibn al-Husayn al-Ghifari over Madinah. He set out on the tenth of Ramadan, fasting with the people until at al-Kadid - a water between 'Usfan and Amaj - he broke fast. Then he camped at Marr al-Zahran with ten thousand Muslims, a thousand of Muzaynah and Sulaym, with numbers and Islam in every tribe; all Muhajirun and Ansar accompanied him, none staying behind. News was blinded to Quraysh; nothing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached them, nor knew they what he would do. That night Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, Hakim ibn Hizam, and Budayl ibn Warqa' went out spying for news. Al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib had met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the road. Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib and 'Abdullah ibn Abi Umayyah ibn al-Mughirah had met him between Madinah and Makkah seeking entry; Umm Salamah spoke for them: ""O Messenger of Allah, your cousin, your aunt's son and in-law."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""I have no need of them. As for my cousin, he tore my honor in Makkah. As for my aunt's son and in-law, he said to me in Makkah what he said.""** When that was conveyed, Abu Sufyan with his little son said, ""By Allah, you shall permit me or I shall take my little son and wander the earth till we die of thirst and hunger."" When that reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he softened, permitted them, they entered and embraced Islam. At Marr al-Zahran al-'Abbas said, ""Woe to Quraysh. By Allah, if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enters Makkah by force before they seek security, it is the ruin of Quraysh forever."" He said: I sat upon the white mule of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and rode to al-Arak, hoping to find a woodcutter, milkman, or needy one going to Makkah to inform them of his place, that they seek security before he enters by force. As I rode seeking this, I heard Abu Sufyan and Budayl ibn Warqa' in dispute, Abu Sufyan saying, ""Never saw I tonight's fires or camp."" Budayl said, ""By Allah, these are the fires of Khuza'ah, war has driven them."" Abu Sufyan said, ""Khuza'ah are too lowly for these to be their fires."" I knew his voice: ""O Abu Hanzalah."" He knew mine: ""Abu al-Fadl?"" I said, ""Yes."" He asked, ""What is it, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you?"" I said, ""Woe to you, Abu Sufyan, this is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the people. Woe to Quraysh, by Allah."" He asked, ""What is the stratagem?"" I said, ""If he captures you he will strike your neck. Ride behind me on this mule till I bring you to the Messenger of Allah and seek security for you."" He rode behind me; his two companions returned. I moved him along; whenever we passed a Muslim fire they asked, ""Who is this?"" Seeing the mule they said, ""The uncle of the Messenger of Allah upon his mule."" Until we passed the fire of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab. He asked, ""Who is this?"" and rose. Seeing Abu Sufyan on the mule's rear he said, ""Abu Sufyan, enemy of Allah? Praise be to Allah Who enabled us over you without pact or covenant."" He hastened toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The mule outran him as a mount outruns a slow man. I dismounted, entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and 'Umar entered: ""O Messenger of Allah, this is Abu Sufyan, Allah enabled us over him without pact or covenant, let me strike his neck."" I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I granted him protection."" I sat by the Messenger of Allah, holding his head: ""By Allah, none shall converse with him tonight but I."" When 'Umar persisted I said, ""Gently, 'Umar. Had he been of Banu 'Adi ibn Ka'b you would not say this; you know he is of Banu 'Abd Manaf."" He said, ""Gently, 'Abbas. By Allah, your Islam the day you embraced it was dearer to me than my father's Islam had he embraced it, for I knew your Islam was dearer to the Messenger of Allah than the Islam of al-Khattab."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Take him to your lodging, O 'Abbas. In the morning bring him to me.""** I lodged him overnight. In the morning I brought him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him he said: > **""Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan, has not the time come for you to testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah?""** He said, ""May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, how noble, forbearing, and joining you are. I thought had there been another with Allah, it would have availed me something."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan, has not the time come for you to know that I am the Messenger of Allah?""** He said, ""May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, how forbearing, noble, and joining you are. By Allah, something of this remained in my soul till now."" Al-'Abbas said, ""Woe to you, Abu Sufyan, embrace Islam and testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah before your neck is struck."" He testified the true testimony and embraced Islam. I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan loves this pride, grant him something."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Yes. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe. Whoever locks his door is safe. Whoever enters the mosque is safe.""** As he turned to leave, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""O 'Abbas, detain him at the strait of the valley by the mountain pass until the armies of Allah pass him and he sees them.""** I detained him as ordered. Tribes passed with banners. Each tribe he asked, ""Who are these, 'Abbas?"" I said, ""Banu Sulaym."" ""What are Sulaym to me?"" Then another: ""Muzaynah."" ""What are Muzaynah to me?"" Until tribes passed, none but he asked and I named, and he dismissed them. Until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed in the green battalion of Muhajirun and Ansar, only their eyes visible. He said, ""Glory be to Allah, who are these, 'Abbas?"" I said, ""This is the Messenger of Allah among the Muhajirun and Ansar."" He said, ""None has power against these. By Allah, Abu al-Fadl, your nephew's sovereignty has become great this morning."" I said, ""O Abu Sufyan, it is prophethood."" He said, ""Then yes, indeed."" I said, ""Safety to your people."" He went till he cried at the top of his voice: ""O Quraysh, this is Muhammad come to you with what you have no power against. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe."" His wife Hind bint 'Utbah rose, seized his mustache: ""Kill the fat coward. Evil scout of a people."" He said, ""Woe to you, let not this deceive you. He has come with what you have no power against. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe."" They said, ""Woe to you, what avails your house?"" He said, ""Whoever locks his door is safe, and whoever enters the mosque is safe."" The people dispersed to their houses and the mosque."
"10235","Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted security to the people on the day of the conquest of Makkah except four persons: 'Abd al-'Uzza ibn Khatal, Miqyas ibn Subabah, 'Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi al-Sarh, and Sarah, a woman. As for 'Abd al-'Uzza, he was killed holding the curtains of the Ka'bah. A man of the Ansar vowed to kill 'Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi al-Sarh if he saw him; he was the milk-brother of 'Uthman ibn 'Affan. 'Uthman brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) interceding. When the Ansari saw him he wrapped his sword and went seeking him, finding him in the circle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and shrank from killing him. He hovered, reluctant to advance upon him in the circle of the Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) extended his hand and pledged him, then said to the Ansari: > **""I waited for you to fulfill your vow.""** He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I was awed by you. Did you not wink to me?"" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""It is not for a prophet to wink.""** As for Miqyas ibn Subabah: his brother was killed by mistake with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent with him a man of Banu Fihr to collect blood-money from the Ansar. When it was collected and they returned, the Fihri slept. Miqyas took a stone and crushed his head, killing him, then recited: ""It healed the soul, one dead reclining in the valley, staining his garments with the blood of the slain. The cares of the soul before his killing stirred and made me forget the tread of beds. Through him I settled my revenge and attained my aim, and I was first to return to idols."" As for Sarah: she was a freedwoman of Quraysh who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining of need, and he gave her something. Then a man gave her a letter for the people of Makkah, seeking favor with them to preserve his family there - he had family there. Jibril informed of it. He sent 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and 'Ali ibn Abi Talib after her. They overtook her, searched her, found nothing, and returned. One of them said to the other something, then they returned again, threatened her, and she produced the letter from her hair."
"10236","Sa'd - meaning Ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) - reported: On the day of the conquest of Makkah the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted security to the people except four men and two women. He said: > **""Kill them even if you find them clinging to the curtains of the Ka'bah: 'Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, 'Abdullah ibn Khatal, Miqyas ibn Subabah, and 'Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi al-Sarh.""** As for 'Abdullah ibn Khatal: he was caught clinging to the curtains of the Ka'bah. Sa'id ibn Hurayth and 'Ammar ibn Yasir raced to him; Sa'id outran 'Ammar - the younger of the two - and killed him. As for Miqyas ibn Subabah: a man caught him in the market. As for 'Ikrimah: he embarked on the sea, a storm struck. The ship's crew said, ""Be sincere, for your gods avail nothing here."" 'Ikrimah said, ""If nothing but sincerity saves me at sea, nothing but it saves me on land. O Allah, I covenant that if You deliver me from this, I shall come to Muhammad and place my hand in his, and I shall find him pardoning, noble."" He came and embraced Islam. As for 'Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi al-Sarh: 'Uthman sheltered him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the people to the pledge, he brought him and stood him before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""O Messenger, pledge 'Abdullah."" He raised his head looking at him, refusing each time, then pledged him after three turns with his fingers. Then he turned, praised Allah, and said: > **""Was there not among you a right-minded man who, seeing me withhold my hand from his pledge, would kill him?""** They said, ""O Messenger of Allah, had you winked to us with your eye?"" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""It is not fitting for a prophet to have the treachery of the eyes.""**"
"10237","Al-Zubayr - meaning Ibn al-'Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) - from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He was given on the day of the conquest of Makkah the banner of Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah. Al-Zubayr entered Makkah with two banners."
"10238","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah, the people looked up to him. He lowered his head upon his saddle in humility."
"10239","Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: We were at Sarif. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Abu Sufyan is near you, so beware of him.""** Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: > **""Embrace Islam, O Abu Sufyan.""** He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, my people, my people."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Your people - whoever locks his door is safe.""** He said, ""Grant me something."" He said: > **""Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe.""**"
"10240","Abu Layla (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: > **""Abu Sufyan is in the arak trees.""** We entered and seized him. The Muslims prodded him with the scabbards of their swords till they brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: > **""Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan. I have brought you this world and the Hereafter, so embrace Islam and you shall be safe.""** Al-'Abbas was his friend. He said to him, ""O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan loves acclaim."" So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a crier in Makkah: > **""Whoever locks his door is safe. Whoever lays down his weapon is safe. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe.""** Then he sent al-'Abbas with him till they sat at the pass of al-Thaniyyah. Banu Salamah came. He asked, ""O 'Abbas, who are these?"" He said, ""Banu Sulaym."" He said, ""What are Sulaym to me?"" Then 'Ali ibn Abi Talib came with the Muhajirun. ""Who are these?"" He said, ""'Ali ibn Abi Talib with the Muhajirun."" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came with the Ansar. ""Who are these?"" He said, ""These are the red death - this is the Messenger of Allah among the Ansar."" Abu Sufyan said, ""I have seen the sovereignty of Kisra and Qaysar, never saw I the like of your nephew's sovereignty."" Al-'Abbas said, ""It is prophethood."""
"10241","'Urwah reported: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out with twelve thousand Muhajirun, Ansar, Aslam, Ghifar, Juhaynah, and Banu Sulaym, leading horses till they camped at Marr al-Zahran. Quraysh knew not of them. They sent Hakim ibn Hizam and Abu Sufyan to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying, ""Take for us protection from him or inform him of war."" Abu Sufyan ibn Harb and Hakim ibn Hizam set out, met Budayl ibn Warqa', and took him along till at al-Arak near Makkah at nightfall they saw tents, the army, and heard neighing of horses. It frightened them. They said, ""These are Banu Ka'b, war has driven them."" Budayl said, ""These are more than Banu Ka'b; their gathering never reached this. Would Hawazin pasture our land? By Allah, I know not this either; this is like the pilgrims."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had sent cavalry ahead to seize spies, and Khuza'ah on the road let none pass. When Abu Sufyan and his companions entered the Muslim camp, horsemen seized them at night, bringing them fearing death. 'Umar ibn al-Khattab rose to Abu Sufyan, struck his neck, the people seized him and led him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he feared death. Al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib was his close friend in Jahiliyyah. He cried at the top of his voice, ""Will you not call 'Abbas for me?"" 'Abbas came, defended him, asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to entrust him to him, and walked in his place. 'Abbas mounted him behind at night and rode him through the camp till all saw him. 'Umar had said to Abu Sufyan when he struck his neck, ""By Allah, you shall not approach the Messenger of Allah till you die."" He sought aid of 'Abbas: ""I am slain."" He protected him from plunder. Seeing the multitude and obedience he said, ""Never saw I tonight's gathering."" Al-'Abbas delivered him, saying, ""You are slain unless you embrace Islam and testify Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah."" He tried to utter what 'Abbas ordered, his tongue would not loose. He spent the night with 'Abbas. Hakim ibn Hizam and Budayl ibn Warqa' entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), embraced Islam, and he questioned them of Makkah. At the call to dawn prayer the company stirred; Abu Sufyan feared: ""O 'Abbas, what do they want?"" He said, ""The Muslims preparing to attend the Messenger."" 'Abbas took him out. Seeing them Abu Sufyan asked, ""O 'Abbas, they do nothing he orders but they do it?"" 'Abbas said, ""Were he to forbid them food and drink they would obey."" 'Abbas said, ""Speak to him of pardon for your people."" Al-'Abbas brought Abu Sufyan to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 'Abbas said, ""O Messenger of Allah, this is Abu Sufyan."" Abu Sufyan said, ""O Muhammad, I sought aid of my god and you sought aid of yours. By Allah, I saw only your triumph over me. Had my god been true and yours false, I would have triumphed."" Then he testified there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. 'Abbas said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I wish to go to my people, warn them of what befell and call them to Allah and His Messenger."" He permitted him. 'Abbas asked, ""How shall I tell them, O Messenger of Allah? Clarify for me a security they trust."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Say to them: Whoever testifies that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah alone without partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger, is safe. Whoever sits at the Ka'bah laying down his weapon is safe. Whoever locks his door is safe.""** 'Abbas said, ""O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is our cousin; I wish he return with me - would you single him with honor?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe.""** Abu Sufyan kept asking understanding: his house at upper Makkah. > **""And whoever enters the house of Hakim ibn Hizam and withholds his hand is safe.""** Hakim's house at lower Makkah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mounted 'Abbas on his white mule gifted by Dihyah al-Kalbi; 'Abbas rode with Abu Sufyan behind. As 'Abbas departed, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent after him: > **""Overtake 'Abbas and return him to me.""** He told them what he feared for him. The messenger overtook him; 'Abbas disliked returning: ""Does the Messenger of Allah fear Abu Sufyan will return desiring few men and disbelieve after Islam?"" He said, ""Detain him."" He detained him. Abu Sufyan said, ""Treachery, O Banu Hashim?"" 'Abbas said, ""We do not betray, but I have a need to you."" He asked what; ""You will repay when Khalid ibn al-Walid and al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam come upon you."" 'Abbas halted at the strait below al-Arak at Marr; Abu Sufyan retained his words. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dispatched cavalry troop after troop, dividing them in halves: al-Zubayr, followed by Khalid ibn al-Walid with the army of Aslam, Ghifar, Quda'ah. Abu Sufyan asked, ""Is this the Messenger of Allah, 'Abbas?"" He said, ""No, but Khalid ibn al-Walid."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah before him in an Ansari battalion: > **""Today is the day of slaughter. Today the sanctuary is made lawful.""** Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered in the battalion of faith: Muhajirun and Ansar. Seeing many unknown faces Abu Sufyan said, ""O Messenger of Allah, you favored these faces over your people?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""You and your people did that. These believed me when you denied me, and aided me when you expelled me.""** With the Prophet that day were al-Aqra' ibn Habis, 'Abbas ibn Mirdas, and 'Uyaynah ibn Hisn ibn Badr al-Fazari. Seeing them about the Prophet he asked, ""Who are these, 'Abbas?"" He said, ""This is the battalion of the Prophet, with it the red death - Muhajirun and Ansar."" He said, ""Proceed, 'Abbas. Never saw I soldiers or company like today."" Al-Zubayr marched till he halted at al-Hajun. Khalid swept in from lower Makkah; ruffians of Banu Bakr fought them; Allah routed them; they killed at al-Hazwarah till they entered houses; a party rose on horses at al-Khandamah; the Muslims pursued. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered among the last, a crier calling: > **""Whoever locks his house and withholds his hand is safe.""** Abu Sufyan called in Makkah, ""Embrace Islam and be safe."" Allah restrained them from 'Abbas. Hind bint 'Utbah came, seized Abu Sufyan's beard: ""O family of Ghalib, kill this foolish elder."" He said, ""Release my beard. By Allah, if you do not embrace Islam your neck shall be struck. Woe to you, he came with truth. Enter your couch and be silent."""
"10242","Sa'id ibn Yarbu' - called al-Sarm - reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of the conquest of Makkah: > **""Four I grant no security, in hill or sanctuary: al-Huwayrith ibn Nufayl, Miqyas ibn Subabah, Hilal ibn Khatal, and 'Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi al-Sarh.""** Al-Huwayrith was killed by 'Ali ibn Abi Talib. Miqyas ibn Subabah was killed by a cousin of his. Hilal ibn Khatal was killed by al-Zubayr. As for 'Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi al-Sarh, 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him), his milk-brother, sought security for him. Two singing-girls of Miqyas sang satire of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); one was killed, the other came and embraced Islam."
"10243","Asma' bint Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) halted at Dhu Tuwa, Abu Quhafah - his sight gone - said to a young daughter, ""O daughter, take me up to Abu Qubays."" She led him up. He asked, ""O daughter, what do you see?"" She said, ""I see a gathered black mass."" He said, ""Those are the cavalry."" She said, ""I see a man running among the mass, coming and going."" He said, ""O daughter, that is the marshal - who orders the cavalry and advances before them."" She said, ""By Allah, the black mass has spread."" He said, ""Then by Allah the cavalry has pressed. Hasten me to my house."" She brought him down; horsemen met him before he reached home; a silver necklace hung on the girl's neck, and a man met her and tore it off. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque, Abu Bakr led his father. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him he said: > **""Why did you not leave the elder in his house till I come to him?""** Abu Bakr said, ""O Messenger of Allah, he is more deserving to walk to you than you walk to him."" He seated him before him, wiped his breast, and said to him: > **""Embrace Islam.""** He embraced Islam. Abu Bakr brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his head like thaghamah (white). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Change this in his hair.""** Then Abu Bakr rose, took his sister's hand: ""I adjure by Allah and Islam the necklace of my sister."" None answered. He said, ""O sister, reckon your necklace lost."" By Allah, trust among people today is little."
"10244","Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) brought his father Abu Quhafah - a blind elder - leading him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the conquest of Makkah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: > **""Did you not leave the elder in his house till we come to him?""** He said, ""I wished Allah reward him. My joy at the Islam of Abu Talib would have been greater than my joy at my father's Islam, seeking thereby the delight of your eye, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""You spoke truth.""**"
"10245","'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr reported: 'Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl fled toward Yemen. Umm al-Hakam bint al-Harith ibn Hisham - then a Muslim, wife of 'Ikrimah - sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to seek her husband. He permitted her and granted him security. She set out with a Roman slave who propositioned her. She kept enticing him till she reached people of 'Akk, sought their help, and they bound him. She reached her husband in Tihamah; he had boarded a ship. Sitting in it he called upon al-Lat and al-'Uzza. The crew said, ""None may be invoked here but Allah alone sincerely."" 'Ikrimah said, ""By Allah, if at sea none but Him, then on land none but Him. I swear by Allah I shall return to Muhammad."" 'Ikrimah returned with his wife, entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), pledged to him, and he accepted. A man of Hudhayl entered upon his wife fleeing when Banu Bakr defeated; she blamed, disabled, and shamed him for fleeing. He said: ""And you, had you seen us at al-Khandamah, when Safwan fled and 'Ikrimah fled, and the Muslims overtook us with swords cutting every arm and skull, you would not utter the least blame."""
"10246","Al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I took Abu Sufyan's hand and brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan loves acclaim, grant him something."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe, and whoever locks his door is safe.""** Then he stood; I took his hand and seated him by the road. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed him troop after troop. He asked, ""Who are these?"" I said, ""Muzaynah."" ""What are Muzaynah to me? No war between us in Jahiliyyah or Islam."" Then a troop passed: ""Who are these?"" I said, ""Juhaynah."" Until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed among the Muhajirun. Seeing them approach he turned to me: ""Your nephew has been given great sovereignty."" He mentioned much speech."
"10247","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived at Makkah, Qays was in his vanguard. Sa'd spoke to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to divert him from the place he was in, fearing he would advance to something. So he diverted him from it."
"10248","Abu Barzah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: > **""All people are safe except 'Abd al-'Uzza ibn Khatal.""** He was killed clinging to the curtains of the Ka'bah."
"10249","Abu Barzah al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I killed 'Abd al-'Uzza ibn Khatal while he clung to the curtain of the Ka'bah."
"10250","Al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) killed 'Abdullah ibn Khatal on the day of the conquest. They brought him out from beneath the curtains of the Ka'bah and struck his neck between Zamzam and the Maqam. He said: > **""No Qurashi shall be killed after this in captivity.""**"
"10251","Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Umm Hani' bint Abi Talib on the day of the conquest, hungry. She said, ""O Messenger of Allah, in-laws of mine sought refuge with me, and 'Ali ibn Abi Talib is not deterred by any blame for Allah's sake. I fear he learns of them and kills them. Grant security to whoever enters the house of Umm Hani' till they hear Allah's speech."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted them security and said: > **""We have granted protection to whom Umm Hani' granted protection.""** He said: > **""Is there food with you to eat?""** She said, ""I have only dry crumbs; I am shy to present them."" He said: > **""Bring them.""** He broke them in water; she brought salt. He said: > **""Is there condiment?""** She said, ""I have nothing, O Messenger of Allah, but some vinegar."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Bring it and pour it over the food.""** He ate, praised Allah, then said: > **""Excellent is vinegar as condiment, O Umm Hani'. No house is empty with vinegar in it.""**"
"10252","Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the conquest sat, Abu Bakr standing at his head with the sword."
"10253","Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived at Makkah he found there three hundred and sixty idols. He pointed with his staff to each idol and said: > **""Truth has come and falsehood has vanished. Indeed falsehood is ever vanishing.""** The idol fell without his touching it."
"10254","Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered on the day of the conquest with three hundred and sixty idols upon the Ka'bah; Iblis had fastened their feet with lead. He came with his staff, striking each idol so it fell face-down, saying: > **""Truth has come and falsehood has vanished. Indeed falsehood is ever vanishing.""** Until he passed them all."
"10255","Abu al-Tufayl (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Makkah, he sent Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) to Nakhlah, where al-Uzza stood over three acacia trees. Khalid cut the trees and demolished the house built over them, then returned and informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Return, for you have done nothing.""** Khalid returned. When her custodians saw him, they fled into the mountain, crying, ""O Uzza, madden him! O Uzza, blind him!"" Khalid came upon a naked woman with disheveled hair, throwing dust upon her head. He struck her with his sword and killed her, then returned and informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""That was al-Uzza.""**"
"10256","Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami reported that Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by al-Lat and said: ""I deny you, I do not glorify you; I have seen that Allah has humiliated you."""
"10257","Al-Zuhri reported: On the day of the Conquest, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Uthman: ""Bring me the key of the Kaaba."" He was delayed, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood waiting, until sweat poured from him like pearls, and he said: > **""What detains him?""** A man hastened to him. The woman holding the key — it was thought she was the mother of Uthman — kept saying, ""If he takes it from you, he will never return it to you."" Uthman persisted with her until she gave him the key. He brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who opened the door, entered the House, then came out with the people and sat by the place of drinking-water. Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""O Messenger of Allah, if we were given prophethood, and given the drinking-water service, and given the veiling service, no people would have a greater share than us."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seemed to dislike his words. Then he called Uthman ibn Talhah, handed him the key, and said: > **""Keep it concealed.""** Abd al-Razzaq said: I narrated it to Ibn Uyaynah, who said: Ibn Jurayj informed me — I think he said — from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali that day when he spoke about the key: > **""I only grant you what you bear cost for, and I do not grant you what you take tribute from.""** Meaning: I grant you the drinking-water service because you spend upon it, and I do not grant you the House, for they take gifts through it. That is the statement of Abd al-Razzaq."
"10258","Urwah, naming those of the Muslims martyred on the day of the Conquest, of Quraysh then of Banu Muharib ibn Fihr, reported: Kurz ibn Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"10259","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: A thousand of Banu Sulaym witnessed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the day of the conquest of Makkah — or Hunayn (the narrator doubted which)."
"10260","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: Those who witnessed the conquest of Makkah included one thousand nine hundred of Juhaynah, one thousand of Muzaynah, nine hundred of Banu Sulaym, somewhat over four hundred of Banu Ghifar, and somewhat over four hundred of Aslam."
"10261","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: The Conquest took place when thirteen nights of Ramadan had passed."
"10262","Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: When Makkah was opened for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: > **""Hold back weapons, except Khuzaah against Banu Bakr.""** He permitted them until he prayed Asr, then said: > **""Hold back weapons.""** The next day, a man of Khuzaah met a man of Banu Bakr at Muzdalifah and killed him. When that reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he stood to address the people — the narrator saw him with his back against the Kaaba — and said: > **""The most hostile of people toward Allah is one who kills in the sanctuary, or kills other than his killer, or kills for the grudges of the Jahiliyyah.""** A man stood and said, ""So-and-so is my son."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""There is no claim of parentage in Islam; the affair of the Jahiliyyah is gone. The child belongs to the bed, and the adulterer gets the stone.""** They asked, ""What is al-athlab?"" He said: > **""The stone.""** He also said: > **""There is no prayer after dawn until the sun rises, and no prayer after Asr until the sun sets.""** And he said: > **""A woman is not married together with her paternal aunt, nor together with her maternal aunt.""**"
"10263","Samurah ibn Jundab (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them on the day of the Conquest: > **""This year is the Greater Hajj. The pilgrimage of the Muslims and the pilgrimage of the polytheists have coincided for three successive days, and the pilgrimage of the Jews and Christians has coincided for six successive days. They have never coincided since the heavens and earth were created, nor will they coincide after this year until the Hour comes.""**"
"10264","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: On the day of Hunayn, a youth of ours from the Ansar said, ""We shall not be defeated today for lack of numbers."" No sooner had we met our enemy than the people fled. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon a mule of his, with Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith holding its bridle and his uncle al-Abbas holding its stirrup. We were in a soft-soiled valley; dust rose until none of us could see his own hand. Then a figure approached, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Halt — who are you?""** He said, ""I am Abu Bakr, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you,"" bearing a dozen or so wounds. Then another figure approached, and he said: > **""Halt — who are you?""** He said, ""I am Umar ibn al-Khattab, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you,"" bearing a dozen or so wounds. Then a figure approached bearing twenty-odd wounds, and he said: > **""Halt — who are you?""** He said, ""Uthman ibn Affan, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you."" Then a figure approached bearing a dozen or so wounds, and he said: > **""Halt — who are you?""** He said, ""Ali ibn Abi Talib, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you."" Then the people came forward, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Is there no loud-voiced man to go and call out among the people?""** A man went and shouted, and no sooner did his voice fall upon their ears than they came back returning. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) charged, and the Muslims charged with him, and the polytheists fled. Durayd ibn al-Simmah withdrew upon a hillock — or he said, upon a mound — with some six hundred men. One of his companions said to him, ""By Allah, I see a squadron approaching."" He said, ""Describe them to me."" They said, ""Their mark is such, their appearance such."" He said, ""No harm upon you; Qudaah gone off in pursuit of the people."" Then they said, ""By Allah, we see a rugged squadron approaching."" He said, ""Describe them to me."" They said, ""Their mark is such, their appearance such."" He said, ""No harm upon you; that is Sulaym."" Then they said, ""We see a horseman approaching."" He said, ""Woe to you — alone?"" They said, ""Alone."" He said, ""Describe him to me."" They said, ""Wrapped in a black turban."" Durayd said, ""By Allah, that is al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam. By Allah, he is your killer, and he will drive you from this place of yours."" Al-Zubayr turned to them and said, ""Why are these ones here?"" Then he advanced with those who followed him, slew three hundred there, cut off the head of Durayd ibn al-Simmah, and placed it before him."
"10265","Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: When we faced the valley of Hunayn, we descended into a valley of the valleys of Tihamah, deep and sloping, descending steeply, in the blindness of dawn. The enemy had lain in wait for us in its ravines, its sides, and its straits — gathered, prepared, and ready. By Allah, nothing startled us as we descended but squadrons charging upon us with the charge of a single man. The people fled, turning back, scattering, none turning to another. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) moved to the right, then said: > **""To me, O people.""** Only a group of Muhajirun and Ansar and his household remained with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — not many. Among those who stood firm with him were Abu Bakr and Umar; of his household, Ali ibn Abi Talib, al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, his son al-Fadl ibn Abbas, Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith, Rabiah ibn al-Harith, Ayman ibn Ubayd — the son of Umm Ayman — and Usamah ibn Zayd. And there was a man of Hawazin upon a red camel of his, holding a black banner of his on a long spearhead, ahead of the people with Hawazin behind him: when he caught up he thrust with his spear, and when the people eluded him he raised it for those behind him, and they followed him."
"10266","Ibn Ishaq said: Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir, from his father Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: While that Hawazin standard-bearer upon his camel was doing what he did, Ali ibn Abi Talib and a man of the Ansar set upon him, both intending him. Ali came at him from behind and struck the camel's hocks, bringing it down upon its hindquarters. The Ansari leapt upon the man and struck him a blow that severed his foot at mid-shin, toppling him from his saddle. The people grappled, and by Allah, those who had fled had not yet returned when they found the captives bound before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ahmad and Abu Yala narrated it with the addition: When the rout came, Kaladah cried out — he was the brother of Safwan ibn Umayyah, a polytheist that day within the respite the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had granted him: ""Sorcery is void today!"" Safwan said to him, ""Silence — may Allah dash your mouth! By Allah, for a man of Quraysh to rule me is dearer to me than for a man of Hawazin to rule me."""
"10267","Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn. The people turned away from him, and eighty men of the Muhajirun and Ansar stood firm with him. We stepped back upon our heels some eighty paces, but we did not turn our backs; they are the ones upon whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down tranquility. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon his mule, pressing forward. His mule shied and he leaned from the saddle. I said to him, ""Rise up — may Allah raise you."" He said: > **""Hand me a handful of dust.""** He struck their faces with it, and their eyes filled with dust. Then he said: > **""Where are the Muhajirun and Ansar?""** I said, ""Here they are."" He said: > **""Call out to them.""** So I called out to them, and they came with their swords in their right hands like meteors, and the polytheists turned their backs."
"10268","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: On the day of Hunayn, the people fled from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib and Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a call: > **""O companions of Surat al-Baqarah! O company of the Ansar!""** Then the call intensified among Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj. When they heard the call they came forward — by Allah, I likened them only to camels yearning for their young. When they met, the fighting joined, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Now the oven is hot.""** He took a handful of white pebbles and cast them, saying: > **""Defeated, by the Lord of the Kaaba.""** Ali ibn Abi Talib was that day the fiercest of people in fighting before him."
"10269","Buraydah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The people scattered from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn, and none remained with him but a man called Zayd, holding the bridle of the Messenger of Allah's gray mule. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: > **""Woe to you — call the people.""** So Zayd called, ""O people, this is the Messenger of Allah calling you!"" But none came. He said: > **""Call the Ansar.""** He said, ""O company of the Ansar, the Messenger of Allah calls you!"" But none came. He said: > **""Woe to you — call Aws and Khazraj specifically.""** He called, ""O company of Aws and Khazraj, this is the Messenger of Allah calling you!"" But none came. He said: > **""Woe to you — call the Muhajirun specifically, for my pledge is upon their necks.""** Buraydah narrated to me that a thousand of them then came forward, having cast aside their sheaths, until they reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They advanced onward until Allah granted them victory."
"10270","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Hunayn: > **""Cut them down at the throat.""** And he gestured with his hand toward the throat."
"10271","Al-Harith ibn Badal (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn, when all his Companions fled except al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib and Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) threw at our faces a handful of earth, and we fled; it seemed to me that every tree and every stone was on our heels."
"10272","Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Fihri (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Hunayn on a fiercely hot, scorching day. We camped under the shade of trees. When the sun declined, I donned my armor, mounted my horse, and came to him in his tent. I greeted him, and he said: > **""And upon you be peace, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings.""** I said, ""Has the time to move come, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: > **""Then call Bilal.""** Bilal sprang from beneath a tree as though his shadow were a bird's shadow, and said, ""At your service, at your glad service, and I am your ransom."" He said: > **""Saddle my horse for me.""** He brought out a saddle whose pads were of palm-fiber, in which there was neither pride nor arrogance. He saddled for him, then he mounted, and we marched that evening and night. When the two cavalries scented each other, the Muslims turned fleeing, as Allah said. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""O servants of Allah, I am Allah's servant and His Messenger.""** He plunged from his horse, dismounted, and took a handful of pebbles. One nearer to him than I narrated to me that he struck their faces and said: > **""May the faces be deformed.""** So Allah routed the polytheists. Their sons narrated to me that their fathers said: Not one of us remained that day but his eyes and mouth were filled with dust, and we heard a rattling from the sky to the earth like iron drawn over a brass basin."
"10273","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) handed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dust, and he cast it at the faces of the polytheists on the day of Hunayn."
"10274","Yasir reported: Amr ibn Murrah used to narrate: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Amr ibn Murrah, he and his people Juhaynah ibn Zayd, to halt on the day of Hawazin. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: > **""O company of Juhaynah, be at the heels of Banu Sulaym; if they surge, put your weapons to their backs.""** That day a clan of them called Banu Isyah surged — for they disobeyed Allah and His Messenger — and Juhaynah slew them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Juhaynah forward toward Hawazin and turned Sulaym from their station. Allah routed them that day, slaughter was great among them, Amr ibn Murrah slew that day Ibn Dhi al-Burdayn al-Hilali, and Juhaynah had a fine trial therein."
"10275","Iyad (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Hawazin with twelve thousand, and there were slain of us, the people of Taif, on the day of Hunayn as many as were slain of Quraysh on the day of Badr. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a handful of valley dust and cast it at our faces, and routed us."
"10276","Zayd ibn Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The people fled from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn, and he said: > **""I am the Prophet, no lie; I am the son of Abd al-Muttalib.""**"
"10277","Amr ibn Dinar said — I know it only from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) — that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Hunayn: > **""Now the oven is hot.""** Then he said: > **""Defeated, by the Lord of the Kaaba.""**"
"10278","Yazid ibn Amir al-Suwai (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that he said, at a rout in which the Muslims were routed on the day of Hunayn and the disbelievers pursued them: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a handful of earth and cast it at their faces, saying: > **""Return — may the faces be deformed.""** There was none of us who met his brother but complaining of grit and rubbing his eyes."
"10279","Yazid ibn Amir al-Suwai — who had witnessed Hunayn with the polytheists then embraced Islam — reported: We asked him about the terror Allah cast into their hearts on the day of Hunayn, how it was. He took a pebble and cast it at a basin, and it rang. He said, ""In our bellies we felt the like of this."""
"10280","Jubayr ibn Mutim (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I saw on the day of Hunayn a black thing like a striped cloak between the sky and the earth; when it was driven down to the earth, it spread upon the earth as dust, and the polytheists fled."
"10281","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Hand me a handful of pebbles.""** So I handed him, and he cast them at the faces of the people. Not one of the people remained but his eyes were filled with pebbles. Then was revealed: ""You did not throw when you threw, but Allah threw."""
"10282","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) handed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dust, and he cast it at the faces of the polytheists on the day of Hunayn."
"10283","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When the Muslims fled on the day of Hunayn, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon his gray mule called Duldul. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: > **""Duldul, kneel.""** She pressed her belly to the earth until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a handful of dust and cast it at their faces, saying: > **""Ha-Mim — they shall not be aided.""** The people fled, though we had shot them with no arrow, thrust them with no spear, and struck them with no sword."
"10284","Musab ibn Shaybah reported from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn. By Allah, neither Islam nor knowledge of him brought me out; rather I disdained that Hawazin should prevail over Quraysh. As I stood with him I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I see piebald horses."" He said: > **""O Shaybah, none sees them but a disbeliever.""** He struck his hand upon my chest, then said: > **""O Allah, guide Shaybah.""** Then he struck it a second time and said: > **""O Allah, guide Shaybah.""** By Allah, he had not lifted his hand from my chest the third time before there was none of Allah's creation dearer to me than him. Then the people met, with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon a she-camel — or a mule — Umar holding its bridle and al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib holding the mouth of his mount. The Muslims fled, and al-Abbas called out in a loud voice: ""Where are the first Muhajirun? Where are the companions of Surat al-Baqarah?"" And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying as he advanced: > **""I am the Prophet, no lie; I am the son of Abd al-Muttalib.""** The Muslims turned back and clashed with swords. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Now the oven is hot.""** And Allah routed the polytheists."
"10285","Ikrimah reported: Shaybah ibn Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) campaigned on the day of Hunayn, I remembered my father and my uncle, slain by Ali and Hamzah, and said, ""Today I shall take my revenge upon Muhammad."" I came at him, and there was al-Abbas at his right, wearing a white cuirass like silver; the dust cleared from it, and I said, ""His uncle — he will not fail him."" I came at him from his left, and there was Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith. I said, ""His cousin — he will not fail him."" I came at him from behind, drawing nearer and nearer until nothing remained but to strike with the sword, when a flame of fire like lightning was raised before me. I feared it would seize me, so I recoiled backward. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to me and said: > **""Come, O Shayb.""** The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his hand upon my chest, and Allah drew Satan out of my heart. I raised my eyes to him, and he was dearer to me than my hearing and my sight and than all else. He said to him: > **""O Shayb, fight the disbelievers.""** Then he said: > **""O Abbas, call out the first Muhajirun who pledged beneath the tree, and the Ansar who sheltered and aided.""** I likened the turning of the Ansar toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only to cattle turning to their young, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dismounted as into a thicket. The spears of the Ansar seemed to me then more fearsome for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than the spears of the disbelievers. Then he said: > **""O Abbas, hand me of the valley dust.""** Allah made the mule comprehend his speech, and she lowered herself with him until her belly nearly touched the earth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took of the gravel, blew it at their faces, and said: > **""May the faces be deformed. Ha-Mim — they shall not be aided.""**"
"10286","Muhammad ibn Sallam al-Jumahi reported: Malik ibn Awf ibn Sad ibn Rabiah ibn Yarbu ibn Wathilah ibn Dahman ibn Nasr ibn Muawiyah ibn Bakr ibn Hawazin. Ibn Sallam said: Awf was a bold chief. His first mention and famed valor were on the day of al-Fijar with his people, when his deeds were many. He was over Hawazin when they met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he drove along with the people their wealth and offspring. Durayd ibn al-Simmah opposed him, persisted, and refused; they inclined to Malik's command and did not praise Durayd's view. He was their chief that day. When he saw the rout of his companions, he made for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — he was fiercely bold — hoping, he claimed, to strike him. Marthad ibn Abi Marthad al-Ghanawi met him and fought him; he spurred his horse Mihaj, but it would not advance. He urged it and shouted at it, but it would not advance, so he said: ""Advance, Mihaj, it is a fateful day — the like of me upon the like of you defends and charges, and thrusts the thrust that rends and tears, spilling gore from the belly, while the spearhead breaks therein, as squadrons surge after squadrons."" After he embraced Islam he witnessed al-Qadisiyyah and said: ""Advance, Mihaj, these are the braceleted ones — let no solitary man dismay you."" Then he fled from Hunayn to Taif, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""If he comes to me, I shall grant him security and give him a hundred.""** So he came, and he did that for him, and sent him forth to fight the people of Taif. Sad ibn Abi Waqqas wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with them both) seeking reinforcement, and he wrote back: ""You seek reinforcement from me while you are among ten thousand, with Malik ibn Awf and Hanzalah ibn Rabiah among you — he who is called Hanzalah the Scribe?"" Ibn Sallam said: One of his people narrated to me that he said to Umar: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me to reconcile me to Islam, but I did not wish to take a wage for Islam — so I return it."" He said, ""He gave it to you only seeing it your right."""
"10287","Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to narrate that he was present with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he was scooping dust at their faces."
"10288","The wife of Rafi ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that Rafi was struck by an arrow in his breast alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud — or the day of Hunayn, she doubted. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, pull out the arrow."" He said: > **""O Rafi, if you wish I shall pull out the arrow and the head together; and if you wish I shall pull out the shaft and leave the head, and testify for you on the Day of Resurrection that you are a martyr.""** He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, pull out the shaft and leave the head."" So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pulled out the shaft and left the head."
"10289","Abd al-Samad ibn Habib al-Awdhi reported from his father, who said: We campaigned with Sinan ibn Salamah — meaning Ibn al-Muhabbaq. He said: I was born on the day of Hunayn. My father was given glad tidings of me — they said, ""A boy is born to you."" He said, ""An arrow I shoot on behalf of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is dearer to me than what you herald to me."" And he named me Sinan (spearhead)."
"10290","Al-Adda ibn Khalid ibn Hawdhah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: We fought the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah neither aided us nor gave us victory."
"10291","Budayl ibn Warqa (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to detain the captives and the property at Jiranah until he should come — so they were detained."
"10292","Abu Jarwal Zuhayr ibn Surad (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took us captive on the day of Hunayn, the day of Hawazin, and began distributing the captives and the sheep, I came to him and began reciting this verse: ""Bestow upon us, O Messenger of Allah, in generosity, for you are the man we hope and await. Bestow upon the tribe that fate has hindered, whose union is scattered, changed by time. Time has left us wailing in grief, with anguish and sorrow upon their hearts. If no outspread mercy reaches them, O weightiest of men in forbearance when tested. Bestow upon women you used to nurse, when your mouth filled with pure milk from their churn. When you were a small child you nursed them, adorned by what you bring and what you leave. Make us not like those whose union is scattered; spare us, for we are a radiant company. We give thanks for blessings though they were denied, and have store after this day. Clothe with pardon those you nursed among your mothers — pardon is renowned. O best of those over whom dark steeds pranced at the battle's blaze when sparks were kindled. We hope for a pardon from you to wear, O guide of creation, pardoning and victorious. So pardon — may Allah pardon what you fear on the Day of Resurrection when triumph is granted you."" When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard this verse, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""What is mine and Banu Abd al-Muttalib's is yours.""** Quraysh said, ""What is ours is for Allah and His Messenger."" And the Ansar said, ""What is ours is for Allah and His Messenger."""
"10293","Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: When the delegation of Hawazin came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Jiranah, having embraced Islam, they said, ""We are a root and a kin; there has befallen us of calamity what is not hidden from you — so bestow upon us, may Allah bestow upon you."" A man of Hawazin of Banu Sad ibn Bakr called Zuhayr, surnamed Abu Surad, said, ""O Messenger of Allah, our women are your paternal aunts, your maternal aunts, and your nursing mothers who raised you. Had we joined al-Harith ibn Abi Shimr or al-Numan ibn al-Mundhir, then suffered from him the like of what we suffered from you, we would hope for his pity and kindness toward us — and you are the best of nurslings."" Then he recited before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a verse he had composed, mentioning his kinship and their nursing of him: ""Bestow upon us, O Messenger of Allah, in generosity, for you are the man we hope and await. Bestow upon the tribe that fate has hindered, whose union is scattered, changed by time. Time has left us wailing in grief, with anguish and sorrow upon their hearts. If no outspread mercy reaches them, O weightiest of men in forbearance when tested. Bestow upon women you used to nurse, when your mouth filled with pearls of milk from their churn. When you were a small child you nursed them, adorned by what you bring and what you leave. Make us not like those whose union is scattered; spare us, for we are a radiant company."" Then he mentioned the rest of the account."
"10294","Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — on the day of Hunayn — when the delegations of Hawazin came to him and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, we are a family and a kin — bestow upon us, may Allah bestow upon you; there has befallen us of calamity what is not hidden from you."" He said: > **""Choose between your women, your property, and your lineages.""** They said, ""You have given us choice between our honors and our property — we choose our children."" He said: > **""What is mine and Banu Abd al-Muttalib's is yours. When I pray Zuhr, say: we seek intercession by the Messenger of Allah with the Muslims, and by the Muslims with the Messenger of Allah, for our women and our children.""** They did so. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""As for what is mine and Banu Abd al-Muttalib's, it is yours.""** The Muhajirun said, ""What is ours is for the Messenger of Allah."" The Ansar said the like. Uyaynah ibn Badr said, ""As for what is mine and Banu Fazarah's — no."" Al-Aqra ibn Habis said, ""As for me and Banu Tamim — no."" Abbas ibn Mirdas said, ""As for me and Banu Sulaym — no."" The two clans said, ""You lie! Rather it is for the Messenger of Allah."" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""O people, return to them their women and their property. Whoever holds to anything of this fay, he shall have six shares upon us from the first fay Allah grants us.""** Then he mounted his mount, and the people clung to him, saying, ""Divide our fay among us!"" until they forced him to a lote-tree and it snatched his cloak. He said: > **""O people, return my cloak to me. By Allah, were livestock as numerous as the trees of Tihamah, I would divide them among you — then you would not find me miserly, nor cowardly, nor lying.""** Then he drew near a camel, took a hair from its hump, placed it between his forefinger and middle finger, raised it, and said: > **""O people, I have of this fay not even this, except the fifth — and the fifth is returned to you. Return the needle and the thread, for misappropriation shall be upon its people on the Day of Resurrection shame, fire, and disgrace.""** A man carrying a ball of hair stood and said, ""I took this to mend the saddle of a mangy camel of mine."" He said: > **""As for what is mine and Banu Abd al-Muttalib's, it is yours.""** The man said, ""O Messenger of Allah, now that it amounts to what I see, I have no need of it."" And he cast it away."
"10295","Atiyyah (may Allah be pleased with him), who was among those who spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the Hawazin captives, reported: He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, your kin and your root, and all the nursing ties before you — for this day we kept you in store. They are your mothers, your sisters, and your maternal aunts."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke to his Companions, and they returned their captives to them except two men. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Go and give them choice.""** One of them said, ""I leave mine."" The other said, ""I shall not leave mine."" When he turned away, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""O Allah, spoil his share.""** He would pass by a virgin girl or a boy and leave them, until he passed an old woman. He said, ""I shall take this one, for she is the mother of a clan — they will ransom her from me for all they can."" Atiyyah said Allahu Akbar and said, ""Take her."" Then he said to the Messenger, ""O Messenger of Allah, her mouth is not cool, her breast is not firm, and she has no kinsman to ransom her — an old woman, O Messenger of Allah, toothless, a captive with none."" When he saw that none would take her, he left her."
"10296","Al-Tabarani reported: Hakim ibn Hizam ibn Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy ibn Kilab, surnamed Abu Khalid. His mother was Safiyyah bint Zuhayr ibn al-Harith ibn Asad, and her mother was Salma bint Abd Manaf ibn Abd al-Dar. He embraced Islam on the day of the Conquest, and he was of those whose hearts were reconciled — the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him one hundred camels of the spoils of Hunayn."
"10297","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided on the day of Hunayn a share among those whose hearts were to be reconciled, and the Ansar felt grievance within themselves. They said, ""He divided among them."" He said: > **""O company of the Ansar, will you not be pleased to go with the Messenger of Allah among you?""** They said, ""Yes indeed."""
"10298","Muhammad ibn Ishaq reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the delegation of Hawazin at Hunayn, asking them about Malik ibn Awf al-Nasri: > **""What did Malik do?""** They said, ""He is at Taif."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Inform Malik that if he comes to me a Muslim, I shall return to him his family and his property, and give him a hundred camels.""** That was conveyed to Malik, and he came out to him from Taif. Malik feared Thaqif for himself — lest they learn what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had said to him and detain him — so he ordered a mount of his to be prepared and a horse of his to be brought from Taif. He set out by night, mounted his horse, and caught up with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), reaching him at Jiranah — or Makkah. He returned to him his family and his property, and gave him a hundred camels."
"10299","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was dividing the spoils of Hunayn with Jibril at his side, when an angel came and said, ""Your Lord commands you such-and-such."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Jibril: > **""Do you know him?""** He said, ""He is an angel — and I do not know all the angels of your Lord."" In an addition: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) feared he might be a devil. Many hadiths on the virtues of the Ansar concerning the spoils of Hunayn belong in their chapter."
"10300","Muhammad ibn Ishaq, naming those martyred on the day of Hunayn, reported: Ayman ibn Ubayd (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"10301","Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: Among those who stood firm with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was Ayman ibn Umm Ayman — that is, Ibn Ubayd (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"10302","Al-Tabarani reported: Ayman ibn Umm Ayman was martyred on the day of Hunayn. He is Ayman ibn Ubayd, the brother (by alliance) of Banu Awf ibn al-Khazraj, and the brother of Usamah ibn Zayd by his mother (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"10303","Urwah reported: There were killed on the day of Hunayn, of the Muslims, of Quraysh then of Banu Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza: Zayd ibn Rabiah. And of Quraysh then of Banu Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza: Zayd ibn Zamaah — al-Tabarani said: So said Ibn Lahiah, and it is a mistake; the correct is Yazid, as shall come from al-Zuhri. And of the Ansar, of Banu Amr ibn Awf then of Banu al-Ajlan: Suraqah ibn al-Hubab (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"10304","Ibn Shihab, naming those martyred with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of the Ansar then of Banu al-Ajlan, reported: Murrah ibn Suraqah ibn al-Hubab — so said Ibn Shihab. And there was martyred with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn, of Quraysh then of Banu Asad: Yazid ibn Zamaah (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"10305","Ibn Ishaq, naming those martyred with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of Quraysh then of Banu Asad, reported: Yazid ibn Zamaah ibn al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib — a horse called al-Janah bolted with him and killed him. And there was martyred on the day of Hunayn with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of the Ansar: Suraqah ibn al-Hubab ibn Adi ibn al-Najjar (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"10306","Abu Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besieged the fortress of Taif, I let myself down to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by a pulley. He said: > **""How did you come down?""** I said, ""I came down by a pulley."" He said: > **""You are Abu Bakrah.""**"
"10307","Ibn Shihab, naming those martyred on the day of Taif, of the Ansar, reported: Thabit ibn Thalabah, and Thalabah who is called al-Jadh (the stump). And of the Ansar, of Banu Amr ibn Awf then of Banu Muawiyah: Ruqaym ibn Thabit ibn Thalabah (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"10308","Urwah, naming those martyred on the day of Taif, of the Ansar then of Banu Salim then of Banu Haram, reported: Thalabah who is called al-Jadh. And of the Ansar, of Banu Amr ibn Awf then of Banu Muawiyah ibn al-Harith: Ruqaym ibn Thabit — or Thabit ibn Thalabah (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"10309","Muhammad ibn Ishaq, naming those martyred on the day of Taif, reported: Julayhah ibn Abdullah ibn Muharib ibn Nashib ibn Sad ibn Layth. And of the Ansar then of Banu Aws: Ruqaym ibn Thabit ibn Thalabah ibn Thawban ibn Muawiyah. And of Quraysh then of Banu Umayyah ibn Abd Shams: Said ibn Said ibn al-Asi (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"10310","Al-Tabarani reported: Abdullah ibn Abi Umayyah ibn al-Mughirah ibn Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Makhzum, the brother of Umm Salamah by her father — his mother was Atikah bint Abd al-Muttalib, the paternal aunt of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He embraced Islam on the day of the Conquest, met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and was martyred on the day of Taif with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"10311","Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: He witnessed Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) in the days of the expedition of Tabuk, in the Army of Hardship. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered charity, strength, and consolation. The Christians of the Arabs had written to Heraclius: ""This one who has come out claiming prophethood is ruined; years of drought have struck him and their wealth has perished. If you wish to overtake your religion, now is the time."" So he sent a chief of theirs called al-Dinad, equipping with him forty thousand. When that reached the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he wrote among the Arabs, sitting each day upon the minbar, supplicating and saying: > **""O Allah, if this band perishes, You shall not be worshipped on earth.""** The people had no strength. Uthman ibn Affan had equipped a caravan for Syria, intending to fetch grain upon it. He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, these are two hundred camels with their saddles and cloths, and two hundred uqiyyah."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praised Allah, and the people raised the takbir. Then he stood another time and ordered charity. Uthman stood and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, and these are two hundred, and two hundred uqiyyah."" He raised the takbir and the people raised it. Uthman brought the camels and brought the charity before him. I heard him say: > **""Nothing Uthman does after this day shall harm him.""**"
"10312","Hamzah ibn Amr al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out for the expedition of Tabuk, and I was in his service that journey. I looked at the ghee-skin and saw how little was in it. I prepared food for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), placed the ghee in the sun, and slept. I woke to the bubbling of the skin. I rose and seized its mouth with my hand. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me and said: > **""Had you left it, it would have flowed a valley of ghee.""**"
"10313","Abu Ruhm (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: We were on a march, and beside me was a man I crowded at night without knowing him — and behold, he was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: > **""Who is this?""** I said, ""Abu Ruhm."" He said: > **""What became of the tall curly dark men of Banu Ghifar? Is any of them with us on the march?""** I said, ""No."" He said: > **""What became of the dark short pug-nosed men of Aslam? Is any of them with us on the march?""** I said, ""No."" He said: > **""What became of the red light-bearded men? Is any of them with us on the march?""** I said, ""No."" He said: > **""None of my family left behind is dearer to me when left behind than Quraysh, the Ansar, Aslam, and Ghifar. What prevents one of them, when he stays behind, from lending a camel of his, so he earns the like of the reward of the one who goes out?""**"
"10314","Abu Ruhm al-Ghifari (may Allah be pleased with him) — who was of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who pledged beneath the tree — reported: I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Tabuk. When he separated, he journeyed by night, and I journeyed close to him. Drowsiness was cast upon me; I would wake to find my mount drawn near his mount, and its nearness startled me, fearing I would strike his foot in the stirrup. So I held my mount back, until at midnight my eyes overcame me and my mount mounted his mount, striking the foot of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the stirrup. I woke only at his saying: > **""Hiss.""** I raised my head and said, ""Seek forgiveness for me, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: > **""Ask.""** He began asking me about Banu Ghifar, and I informed him. Then he asked me: > **""What became of the red tall light-bearded ones — or the short ones — who have livestock at the cleft of Sharkh?""** I recalled them among Banu Ghifar but could not place them, until I remembered a group of Aslam. I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, what prevents one of them, when he stays behind, from mounting an energetic man in the path of Allah upon a camel of his?"" The dearest of my family to me, when left behind from me, are the Muhajirun of Quraysh, the Ansar, Aslam, and Ghifar."
"10315","And in a narration: ""What became of the short dark curly ones?"" I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, they are our successors left among us."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""What prevented one of them, when he stayed behind, from mounting upon his camels an energetic man in the path of Allah? The dearest of my family to me, when left behind from me, are the Muhajirun of Quraysh, the Ansar, Aslam, and Ghifar.""**"
"10316","Sad ibn Khaythamah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: I stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I entered a garden and saw a trellis sprinkled with water, and I saw my wife. I said, ""This is not fairness — the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is in the scorching wind and heat, while I am in shade and bliss."" So I rose to a water-camel, saddled it, and to some dates, taking them as provision. My wife called out to me, ""Where to, O Abu Khaythamah?"" I set out intending the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Partway, I met Umayr ibn Wahb. I said, ""You are a bold man, as I know. I come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as a sinful man — hold back from me until I am alone with the Messenger of Allah."" So Umayr held back from me. When I came in sight of the camp and the people saw me, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Be Abu Khaythamah.""** I came and said, ""I nearly perished, O Messenger of Allah."" I told him my story, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke good to me and supplicated for me."
"10317","Fadalah ibn Ubayd (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) campaigned the expedition of Tabuk, and the mounts were sorely exhausted. They complained to him of that. He saw them as men who could not rest their mounts. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked from a strait through which the people passed, stood over it while the people passed, breathed upon them, and said: > **""O Allah, carry them in Your path, for You carry the strong and the weak, the moist and the dry, upon land and sea.""** They moved onward, and we did not enter Madinah but that they were pulling at their bridles."
"10318","Abdullah ibn Salam (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed al-Julayhah on his journey to Tabuk, his Companions said to him, ""The halting place, O Messenger of Allah — shade and water,"" for there were dom-palms and water there. He said: > **""It is sown land and cool — leave it, for she is commanded.""** Meaning his she-camel. She advanced until she knelt beneath the dom-tree that stood in the mosque of Dhu al-Marwah."
"10319","Ubadah — meaning Ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) — reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended the expedition of Tabuk — then he mentioned the account."
"10320","Abu al-Shumus al-Balawi (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade his Companions on the day of al-Hijr from their well. So the dough-owner cast away his dough, and the hays-maker cast away his hays."
"10321","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) camped at al-Hijr, and the people drew water from their well. Then he moved on from there. When he settled, he ordered the people not to drink of its water nor perform ablution from it, and that any dough kneaded with its water be fed to animals. So the people did."
"10322","---"
"10323","Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that they were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Tabuk when they came to a valley. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: > **""You are in an accursed valley — so hasten.""** He mounted his horse and spurred, and the people spurred. Then he said: > **""Whoever has kneaded dough, or whoever has cooked a pot — let him overturn it.""** Then we marched. Then he said: > **""O people, there is today no living soul over whom a hundred years shall pass but Allah shall pay it no heed.""**"
"10324","Samurah ibn Jundab (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), on the day he reached Thamud, forbade them a well at the side of the town — that any of them drink from it or draw from it — and forbade us entering their houses."
"10325","Abu Kabshah al-Anmari (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: During the expedition of Tabuk, the people hastened toward the land of al-Hijr, entering upon them. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he called the people: ""Prayer is congregational."" I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he held his camel, saying: > **""Why would they enter upon a people with whom Allah is angry?""** A man called out to him, ""We marvel at them, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: > **""Shall I not tell you of something more marvelous than that? A man of yourselves tells you of what was before you and what shall be after you. So be upright and steadfast, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, pays no heed to your torment at all. And a people shall come who ward nothing from themselves.""**"
"10326","Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Do not ask about signs — or: do not ask your Prophet for signs — for the people of Salih asked their prophet to bring them a sign, and Allah, Blessed and Exalted, sent them the she-camel. She would come from this pass and drink their water on her day, and leave by this pass. Then they defied the command of their Lord and hamstrung the she-camel. It was said to them: Enjoy your homes for three days — or it was said to them: torment shall come to you within three days. Then the blast came to them, and Allah destroyed all of them beneath the easts and wests of the earth, except a man who was in the sanctuary of Allah, which shielded him from Allah's torment.""** They said, ""O Messenger of Allah, who was he?"" He said: > **""Abu Righal.""** It was said, ""And who is Abu Righal?"" He said: > **""The forefather of Thaqif.""**"
"10327","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported: It was said to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), ""Narrate to us about the affair of the Hardship."" Umar said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Tabuk in fierce heat. We camped a camp where dire thirst struck us, until we thought our necks would snap — until a man of us would go seeking privacy and not return before thinking his neck would snap, and until a man would slaughter his camel, wring its dung to drink it, and place it upon his belly. Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""O Messenger of Allah, Allah has accustomed you to good in supplication — so supplicate Allah."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: > **""Would you like that, O Abu Bakr?""** He said, ""Yes."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hands, and he had not lowered them before the sky spoke — it clouded, then poured. They filled all they had with them. Then we went to look, and we found it had not gone beyond the camp."
"10328","Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out on the day of the expedition of Tabuk, and it reached him that the water he was heading for was scant. He ordered a caller, who called out among the people: > **""Let none precede me to the water.""** He reached the water to find a people had preceded him — so he cursed them."
"10329","Abu al-Tufayl (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) approached from the expedition of Tabuk, he ordered a caller, who called out: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is taking the pass — let none take it. While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was being led by Ammar and driven by Hudhayfah, a veiled party upon mounts approached until they crowded Ammar as he drove the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ammar set about striking the faces of the mounts. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Hudhayfah: > **""Drive on — drive on.""** Until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended. When he descended, he camped, and Ammar returned. He said: > **""O Ammar, did you know the people?""** He said, ""I knew most of the mounts, but the people were veiled."" He said: > **""Do you know what they intended?""** He said, ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He said: > **""They intended to startle the Messenger of Allah's mount and cast him down.""** Ammar (may Allah be pleased with him) later spoke privately to a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said, ""I adjure you by Allah — how many were the companions of the pass?"" He said, ""Fourteen."" He said, ""If you were among them, they were fifteen."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) excused three of them, who said, ""By Allah, we did not hear the caller of the Messenger of Allah, nor did we know what the people intended."" Ammar said, ""I testify that the remaining twelve are war against Allah and His Messenger in the life of this world and on the day the witnesses stand."" Abu al-Walid said: Abu al-Tufayl mentioned of that expedition that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the people, when told the water was scant, ordering a caller who called out: Let none reach the water before the Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached it to find a party had reached it before him — so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed them that day."
"10330","'Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his uncle, reported: Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf used to lampoon the Prophet ﷺ in poetry. The Prophet ﷺ ordered Sa'd ibn Mu'adh radiya Allahu anhu to send five men to him. They came to him while he was in a gathering of his people at al-'Awali. When he saw them he was alarmed by them. He said, ""What brings you?"" They said, ""We came to you for a need."" He said, ""Let one of you come near to me and tell me his need."" One of them drew near, and they said, ""We came to you to sell you our coats of mail."" He said, ""By Allah, if you do so, you have indeed suffered hardship ever since this man arrived among you."" They promised to return to him after the night had quieted. They came to him and he stood to meet them. His wife said to him, ""These men have not come to you at this hour for anything you will like."" He said, ""They have already told me of their need."" When he drew near them, Abu 'Abs embraced him, Muhammad ibn Maslamah struck him with the sword and stabbed him in his side, and they killed him. In the morning the Jews came to the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ reminded them of how Ka'b had lampooned him in his poems and harmed him, then the Prophet ﷺ invited them to write a treaty between him and them. That document remained with 'Ali radiya Allahu anhu. > **""What brings you?""**"
"10331","Ibn 'Abbas radiya Allahu anhuma said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ walked with them to Baqi' al-Gharqad, then sent them forth and said: > **""Go forth in the name of Allah. O Allah, help them.""** That is, the party he sent toward Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf. In another version, when the Prophet ﷺ sent Muhammad ibn Maslamah and his companions to kill Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf, the rest is similar, with the addition that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ then returned to his house."
"10332","'Ubadah ibn al-Samit radiya Allahu anhu said: Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf used to lampoon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was with Abu Wada'ah at Makkah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded Hassan ibn Thabit radiya Allahu anhu, who lampooned him in return. When Hassan's lampoon of Abu Wada'ah reached Quraysh, they expelled Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf. When he arrived at Madinah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Muhammad ibn Maslamah, Abu 'Abs ibn Jabr, and Abu Na'ilah to him. They killed Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf at Sharj al-'Ujul among Banu Umayyah ibn Zayd."
"10333","'Urwah reported: Sa'd ibn Mu'adh sent al-Harith ibn Aws ibn al-Nu'man, brother of Banu Harithah, with Muhammad ibn Maslamah to Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf. When he struck Ibn al-Ashraf, the tip of the sword hit the leg of al-Harith, and his companions carried him away."
"10334","'Abdullah ibn Unays radiya Allahu anhu said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me, Abu Qatadah, an ally of theirs from the Ansar, and 'Abdullah ibn 'Atik to Ibn Abi al-Huqayq to kill him. We set out and came to Khaybar by night. We traced their doors and locked them from outside, then gathered the keys and threw them away. The people climbed into the date-palms. I and 'Abdullah ibn 'Atik entered the stairway of Ibn Abi al-Huqayq. 'Abdullah ibn 'Atik spoke. Ibn Abi al-Huqayq said, ""May your mother be bereft of you, 'Abdullah, how did you come to this town? Rise and open,"" speaking to his wife, ""for the generous man does not turn back from his door at this hour."" She rose. I said to 'Abdullah ibn 'Atik, ""Take him."" He drew the sword against them. His wife tried to scream, so he raised it against her, then remembered that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden killing women and children, so he withheld. 'Abdullah ibn Unays said: I entered upon him in his upper chamber and stood watching his intense whiteness in the darkness of the house. When he saw me he took a pillow and shielded himself with it. I went to raise the sword to strike him but could not because of the lowness of the room, so I stabbed him with a thrust. Then I went out. My companion said, ""Did you do it?"" I said, ""Yes."" He entered and stood over him. Then we came down the stairs. 'Abdullah ibn 'Atik fell on the stairs and said, ""O my leg, my leg is broken."" I said to him, ""There is nothing wrong with your leg."" I put down my bow, carried him — 'Abdullah was short and slight — and set him down, and his leg was unharmed. We went on until we joined our companions. The woman cried out, ""O night-raid!"" and the people of Khaybar rose up. Then I remembered my bow left on the stairs. I said, ""By Allah, I will return and take my bow."" My companions said, ""The people of Khaybar will rise over his killing."" I said, ""I will not return until I take my bow."" I went back to find the people of Khaybar in uproar, their only talk being who killed Ibn Abi al-Huqayq. I looked at no man's face nor did any look at mine except that I said the same as they said. Until I reached the stairway, climbed with the people, took my bow, and rejoined my companions. We traveled by night and hid by day, posting a watcher when we hid by day, until we neared Madinah. At al-Bayda' I was their watcher. I signaled to them with my garment, they descended quickly, and I followed until we reached Madinah. My companions said to me, ""Did you see anything?"" I said, ""No, but I saw the hardship that had reached you and wanted fear to carry you on."" We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was addressing the people. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Prosperous faces.""** We said, ""And your face, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: > **""Did you kill him?""** We said, ""Yes."" He called for the sword with which he was killed and said: > **""This is its food in the foam of the sword.""**"
"10335","'Abdullah ibn Unays radiya Allahu anhu said: The party whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to Ibn Abi al-Huqayq to kill him were 'Abdullah ibn 'Atik, 'Abdullah ibn Unays, Abu Qatadah, an ally of theirs, and a man from the Ansar. They arrived at Khaybar by night and went to their doors, locking them from outside. The wife of Ibn Abi al-Huqayq said, ""This is surely the voice of 'Abdullah ibn 'Atik."" He said, ""Open."" She opened, and I and 'Abdullah ibn 'Atik entered. 'Abdullah said, ""Take him."" I went to strike her with the sword but remembered the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ against killing women and children, so I withheld from her. 'Ali ibn al-Madini said: This is 'Abdullah ibn Unays al-Ansari, not the Juhani from whom Jabir ibn 'Abdillah narrated."
"10336","Jundab ibn 'Abdillah radiya Allahu anhu reported from the Prophet ﷺ: He sent a party and placed Abu 'Ubaydah over them. When he went to depart, he wept out of longing for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and sat down. So he placed 'Abdullah ibn Jahsh over them in his place, wrote a letter for him and ordered him not to read it until he reached such-and-such a place, and said: > **""Do not compel any of your companions to march with you.""** When he read the letter he said, ""To Allah we belong and to Him we return,"" and said, ""Hearing and obeying Allah and His Messenger."" He informed them of the matter and read the letter to them. Two men returned and the rest went on. They met Ibn al-Hadrami and killed him, not knowing whether that day was of Rajab or Jumada. The polytheists said to the Muslims, ""You killed in the sacred month."" Then Allah revealed: > **""They ask you about fighting in the sacred month.""** Some said, ""If they incurred no sin, they have no reward."" Then Allah revealed: > **""Indeed those who believed and emigrated and strove in the way of Allah, they hope for the mercy of Allah, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful.""**"
"10337","Ibn 'Abbas radiya Allahu anhuma, concerning His saying, ""They ask you about fighting in the sacred month, say: fighting therein is grave,"" said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent 'Abdullah ibn so-and-so in a detachment. They met 'Amr ibn al-Hadrami at Batn Nakhlah. Then he mentioned the long hadith."
"10338","'Asim ibn 'Amr ibn Qatadah said: After Uhud a party from 'Adal and al-Qarah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, there is Islam among us, so send with us men from your companions to instruct us in religion, teach us the Qur'an, and teach us the laws of Islam."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent six of his companions, among them Marthad ibn Abi Marthad al-Ghanawi, ally of Hamzah ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib radiya Allahu anhu. Then he mentioned the story. As for Marthad ibn Abi Marthad, Khalid ibn al-Bukayr, and 'Asim ibn Abi al-Aqlah, they said: > **""By Allah, we shall never accept a covenant or pact from a polytheist.""** They fought them until they killed them."
"10339","'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr said: The case of Khubayb ibn 'Adi ibn 'Abdillah al-Ansari of Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf, 'Asim ibn Thabit ibn Abi al-Aqlah ibn 'Amr ibn 'Awf, and Zayd ibn al-Dathinah al-Ansari of Banu Bayadah, is that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent them as scouts toward Makkah to report to him the news of Quraysh. They took the Najd road until, at al-Raji' in Najd, Banu Lihyan of Hudhayl intercepted them. As for 'Asim ibn Thabit, he fought with his sword until he was killed. As for Khubayb and Zayd ibn al-Dathinah, they climbed the mountain. The people could not reach them until they offered them covenants and pledges. They came down, and they bound them with ropes, brought them to Makkah, and sold them to Quraysh. As for Khubayb, 'Uqbah ibn al-Harith bought him, partnered in his purchase by Abu Ihab ibn 'Aziz and others — 'Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, al-Akhnas ibn Shunun, 'Ubaydah ibn Hakim, Umayyah ibn Abi 'Utbah, the sons of al-Hadrami, Sa'yah ibn 'Abdillah, and Safwan ibn Umayyah. They handed him to 'Uqbah ibn al-Harith, who imprisoned him in his house as long as Allah willed. A woman of the family of 'Uqbah used to open for him and feed him. He said to her, ""When the people intend to kill me, inform me beforehand."" When they intended to kill him, she informed him. He said, ""Seek for me a razor to cleanse myself with,"" meaning to shave his pubic hair. The son of the woman who used to help him entered with the razor in his hand. He took the boy's hand and said, ""Has Allah enabled you against us?"" She said, ""What is this I thought of you?"" Then he handed her the razor and said, ""I was only jesting."" The partners who shared in him brought him out, the people of Makkah with them, with a wooden cross, until at al-Tan'im they erected it and crucified him upon it. The one who undertook his killing was 'Uqbah ibn al-Harith, in retaliation for al-Harith ibn 'Amir, who had been killed at Badr as a disbeliever. Khubayb said to them at his killing, ""Release me from the bonds until I pray two rak'ahs."" They released him. He prayed two light rak'ahs, then turned and said: > **""Were it not that you would think I feared death, I would have lengthened them, and for that I shortened them.""** He said, ""O Allah, I look only upon the face of an enemy. O Allah, I find no messenger to Your Messenger, so convey my greeting of peace to him."" Jibril alayhi al-salam came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of that. Khubayb said as they raised him upon the wood: > **""O Allah, count them in number, kill them piecemeal, and leave none of them.""** The sons of the polytheists killed at Badr killed Khubayb. When they put weapons into him while crucified, they called to him and adjured him, ""Would you love that Muhammad were in your place?"" He said: > **""No, by Allah the Mighty, I would not love that even a thorn prick his foot in my ransom.""** They laughed. When they raised him to the wood Khubayb recited: ""The confederates gathered around me and incited their tribes, assembling every assembly; they gathered their sons and women, and I was brought near to a long, fortified trunk; to Allah I complain of my estrangement then my anguish, and what the confederates prepared for me at my death-place; O Throne-Possessor, grant me patience for what is intended for me, for they have cut my flesh and my hope is gone; that is for the sake of Allah, and if He wills He blesses the joints of a torn corpse; by my life, I care not, when I die a Muslim, upon what side my resting-place for Allah is."" As for Zayd ibn al-Dathinah, Safwan ibn Umayyah bought him and killed him for his father Umayyah ibn Khalaf; Nitas, freedman of Banu Jumah, killed him. Both were killed at al-Tan'im. 'Amr ibn Umayyah buried Khubayb. Hassan said concerning Khubayb: ""Would that Khubayb's covenant had not betrayed him, would that Khubayb had known the people; the straps of Zuhayr ibn al-Agharr and Jami', who of old rode forbidden deeds; you granted protection, then when you granted it you betrayed, at the outskirts of al-Raji' you were defeatists."""
"10340","Ibn Shihab, in naming those killed on the day of al-Raji', said: Marthad ibn Abi Marthad al-Ghanawi."
"10341","'Urwah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Marthad ibn Abi Marthad al-Ghanawi, ally of Hamzah ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib radiya Allahu anhu, to a clan of Hudhayl. Among the Muslims killed there — then of Banu Hashim by alliance — was Marthad ibn Abi Marthad."
"10342","'Amr ibn Murrah radiya Allahu anhu said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Juhaynah and Muzaynah against Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib, who was hostile to the Prophet ﷺ. When they had gone only a short distance, Abu Bakr al-Siddiq radiya Allahu anhu said, ""O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be ransom for you, on what basis are you sending two shrewd armies who nearly annihilated one another in Jahiliyyah, whom Islam found with remnants of that still among them?"" The Prophet ﷺ ordered them returned until they stood before him. He said: > **""O Muzaynah, greet Juhaynah; O Juhaynah, greet Muzaynah.""** He bound the banner of both armies — Juhaynah and Muzaynah — for 'Amr ibn Murrah, then said: > **""March on the blessing of Allah.""** They marched to Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith. Allah routed them, and many of his companions were slain. Hence Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith says: ""Who will reproach me or aid me, with the Mashrafi sword, against Juhaynah? A thousand led by Ibn Murrah, possessor of doomed battalions; they went off with the weapons and stirred Muzaynah against us."""
"10343","Jundab ibn Makith al-Juhani radiya Allahu anhu said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Ghalib ibn Abjar al-Kalbi of Kalb Layth to Banu al-Mulawwah at al-Kadid, ordering him to raid them. He set out, and I was in his detachment. We marched until at Qudayd we met al-Harith ibn Malik, Ibn al-Barsa' al-Laythi, and seized him. He said, ""I came only to accept Islam."" Ghalib ibn 'Abdillah said, ""If you came only to accept Islam, a day and night in bonds will not harm you; if otherwise, we shall secure ourselves against you."" He bound him, left a black man of ours with him, and said, ""Stay with him until we pass you; if he resists you, sever his head."" We went on to Batn al-Kadid and camped there in the evening after 'Asr. My companions sent me as scout. I headed to a hill overlooking the settlement and lay flat upon it, just before Maghrib. A man of theirs came out, saw me lying on the hill, and said to his wife, ""By Allah, I see a dark shape on this hill I did not see this morning. Look, lest the dogs dragged off some of your vessels."" She looked and said, ""No, by Allah, I miss nothing."" He said, ""Hand me a bow and two arrows."" She handed them. He shot an arrow into my side. I pulled it out, set it aside, and did not move. He shot another into the top of my shoulder. I pulled it out and did not move. He said to his wife, ""By Allah, both my arrows struck it; were it anything that moves it would have stirred. In the morning seek my arrows and take them, lest the dogs chew them."" We waited until their livestock returned at evening, they milked and settled, and part of the night passed. Then we launched the raid, killed whom we killed, and drove the livestock back, departing. Their crier went howling to his people. We hastened until we passed al-Harith ibn al-Barsa' and his guard, took him with us, and the people's rescue party came in numbers we could not face, until only the valley floor lay between us. Then a flood sent by Allah from whence He willed came between us and them — we had seen neither rain nor circumstance before it — so none of them could advance upon it. I saw them standing, looking at us, unable to approach, while we drove the livestock swiftly until we brought them to al-Mashlal, then down past it, escaping the people with what was in our hands."
"10344","'Abdullah ibn Unays radiya Allahu anhu said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned me and said: > **""It has reached me that Khalid ibn Sufyan ibn Nubayh al-Hudhali is gathering men against me to attack me. Go to him and kill him.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, describe him to me so I may know him."" He said: > **""When you see him, you will feel a shudder.""** I set out girt with my sword until I found him at 'Uranah with womenfolk, seeking a dwelling for them, at the time of 'Asr. When I saw him I felt what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had described. I approached, fearing some obstacle between me and him, so I prayed, gesturing with my head for bowing and prostration. When I reached him he said, ""Who is the man?"" I said, ""A man who heard of you and your gathering against this man, and came to you in that regard."" He said, ""Yes, I am engaged in that."" I walked with him a while until opportunity came; I attacked with the sword and killed him, then left, leaving his women bent over him. When I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he saw me, he said: > **""A prosperous face.""** I said, ""I killed him, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: > **""You spoke the truth.""** Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose with me, took me into his house, gave me a staff, and said: > **""Keep this with you, O 'Abdullah ibn Unays.""** I went out with it to the people. They said, ""What is this staff?"" I said, ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave it to me and ordered me to keep it."" They said, ""Why not return to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and ask him about it?"" I returned and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, why did you give me this staff?"" He said: > **""A sign between me and you on the Day of Resurrection; the fewest people that day are those carrying a staff.""** 'Abdullah joined it to his sword. It remained with him until he died, when he ordered it joined with him in his shroud, and they were buried together."
"10345","Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi said: 'Abdullah ibn Unays said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Who will deal with Khalid ibn Nubayh for me?""** A man of Hudhayl, then at 'Uranah. 'Abdullah said, ""I will, O Messenger of Allah. Describe him to me."" He said: > **""Were you to see him, you would fear him.""** I said, ""By Him Who honored you, I have never feared anything."" I set out until I met him near 'Uranah before sunset. When I met him I felt awe of him, and knew then what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had meant. He said, ""Who is the man?"" I said, ""One seeking a need; is there lodging?"" He said, ""Yes, follow me."" I followed. I prayed 'Asr in two light rak'ahs, then went on, fearing he would see me. I caught up with him and struck him with the sword, then took to the mountain and hid until the people left. I came out until I reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Madinah and told him the news. Muhammad ibn Ka'b said: The Prophet ﷺ gave him a short staff and said, ""Carry this until you meet me with it on the Day of Resurrection; the fewest people that day are those carrying one."" When 'Abdullah ibn Unays died, he ordered it placed upon his belly, shrouded over it, and buried with him."
"10346","'Abdullah ibn Unays radiya Allahu anhu said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Who will deal with Sufyan al-Hudhali? He lampoons me, reviles me, and harms me.""** I said, ""I will, O Messenger of Allah. Send me to him."" He sent him. He came to him by night, entered his house, and said, ""Where is Sufyan?"" One of his household looked out and said, ""What do you want?"" He said, ""I want Sufyan; bid him come out to me."" Sufyan looked out and said, ""What do you want?"" He said, ""I want you to come down to me, for I have a coat of mail I wish to show you."" He said, ""Where is it?"" He said, ""Here; come down to me in your cloak, come out with me and I will show it to you."" He came out with him. He drew his sword and struck him until he was cold. Then he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the mosque and informed him he had killed him. The Prophet ﷺ had a staff he carried; he handed it to him and said: > **""Carry this, for the fewest on the Day of Resurrection are those who carry one.""** It remained with him until he died and was buried with him."
"10347","'Ubadah ibn al-Samit radiya Allahu anhu said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O company of Ansar, is there no man to suffice me against Sufyan al-Hudhali, for he has lampooned me?""** 'Abdullah ibn Unays rose and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, where is he?"" He said: > **""At 'Uranah.""** He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, describe him to me."" He said: > **""When you see him, you will dread him.""** He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I have dreaded nothing since I accepted Islam."" 'Abdullah ibn Unays went out running on his feet until he killed him, then returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"10348","Al-Sha'bi, from Ri'yah al-Suhaymi, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to him on red leather. He took the letter of the Prophet ﷺ and patched his bucket with it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a detachment that left him neither grazing nor returning livestock, neither family nor wealth, but took all. He escaped naked upon his horse with no covering until he reached his daughter, married among Banu Hilal — she had accepted Islam, and her household had accepted Islam. The men's sitting place was in her courtyard, so he circled until he entered to her from behind the house. When she saw him she cast a garment over him. She said, ""What is with you?"" He said, ""Every evil has befallen your father; left to him neither grazing nor returning beast, neither family nor wealth, but all taken."" She said, ""You were invited to Islam."" He said, ""Where is your husband?"" She said, ""Among the camels."" He went to him. He said, ""What is with you?"" He said, ""Every evil has befallen him; left him neither beast nor family nor wealth but taken, and I wish to go to Muhammad to outrun him before he divides my wealth and family."" He said, ""Take my mount with its saddle."" He said, ""I have no need of it."" He took the shepherd's young camel and a waterskin of water, and set out wearing a garment that, when he covered his face, his nakedness showed, and when he covered his nakedness, his face showed, hating to be recognized, until he reached Madinah. He tied his mount, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and was beside him where he took his midday rest. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Fajr, he said, ""O Messenger of Allah, extend your hand that I may pledge to you."" He extended it; when he went to strike upon it, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ withdrew it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did that three times. On the third he said: > **""Who are you?""** He said, ""I am Ri'yah al-Suhaymi."" The Prophet ﷺ took his upper arm, raised him, and said: > **""O company of Muslims, this is Ri'yah al-Suhaymi, to whom I wrote and who took my letter and patched his bucket with it.""** He began to plead with him. I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, my family and wealth."" He said: > **""As for your wealth, it has been divided; as for your family, whomever of them you find.""** His son recognized the mount, standing beside it. He returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, this is my son."" He said: > **""O Bilal, go out with him and ask him: is this your father? If he says yes, hand him to him.""** He went out to him and said, ""Is this your father?"" He said, ""Yes."" He returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I never saw anyone weep for his companion so."" He said: > **""That is the harshness of the Bedouins.""**"
"10349","Abu Ishaq, from Ri'yah al-Juhani: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote him a letter and he patched his bucket with it. A detachment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by him and drove off his camels. He accepted Islam, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: > **""Whatever of your property you find intact before it is divided, you have the most right to it.""**"
"10350","'Amir — that is al-Sha'bi — said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent the army of Dhat al-Salasil, placing Abu 'Ubaydah over the Muhajirun and 'Amr ibn al-'As over the Bedouins, and said to them both: > **""Cooperate.""** They were ordered to raid Bakr. 'Amr set out and raided Quda'ah, because Bakr were his maternal uncles. Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah went to Abu 'Ubaydah and said, ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed you over us, yet the son of so-and-so has taken charge of the people's affair, and you have no authority with him."" Abu 'Ubaydah said, ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered us to cooperate; I shall obey the Messenger of Allah ﷺ even if 'Amr disobeys him."""
"10351","Abu Hadrad al-Aslami said he married a woman, then came to the Prophet ﷺ seeking help with her dowry. He said: > **""How much did you fix as dowry?""** He said, ""I said: two hundred dirhams."" He said: > **""Had you scooped dirhams from this valley of yours, it would be no more. I have nothing to give you.""** I remained, then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned me and sent me in a detachment toward Najd. He said: > **""Go out in this detachment; perhaps you will gain something and I shall grant it to you.""** We set out until we reached the settlement at evening. When the early part of 'Isha' had passed, our commander sent us two by two. We surrounded the camp. He said, ""When I say takbir and charge, say takbir and charge."" When he sent us two by two he said, ""Do not separate, let me not ask either of you about his companion's news and find he has none, and do not go deep in pursuit."" When we were about to charge, I heard a man of the settlement cry, ""O Khadrah!"" I took it as an omen that we would gain verdure from them. When night deepened, our commander said takbir, we said takbir and charged. A man with a sword in his hand passed by me and I followed him. My companion said to me, ""Our commander bound us not to go deep in pursuit, so return."" I refused but to follow him. He said, ""By Allah, I shall return to him and inform him you refused."" I said, ""By Allah, I shall follow him."" I followed until I drew near and shot him with an arrow in the middle of his back; he fell. He said, ""Come, O Muslim, to Paradise."" When he saw I would not draw near, and I struck him with another arrow and wounded him deeply, he threw his sword at me and missed. I took the sword, killed him with it, severed his head, and we pressed on, taking many camels and sheep. Then we departed. In the morning my camel was haltered with a beautiful young woman upon it. She kept looking behind her. I said to her, ""Where are you looking?"" She said, ""At a man — by Allah, if he is alive he has mingled with you."" I said, thinking he was my comrade whom I killed, ""By Allah, I killed him; this is his sword hung on the saddle of the camel I ride."" The sword's scabbard, empty, hung on her camel's saddle. When I said that she said, ""Then take this scabbard and sheathe it in it, if you are truthful."" I took it and sheathed it like a cloth. When she saw that, she wept. We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he gave me from those camels we brought."
"10352","'Adi ibn Hatim radiya Allahu anhu said: The cavalry — or he said, the messengers — of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came while I was at 'Aqrab. They took my aunt and others. When they brought them to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, they lined up for him. She said, ""O Messenger of Allah, the delegate is distant, the father is cut off, and I am an old woman with no servant. Bestow favor upon me, may Allah bestow upon you."" He said: > **""And who is your delegate?""** She said, ""Adi ibn Hatim."" He said: > **""The one who fled from Allah and His Messenger?""** She said, ""So bestow upon me."" He bestowed upon her. When he turned away, with a man beside him — she thought it was 'Ali — he said, ""Ask him for a mount."" She asked, and he ordered one for her. She came to me and said, ""You did a deed your father would never do. Go to him, willing or reluctant, for so-and-so came to him and obtained from him, and so-and-so came to him and obtained from him."" I came to him to find a woman and children — or a child — with him. He mentioned their closeness to the Prophet ﷺ, and I knew he was not a king like Kisra or Caesar. He said to him: > **""O 'Adi ibn Hatim, what makes you flee? That you say: there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah? Is there any deity but Allah? What makes you flee from saying: Allah is Greatest? Is anything greater than Allah?""** I accepted Islam, and I saw his face brighten. He said: > **""Indeed those who earned anger are the Jews, and the astray are the Christians.""** Then they asked him. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: > **""To proceed, O people: you may give from surplus — a man gives a sa', part of a sa', a handful, part of a handful.""** Shu'bah said, to the best of my knowledge he said: > **""A date, half a date. Each of you shall meet Allah and He will say what I say: Did I not make you hearing, seeing? Did I not give you wealth and children? What did you send forth? He will look before him, behind him, to his right and left, and find nothing shielding his face from the Fire. So shield yourselves from the Fire even with half a date; if you find none, with a gentle word. I do not fear poverty for you; Allah will aid you, or Allah will grant you, or Allah will open for you, until the woman-traveler journeys between al-Hirah and Yathrib fearing nothing but theft for her litter.""**"
"10353","Jufaynah said: the Prophet ﷺ wrote him a letter and he patched his bucket with it. His daughter said to him, ""You took the letter of the chief of the Arabs and patched your bucket with it!"" He fled, taking whatever little and much was with him. Then he came afterward as a Muslim, and the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""See what you find of your goods before the division of shares, and take it.""**"
"10354","Asma' bint Yazid said: the Prophet ﷺ sent a detachment to the outskirts of Mudar. They reported they camped in desert land. In the morning they found a man in a tent with sheep in his courtyard. They came to him and said, ""Give us a slaughter-animal."" He gave them a sheep; they cooked from it, then another, which they seized haughtily. He said, ""No meat-sheep remains in my flock but a pregnant ewe or a ram."" They rushed and took a sheep from them. When they prevailed and burned, on a hot summer day with no shade, they said, ""His sheep are in his shade."" They said, ""We have more right to the shade than these sheep."" They came and said, ""Remove your sheep from us that we may shade ourselves."" He said, ""If you remove them they will perish and cast their young; and I have believed in Allah and His Messenger, I have prayed and paid zakat."" They removed his sheep; within an hour of the day they bled and cast their young. He hastened to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him. The Prophet ﷺ grew intensely angry, then said: > **""Sit until the people return.""** When they returned, he gathered them and him. They persisted in saying, ""He lies, he lies,"" and the Prophet's ﷺ anger eased. When the Bedouin saw that, he said, ""By Allah, Allah knows I am truthful and they lie; perhaps Allah will inform you of that, O Prophet of Allah."" It fell into the Prophet's ﷺ heart that he was truthful. He called them man by man, adjuring each by his oath, and none he adjured but said as the Bedouin had said. The Prophet ﷺ rose and said: > **""What drives you to follow one another in lying as moths follow one another into fire? Lying is recorded against the son of Adam except in three traits: a man who lies to his wife to please her, a man who lies in the stratagem of war, and a man who lies between two Muslim women to reconcile between them.""**"
"10355","Ibn 'Abbas radiya Allahu anhuma: the Prophet ﷺ sent a detachment. They took spoils, and among them was a man who said, ""I am not of them; I loved a woman of them and followed her. Let me look at her, then do with me as you will."" He came to a tall, dark-skinned woman and said to her, ""Accept Islam, Hubaysh, before life runs out. Did you see, when I followed you and reached you at al-Halabah or met you at al-Khawaniq — was it not right that a lover who undertook night-journeying and midday travel should attain?"" She said, ""Yes, may I be ransom for you."" They brought him forward and struck his neck. The woman came, fell upon him, sobbed once or twice, then died. When they came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Was there no merciful man among you?""**"
"10356","'Isam al-Muzani, a Companion, said: When the Prophet ﷺ sent an army or detachment he would say to them: > **""When you see a mosque or hear a caller to prayer, kill no one.""** The Prophet ﷺ sent us in a detachment with that order. We marched through Tihamah and overtook a man driving women-camels. We offered him Islam. We said, ""Are you a Muslim?"" He said, ""What is Islam?"" We informed him, and he did not know it. He said, ""If I do not, what will you do?"" We said, ""We shall kill you."" He said, ""Will you grant me time until I reach the women?"" We said, ""Yes,"" able to overtake him. He went to a woman in her howdah and said, ""Accept Islam, Hubaysh, before life ends."" She said, ""I accept ten times over, nine in succession."" Then he said: ""Do you remember when I sought you and found you at al-Halabah or reached you at al-Khawaniq? Was it not right that a lover who undertook night-journeying and midday travel should attain? I bear no sin when I said, while our people are together: repay with love before one of the straits; repay with love before distance removes and the commander distances the parted beloved."" Then he came to us and said, ""Do as you will."" We brought him forward and struck his neck. The other woman descended from her howdah and knelt over him until she died."
"10357","'Urwah: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a detachment toward al-'Umrah of Najd, commanded by Thabit ibn Aqram. Thabit ibn Aqram was killed in it."
"10358","Jabir ibn Samurah radiya Allahu anhu said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us in a detachment and we were routed. Sa'd pursued a rider of theirs. He turned to him and saw his leg outside the stirrup. He shot him with an arrow; I saw the blood flowing as though a sandal-strap, and he made his mount kneel."
"10359","Khabbab radiya Allahu anhu said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us in a detachment. Thirst struck us and we had no water. A she-camel of one of us knelt, and between her legs was like a waterskin — her udder full — and we drank from her milk."
"10360","Ibn 'Abbas radiya Allahu anhuma said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Khalid ibn al-Walid and 'Ali ibn Abi Talib to Yemen, placing 'Ali ibn Abi Talib — may Allah's pleasure be upon him — over the Muhajirun and Khalid ibn al-Walid over the Bedouins. He said: > **""If there is fighting, 'Ali ibn Abi Talib commands all the people.""**"
"10361","Khalid ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As, from his father, from his grandfather, said: Bakr ibn Wa'il came to Makkah. The Prophet ﷺ said to Abu Bakr: > **""Go to them and present it to them.""** He came to them and said, ""Who are the people?"" They said, ""Banu Dhuhl ibn Tha'labah."" He said, ""It is not you I seek; you are the tails."" Daghfal rose to him and said, ""Who are you?"" He said, ""A man of Quraysh."" He said, ""Of Banu Hashim?"" He said, ""No."" He said, ""Of Banu Umayyah?"" He said, ""No."" He said, ""Then you are of the tails."" He returned to them a second time and said, ""Who are the people?"" They said, ""Banu Dhuhl ibn Shayban."" He presented Islam to them. They said, ""Until our elder comes"" — Khallad thought he said al-Muthanna ibn Kharijah. When their elder came, Abu Bakr radiya Allahu anhu presented to them. He said, ""Between us and the Persians is war; when we finish what is between us and them, we shall return and consider."" Abu Bakr said to him, ""What if you overcome them — will you follow us in our affair?"" He said, ""We do not stipulate that for you; but when we finish what is between us and them, we shall return and consider what you say."" When they met the Persians on the day of Dhi Qar, their elder said, ""What is the name of the man who called you to Allah?"" They said, ""Muhammad."" They said, ""He is your battle-cry."" They were aided over the people. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Through me they were aided.""**"
"10362","Bashir ibn Yazid al-Duba'i, who had lived in Jahiliyyah, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the day of Dhi Qar: > **""This is the first day in which the Arabs were avenged on the non-Arabs.""**"
"10363","Sa'd — that is Ibn Abi Waqqas radiya Allahu anhu — said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""The Muslims will prevail over Rome, the Muslims will prevail over Persia, and the Muslims will prevail over the Arabian Peninsula.""**"
"10364","Jubayr ibn Nufayr said: Ibn Hawalah said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and they complained to him of poverty, nakedness, and lack. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Rejoice, for by Allah, I fear abundance for you more than scarcity. By Allah, this affair shall remain among you until there are opened for you an army in Sham, an army in Iraq, and an army in Yemen, until a man is given a hundred and resents it.""** 'Abdullah ibn Hawalah said, ""When can we take Sham with Rome of the horned might?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah will open it for you and make you successors in it, until a band of them, white their shirts, shaven their necks, stand over your little black man — whatever he orders them, they do. In it today are men in whose eyes you are more contemptible than ticks on camels' rumps.""** Then he mentioned the hadith."
"10365","Jubayr ibn Nufayr said: 'Abdullah ibn Wazzah, an early Companion, used to say: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Soon a little man will be placed in command over them; a people will gather to him, shaven their necks, white their shirts. When he orders them anything, they attend.""** Then your Lord willed that 'Abdullah ibn Wazzah governed one of the cities. A people of landowners gathered to him, shaven their necks, white their shirts; when he ordered them anything, they attended. He would say, ""Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth."""
"10366","'Adi ibn Hatim radiya Allahu anhu said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Al-Hirah was displayed to me like dogs' fangs, and you shall conquer it.""** A man stood and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, grant me the daughter of Buqaylah."" He said: > **""She is yours.""** They gave her to him. Her brother came and said, ""Will you sell her?"" He said, ""Yes."" He said, ""Decide what you will."" He said, ""For a thousand dirhams."" He said, ""I take her for a thousand."" They said to him, ""Had you said thirty thousand."" He said, ""Is there a number greater than a thousand?!"""
"10367","Al-Mustawrid said: While I was with 'Amr ibn al-'As, I said to him: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The most severe against you are Rome, and their destruction is only with the Hour.""** 'Amr said to him, ""Did I not forbid you from the like of this?!"""
"10368","A man of Khath'am said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ in the Tabuk campaign. One night he stood and his companions gathered to him. He said: > **""Indeed Allah has given me tonight the two treasures: the treasure of Persia and Rome, and aided me with kings, the kings of Himyar, the red kings. There is no king but Allah. They come taking from Allah's wealth and fighting in Allah's way.""** He said it three times."
"10369","'Iyad al-Ash'ari said: I witnessed Yarmuk under five commanders: Abu 'Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan, Ibn Hasanah, Khalid ibn al-Walid, and 'Iyad — not the 'Iyad who narrated to Simak. 'Umar said, ""When fighting befalls you, Abu 'Ubaydah is over you."" We wrote to him: death has surged toward us, seeking reinforcements. He wrote to us: ""Your letter seeking my aid has come to me. I guide you to One mightier in aid and nearer in soldiers: Allah. Seek His aid, for Muhammad ﷺ was aided on Badr with fewer than your number. When this letter of mine reaches you, fight them and do not refer back to me."" We fought them, killed them, routed them four farsakhs. We gained wealth and consulted; 'Iyad advised us to give ten for every head. Abu 'Ubaydah said, ""Who will race me?"" A youth said, ""I will, if you will not be angry?"" He outran him; I saw Abu 'Ubaydah's two braids bouncing behind him on a bare horse."
"10370","Al-Zuhri said: After the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr sent commanders over Sham, placing Khalid ibn Sa'id over an army."
"10371","Khubayb ibn Abi Thabit: al-Harith ibn Hisham, 'Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, and 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah were struck down on Yarmuk day. Al-Harith called for drink. 'Ikrimah looked at him and said, ""Hand it to 'Ikrimah."" It was handed to him. 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah looked at him, and 'Ikrimah said, ""Hand it to 'Ayyash."" It reached none of them before they all died, without tasting it."
"10372","Muhajir ibn Dinar: Asma' bint Yazid ibn al-Sakan, cousin of Mu'adh ibn Jabal, killed nine Romans on Yarmuk day with a tent-pole."
"10373","Abu Wa'il said: 'Abdullah — that is Ibn Mas'ud radiya Allahu anhu — heard a man say, ""Where are the ascetics in this world, desirers of the Hereafter?"" 'Abdullah said, ""Those are gone — the companions of al-Jabiyah. Five hundred Muslims stipulated they would not return until killed. They shaved their heads, met the enemy, and were killed, save one to report of them."""
"10374","'Urwah, concerning those killed at Ajnadayn: Of Quraysh, then of Banu 'Abd Shams ibn Manaf: Aban ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Sahm ibn Husays: Tamim ibn al-Harith ibn Qays, and Jundab ibn Hamamah al-Dawsi, ally of Banu Umayyah ibn 'Abd Shams. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Umayyah: 'Amr ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Sahm: Hajjaj ibn al-Harith ibn Qays. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Sahm: al-Harith ibn al-Harith ibn Qays. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Sahm: Sa'id ibn al-Harith ibn Qays. Of Banu 'Adi ibn Ka'b: Nu'aym ibn 'Abdillah."
"10375","Ibn Shihab, in naming those martyred at Ajnadayn: Of Quraysh, then of Banu Sahm: Tamim ibn al-Harith ibn Qays, and Jundab ibn 'Amr ibn Hamhamah al-Dawsi, ally of Banu Umayyah ibn 'Abd Shams. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Sahm: Hajjaj ibn al-Harith. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Sahm: al-Harith ibn Abi Qarib. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Sahm: Sa'id ibn al-Harith ibn Qays."
"10376","Muhammad ibn Ishaq, in naming those martyred at Ajnadayn: Of Quraysh, then of Banu Sahm: Hajjaj ibn al-Harith. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Sahm: al-Harith ibn al-Harith."
"10377","Al-Tabarani said: al-Harith ibn Hisham al-Makhzumi was martyred at Yarmuk. Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father Qurrah, said: On Qadisiyyah day, al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah sent to the Persian commander, saying, ""Send ten with me."" He tightened his garments, took a leather shield, and went until they came to him. He said to the people, ""Throw me a cloak,"" and sat upon it. The foreigner said, ""You Arabs — I know what brought you to us. You are a people who find no food in your land to satisfy you. Take, we shall give you food you need. We are Magians and dislike killing you, and you defile our land."" Al-Mughirah said, ""By Allah, that is not what brought us. We were a people who worshipped stones and idols; when we saw a stone better than a stone we cast it and took another, knowing no Lord, until Allah sent us a Messenger from ourselves. He called us to Islam and we followed him. We did not come for food. He commanded us to fight our enemy who abandoned Islam. We did not come for food, but we came to kill your fighters and take your offspring captive."" ""As for the food you mentioned, by my life we indeed found no food to satisfy us, sometimes not even water to quench us. We came to this land of yours and found abundant food. By Allah, we shall not leave it until it is ours or yours."" The foreigner said in Persian, ""He spoke truth — and your eye will be gouged tomorrow,"" in Persian. His eye was gouged the next day; an arrow struck it."
"10378","Abu al-Salt said: 'Umar radiya Allahu anhu wrote to us while we were with al-Nu'man ibn Muqarrin al-Muzani: > **""When you meet the enemy, do not flee; when you gain spoils, do not steal.""** When we met the enemy, al-Nu'man said, ""Grant the people time — it is Friday — until the Commander of the Faithful ascends and seeks aid."" He fought them. Al-Nu'man was struck and said, ""Cover me with a garment, turn to your enemy, and let me not alarm you."" We turned upon them and Allah granted us victory. News reached 'Umar that al-Nu'man and so-and-so were struck, and men we do not know. He said, ""But Allah knows them."""
"10379","Ma'qil ibn Yasar: 'Umar consulted al-Hurmuzan about Isbahan, Persia, and Adharbayjan. He said, ""O Commander of the Faithful, Isbahan is the head, Persia and Adharbayjan the two wings. If you cut one wing, the head rises with the other wing; if you cut the head, both wings fall. Begin with the head."" 'Umar entered the mosque to find al-Nu'man ibn Muqarrin al-Muzani praying. He waited until he finished, then said, ""I shall employ you."" He said, ""As collector, no; as raider, yes."" He said, ""You are a raider."" He dispatched them and wrote to Kufah's people to reinforce him and join him — among them Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, al-Ash'ath, 'Amr ibn Ma'di Karib, and 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr. Al-Nu'man came to them with a river between him and them. He sent al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah as envoy. Their king was Dhu al-Janahayn. He consulted his companions: ""Shall I sit for him in war-array or in royal splendor?"" They said, ""Sit for him in royal splendor."" He sat on a throne with the crown on his head, two rows around him in brocade, earrings, and bracelets. Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah kept lowering his gaze, spear and shield in hand, the people around him in two rows on his carpet, piercing it with his spear to make them augur evil. Dhu al-Janahayn said to him, ""You Arabs suffered severe hunger; if you wish, we shall provision you and you return to your land?"" Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah spoke, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said, ""We Arabs ate carrion and dead meat, people trod upon us and we trod upon none. Allah raised among us a Messenger of our noblest, middlemost in lineage, truest in speech. He promised us that here it would be opened for us. We found all he promised true. I see here finery I never saw the like of."" The king said, ""By Allah, you shall not return until some of you are killed and some captured."" Al-Mughirah returned to al-Nu'man and informed him. Al-Nu'man said, ""I witnessed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: when we did not fight at the day's start, he delayed fighting until the sun declined, the winds blew, and aid descended."" Al-Nu'man said, ""O people, stir — at the first shake let each man relieve his need; at the second let each look to his weapon and sandal-strap; at the third I shall charge, so charge. If any is killed, let none turn to any; if I am killed, do not turn to me. I shall supplicate Allah with a supplication, and I adjure every man of you to say amen to it."" He said: > **""O Allah, grant al-Nu'man martyrdom today with victory for the Muslims, and open for them.""** The people said amen. He shook his banner thrice, then charged, and was the first fallen. I passed by him, remembered his adjuration, did not turn to him, and marked his place. When we killed a man of theirs, his companions busied themselves dragging him away. Dhu al-Janahayn fell from a gray mule and his belly split. Allah opened for the Muslims. I came to al-Nu'man's place with life-breath in him. I came and said, ""Allah has opened for them."" He said, ""Praise be to Allah. Write that to 'Umar."" His soul flowed out. They gathered to al-Ash'ath ibn Qays. We came to his slave-mother and said, ""Did he entrust you with anything?"" She said, ""No, but a case with a letter."" I read it: ""If so-and-so is killed, then so-and-so; if killed, then so-and-so."" Hammad said: 'Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us: Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi said he came to 'Umar, who asked about al-Nu'man. He said, ""To Allah we belong and to Him we return."" He said, ""What of so-and-so?"" I said, ""He was killed, O Commander of the Faithful, and others we do not know."" He said, ""I do not know them, but Allah knows them."""
"10380","Ibn Shihab, in naming the Muslims martyred on the Bridge day, year fifteen: Of the Ansar, then of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal: Aws ibn Aws. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: As'ad ibn Harithah ibn Lawdhan. Of the Ansar: Thabit ibn 'Atik, and Tha'labah ibn 'Amr ibn Muhsin. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Mu'awiyah: al-Harith ibn 'Adi ibn Malik, and al-Harith ibn Mas'ud ibn 'Abd ibn Muzahir."
"10381","'Urwah, concerning those killed on the Bridge of al-Mada'in day: Of the Ansar, then of Banu Za'wra': Aws ibn 'Atik ibn 'Amir. Of the Ansar, then of Banu 'Amr ibn Mabdhul: Tha'labah ibn 'Amr ibn Muhsin, and Thabit ibn 'Atik. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Najjar: Zayd ibn Suraqah ibn Ka'b. Of the Ansar, then of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal, then of Banu Za'wra': Sa'd ibn Salamah."
"10382","Muhammad ibn Ishaq, concerning those killed on the Bridge day: Of the Ansar, then of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal, then of Banu Za'wra': Aws ibn 'Atik ibn 'Amir. Of the Ansar: Thabit ibn 'Atik. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Mu'awiyah: al-Harith ibn Mas'ud ibn 'Abd ibn Muzahir."
"10383","'Amr ibn al-'As radiya Allahu anhu said: An army of Muslims set out, I their commander, until we camped at Alexandria. Its master said, ""Send out to me a man of you that I may speak with him and he with me."" I said, ""None shall go but I."" I went out with an interpreter, he with an interpreter, until two pulpits were set for him. He said, ""Who are you?"" We said, ""We are Arabs, people of thorn and acacia, people of Allah's House. We were the narrowest of people in land, hardest in life — eating dead meat, raiding one another, the worst life men lived — until a man arose among us, neither greatest in honor that day nor most in wealth. He said: > **""I am the Messenger of Allah.""** He commanded us what we knew not and forbade us what we and our fathers were upon. We scorned him, belied him, rebutted his word, until a people other than us went out to him and said, 'We believe you, trust you, follow you, and fight whoever fights you.' He went out to them. We went out to him and fought him; he killed us, prevailed over us, overcame us, then took those Arabs near him and fought them until he prevailed. Had those behind me known the life you enjoy, none would remain but would join you in it."" He laughed, then said, ""Your messenger spoke truth. Our messengers came to us with the like of what your messenger brought you. We were upon it until kings arose among us, ruling us by their desires, abandoning the prophets' command. If you hold to your Prophet's command, none shall fight you but you overcome him, none shall assail you but you prevail. If you do as we did, abandon the prophets' command, and act as they acted by their desires, it will be left between us and you — you shall be no more in number nor mightier in strength."" 'Amr ibn al-'As said: I never spoke with a shrewder man."
"10384","Bishr al-Khath'ami heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Constantinople shall surely be opened; excellent the commander, its commander, and excellent the army, that army.""** He said: Maslamah ibn 'Abd al-Malik summoned me, I narrated it to him, and he raided Constantinople."
"10385","Abu Qabil said: We were with 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr when he was asked, ""Which of the two cities is opened first: Constantinople or Rome?"" 'Abdullah called for a locked chest of his, brought out a book, and said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ writing, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked, ""Which of the two cities is opened first, Constantinople or Rome?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The city of Heraclius is opened first,""** meaning Constantinople."
"10386","Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani, Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said while at al-Fustat under Mu'awiyah — Mu'awiyah having raided Constantinople most of all men — : ""By Allah, half a day shall not incapacitate this community. When you see Sham as one man's table and his household's, then is the opening of Constantinople."""
"10387","'Amr ibn 'Awf said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""The Hour shall not come until there is a Muslim outpost at Bulan. O 'Ali!""** Al-Muzani said, meaning 'Ali ibn Abi Talib: he said, ""At your service, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: > **""Know that you shall fight the Yellow Ones, and those after you of the believers shall fight them. Then the elite of the Muslims, the people of Hijaz who strive in Allah's way, fearing no blamer's blame for Allah, shall go out to them until Allah opens for them Constantinople and Rome with glorification and magnification. They shall demolish their fortresses, gain immense wealth the like of which they never gained, until they divide by shields. Then a crier shall cry: O people of Islam, the False Messiah has emerged among your lands and offspring! The people shall withdraw from the wealth — some taking, some leaving; the taker regretful, the leaver regretful. Then they shall say: who is this crier? Not knowing who he is, they shall say: send scouts to Lydda. If the Messiah has emerged, they shall bring you his news. They shall come, look, see nothing, and see men silent. They shall say: the crier cried only to us. Resolve, then be guided. We shall all go out to Lydda. If the False Messiah is there, we shall fight him until Allah judges between us and him, and He is the Best of Judges. If it is the other, those are your lands, your kinsfolk, your armies — you shall return to them.""**"
"10388","'Amir — that is al-Sha'bi — said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died and those who apostatized did so, a people said, ""We shall pray but not pay zakat."" The people said to Abu Bakr, ""Accept it from them."" He said, ""Were they to withhold even a young goat, I would fight them."" He sent Khalid ibn al-Walid. 'Adi ibn Hatim arrived with a thousand of Tayyi' until he reached Yamamah. Banu 'Amir had killed the officials of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and burned them with fire. Abu Bakr wrote to Khalid to kill Banu 'Amir and burn them with fire. He did, until the women cried out. Then he marched to the water. They came out to him saying, ""Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; we bear witness there is no deity but Allah, and Muhammad is Allah's Messenger."" When he heard that, he withheld from them. Abu Bakr ordered him to march until he camped at al-Hirah, then proceed to Sham. When he camped at al-Hirah he wrote to the Persians, then said, ""I love not to depart until I frighten them."" He raided to Sura, killing and capturing, raided 'Ayn al-Tamr, killing and capturing, then marched to Sham. 'Amir said: he brought out to Ibn Buqaylah Khalid's letter: ""In the name of Allah, the Merciful, the Compassionate. From Khalid ibn al-Walid to the Persian grandees. Peace upon whoever follows guidance. I praise Allah, besides Whom there is no deity, with the praise that severed your bonds, scattered your union, weakened your might, and stripped your kingdom. When this letter of mine reaches you, take protection from me, pay me the tribute, and send me hostages. Else, by Allah besides Whom there is no deity, I shall meet you with a people who love death as you love life. Peace upon whoever follows guidance."""
"10389","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: When Khalid ibn al-Walid finished Yamamah, Abu Bakr radiya Allahu anhu sent al-'Ala' ibn al-Hadrami to Bahrayn. Al-'Ala' was the one the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had sent to al-Mundhir ibn Sawa al-'Abdi; al-Mundhir accepted Islam, and al-'Ala' remained there as the Messenger's ﷺ governor. Rabi'ah in Bahrayn apostatized among the Arabs who apostatized, except al-Jarud ibn 'Amr, who held to Islam with his followers. Rabi'ah gathered in Bahrayn and apostatized, saying, ""Restore kingship to the house of al-Mundhir."" They spoke to al-Mundhir ibn al-Nu'man ibn al-Mundhir, called al-Gharur. Afterwards, when he accepted Islam with the people under the sword, he would say, ""I am not al-Gharur but al-Maghrur."" When Rabi'ah gathered in Bahrayn, al-'Ala' ibn al-Hadrami marched to them, reinforced by Thumamah ibn Uthal al-Hanafi, who had accepted Islam with his people. Al-'Ala' placed Thumamah in command; he marched with Banu Suhaym until they waded the sea to Rabi'ah. Rabi'ah marched to them and besieged them at Juwatha, a fortress in Bahrayn, until the Muslims nearly perished of hardship. 'Abdullah ibn Hadaq al-'Amiri said then: ""Convey to Abu Bakr a message, and to Madinah's youths all: have you concern for youths of yours who spend the evening, all besieged in Juwatha? We trust in the Merciful; we found victory for the trusting."" 'Abdullah ibn Hadaq said, ""Let me descend from the fortress and I shall bring you news."" With him was a woman of Banu 'Ijl. He descended; they seized him and said, ""Of whom are you?"" He gave his lineage, crying, ""O Abjarah!"" — who was among them. Abjar came, recognized him, and said, ""What is with you?"" He said, ""I perish of hunger."" He carried him, gave him drink. He said, ""Carry me and free my way."" He set him on a mule and said, ""Go your way."" When 'Abdullah ibn Hadaq left them he returned to his companions and informed them the people were drunk and worthless. Al-'Ala' with the Muslims of Arabs and non-Arabs fell upon them by night, slew them grievously, and they fled."
"10390","'Urwah said: Abu Bakr sent al-'Ala' ibn al-Hadrami in an army from Bahrayn toward Bahrayn's people. They had withheld the tribute they had submitted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when al-'Ala' had opened it and made peace with them upon tribute. Abu Bakr sent to them when they withheld Allah's due in their wealth. He marched to them with the sea between him and them, until they walked it on their feet — crossing at a place where ships ran before and run today. He fought them, Allah made him prevail, and they submitted, withholding Allah's due from their wealth."
"10391","Muhammad ibn Salam — that is al-Jumahi — said: Abu 'Ubaydah said Dirar ibn al-Azwar undertook the killing of Malik ibn Nuwayrah. Concerning it Mutammim ibn Nuwayrah says, alluding to Khalid ibn al-Walid: ""Excellent the slain, when winds contend where the lote-trees are — your slain, O son of al-Azwar; excellent the stuffing of the coat when you met him, excellent the shelter of the nightly visitor; generous with arrow-tips when exhilarated, sweet, lawful in wealth, no betrayer; he wears no indecency beneath his clothes, hard to lead, chaste of waist-wrapper; did you call him by Allah then kill him? Had he called you by covenant he would not betray; excellent the horsemen the day it befell — Fihr's horsemen departed in dark dust."" And it is narrated: ""in murky murk."""
"10392","Tariq ibn Shihab said: The apostates of Asad and Ghatafan came to Abu Bakr after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, seeking peace. He said, ""On condition you surrender arms and mounts, remain selling cattle-tails until Allah shows the Prophet's successor and the believers a view excusing you, you testify your slain are in the Fire and ours in Paradise, you pay blood-money for our slain, and we pay none for yours."" 'Umar said, ""O successor of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the word is as you said, except that our slain were killed under Allah's covenant — no blood-money for them."""
"10393","Khuraym ibn Aws said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""This white al-Hirah has been raised for me, and this al-Shayma' bint Buqaylah al-Azdiyyah upon a gray mule, wrapped in a black veil.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, if we enter al-Hirah and find her in this description, is she mine?"" He said: > **""She is yours.""** Then the Arabs apostatized; none of Tayyi' apostatized. We fought Qays for Islam — among them 'Uyaynah ibn Hisn — and fought Tulayhah ibn Khuwaylid al-Faq'asi. Khalid ibn al-Walid praised us, saying: ""May Allah reward Tayyi' in their abodes, at heroes' battleground, with best reward; they are banners of liberality and dew when dawn inclines each tent; they smote Qays for religion after they answered a caller of darkness and blindness."" Then Khalid marched to Musaylimah; we marched with him. When we finished Musaylimah and his men, we turned toward Basrah and saw Hurmuz at Kazimah in a great host — none more hostile to Arabs than Hurmuz. Abu al-Sakan said: the proverb is coined of him; Arabs say, ""More disbelieving than Hurmuz."" Khalid ibn al-Walid came out to him, calling to single combat; Hurmuz came out, and Khalid killed him. He wrote to Abu Bakr radiya Allahu anhu, who granted him his spoils; his cap reached a hundred thousand. Then we marched the Tarf road until we entered al-Hirah. The first to meet us there was al-Shayma' bint Buqaylah upon a gray mule in a black veil, as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said. I clung to her and said, ""This the Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted me."" Khalid demanded proof; I brought it, and he surrendered her to me. Her brother 'Abd al-Masih descended to us and said to me, ""Sell her to me?"" I said, ""By Allah, I shall not lessen her from ten hundred a thing."" He paid me a thousand dirhams. I was told, ""Had you said a hundred thousand he would have paid you."" I said, ""I did not reckon wealth greater than ten hundred."" It reached me in other than this hadith that the two witnesses were Muhammad ibn Maslamah and 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar."
"10394","Muhammad ibn Sirin said: On Musaylimah's day, al-Bara' ibn Malik met a man called Himar al-Yamamah — the man tall, a white sword in his hand. Al-Bara' was short. Al-Bara' struck his legs with the sword as though missing him, and fell on his back. He said: I took his sword, sheathed mine — I struck with it but one blow before it broke. I cast it and took my sword."
"10395","'Urwah, concerning those martyred at Yamamah: Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: Usayd ibn Yarbu'. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj: Bashir ibn 'Abdillah. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Malik ibn Taym Allah: Thabit ibn Khalid ibn al-Nu'man ibn Khalid ibn Khansa'. Of Quraysh: Jubayr ibn Malik, Ibn Buhaynah, of Banu Nawfal ibn 'Abd Manaf. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Jahjaba: Juz' ibn Malik ibn Huzayr. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Makhzum: Hakim ibn Hazn ibn Abi Wahb ibn 'Amr ibn 'A'idh. Of Quraysh, then of Banu 'Amir ibn Lu'ayy: Rabi'ah ibn Kharashah. Of the Ansar: Rabah, freedman of Jahjaba. Of Quraysh, then of Banu 'Adi ibn Ka'b: Zayd ibn al-Khattab and Zayd ibn Ruqaysh, ally of Banu Umayyah. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: Sa'd ibn Harithah ibn Lawdhan ibn 'Abd Wudd. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: Sa'd ibn Hibban, their ally. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Jahjaba: Sa'id ibn Rabi' ibn 'Adi ibn Malik. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Ashhal: Sahl ibn 'Adi of Banu Tamim, their ally, and Salim, freedman of Abu Hudhayfah ibn 'Utbah ibn Rabi'ah ibn 'Abd Shams. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: Simak ibn Kharashah, that is Abu Dujanah."
"10396","Ibn Shihab, in naming those martyred at Yamamah: Of the Muslim Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: Usayd ibn Yarbu'. Of the Ansar, then of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal: As'ad ibn Salamah. Of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Najjar: Thabit ibn Khalid ibn al-Nu'man. Of the Ansar, then of al-Aws, then of Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf: Juz' ibn Malik and Rabah, freedman of Jahjaba. Of Quraysh, then of Banu 'Amir ibn Lu'ayy: Rabi'ah ibn Kharashah. Of Quraysh, then of Banu 'Adi ibn Ka'b: Zayd ibn al-Khattab. Of Quraysh, then of Banu Zuhrah: Zayd ibn Usayd ibn Harithah. Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: Sa'd ibn Hammar, their ally. Of the Ansar, then of al-Aws, then of Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf: Sa'id ibn Rabi' ibn 'Adi ibn Malik."
"10397","Muhammad ibn Ishaq, in naming those martyred at Yamamah: Of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: Simak ibn Kharashah, that is Abu Dujanah."
"10398","Shabab said: 'Umarah ibn Hazm was martyred at Yamamah in year eleven."
